\paragraph*{\bf Introduction.}

%\andrea{Alternative: start from cooperation. How cooperation can thrive is an open problem, etc... These problems are studied in EGT framework, etc.. What is the point of using EGT...and what is the point of co-evolutionary: existing works and our contribution.}

%a concise but comprehensive framework to capture through evolutionary game principles how agents exhibit collective nontrivial cooperative behaviors when faced with a strategy-dependent setting.

Evolutionary game theory (EGT) is the paradigmatic framework that combines game theory with natural selection principles. Its rich mathematical framework has been successfully applied over the years to study behavioral dynamics across a wide range of contexts, from biological to socio-political environments \cite{axelrod1981evolution, maynard1976evolution, taylor1978evolutionary, nowak2006evolutionary}. 
%
EGT models how behavioral traits propagate %\sout{at a collective level} 
in a population of agents driven by self interest \cite{smith1973logic, smith1982evolution, nowak1992evolutionary, weibull2024evolutionary}. 
%
%In the game theoretic description, 
Each agent in the population is associated with a strategy and earns a payoff depending on the strategies of other players and the specific game setting  ~\cite{von2007theory, myerson2013game}.
%\andrea{Questo pezzo sotto puo' essere riscritto in forma compatta, evitando ripetizioni}
%\andrea{Something like: In EGT the strategic scenario/environment is completely defined by the payoff structure of the game, which associates to each strategy profile of the players a payoff.}
%In the game theoretic description each agent is associated with a strategy, leading to a payoff defined by the game setting ~\cite{von2007theory, myerson2013game}. 
%
Strategies propagate through the population based on their fitness, with more successful strategies - i.e., those that yield a higher average payoff - being more likely to spread.
%
In biological systems, this mimics the Darwinian principle of survival of the fittest, whereas in social contexts draws from the phenomenon of imitation of the most successful agent~\cite{livan2019don,de2024imitation}. 
%
A game is therefore completely defined in terms of its payoff structure, which associates the payoff to the strategy profile of the players. 
%
Games of particular relevance, especially in modeling social environments, are social dilemmas, which capture the tension between individual benefit and collective interest. In a social dilemma, agents choose between two strategies: cooperation, which benefits the group at a personal cost, and defection, which involves no contribution to the common good and yields a higher payoff, provided there are cooperators in the population to exploit~\cite{axelrod1988further, doebeli2004evolutionary, nowak2006five, perc2017statistical}. Paradigmatic examples are the \textit{Prisoner's Dilemma} and the \textit{Snowdrift Game}  \cite{axelrod1981evolution, maynard1976evolution}.
%
%In the game evolutionary paradigm, the changes in strategic behavior are dictated by the respective value of agents payoffs. 

The game’s payoff structure defines the specific strategic interactions and, more broadly, the environment in which these interactions take place.
In EGT the time scales for the evolution of the game environment and the strategic decision process are usually very different: 
the environment is considered fixed, and only the evolution of strategies within this static setting is considered.
However, during their evolution, real world systems are typically subject to different environmental conditions which influence the strategic decisions of interacting individuals ~\cite{levin2013social}. 
Building on this observation, 
recent research has begun to extend the evolutionary paradigm to account for mutable environments, by modeling game settings that change in time with payoffs no longer considered fixed \cite{akccay2020deconstructing}. For instance, the framework of stochastic games has been applied to model fluctuations in the strategy adoption through the payoffs \cite{shapley1953stochastic, wang2014different, hilbe2018evolution, kleshnina2023effect, wang2024evolution}.
Yet, these models do not underscore the direct and often deterministic effects of the population behavior on the environment itself ~\cite{peck1986evolution, bowles2003co, tilman2018revenue, estrela2019environmentally, cortez2020destabilizing}. 
%Indeed, the individual action may also bear foreseeable consequences on the environment hosting it, as strategies can in turn be impactful on the environmental state 
%\fede{refs esterne a GT}.
Following this line of reasoning, several co-evolution models couple an external environmental dynamics to the abundance of strategies in the population \cite{weitz2016oscillating, tilman2020evolutionary, ito2024complete}. 
%
On the one hand a resource-driven environment favors a specific strategy, while on the other hand the resource and thus the environment themselves are influenced by the strategic composition of the population, in a feedback loop.
Such models are suitable for describing the dynamics of ecological or biological systems driven by reciprocal influence between evolving species and changing environment, e.g. a rich or depleted resource depending on its self-renewal dynamics as well as on the cooperation that agents put up in order to preserve it.
However, in many real world scenarios, the evolution of the environment does not depend on external drivers, but is instead a direct consequence of the behavior of the individuals in the population. That is, the evolution of the game is not driven by exogenous variables, but may endogenously arise from  strategy-payoff feedback. Such situations can be representative of two opposite scenarios occurring for instance in social and financial settings: growing pro-social behavior or freeriding promoted by increasing cooperation.
