In the present work, we propose a novel co-evolutionary game theoretic framework for pairwise symmetric games. In particular, we put forward a parsimonious framework for games  (i.e., environments) whose evolution is endogenously driven by the strategic abundance of the population in time, 
%changing EGT equilibria
without assuming a specific external variable describing the game (i.e. environmental) dynamics.
% in fact in our model the environment is not described by an external variable but the payoff matrix itself \fede{paper flex di egt}
% Through this approach we deviate from previous models, obtaining a comprehensive description of game setting and strategy co-evolution by including just the system's only degree of freedom available, the cooperation level. % il degree of freedom esterno rientra nella dinamica di payoff
In the context of social dilemmas, the effects of our co-evolutionary model on the cooperation levels are compared with standard (i.e., in a static setting) evolutionary game theory (EGT) results. Results show that even linear feedbacks produce complex transitions from one social dilemma class to another. In particular, we demonstrate the emergence of game settings endowed with apparently irrational dynamical equilibria, which we refer to as \textit{Chimera games}. Such scenarios reveal how cooperation can be sustained (or suppressed) by the very incentives it collectively generates.

