\paragraph*{\bf Model.}
%\label{Model}

%\subparagraph*{\bf Social dilemmas in EGT}
%\label{socialdilemmas}

We consider pairwise symmetric games where each agent can choose between two pure strategies.
We focus on the so-called social dilemmas games, where the two available strategies are cooperation (C) and  defection (D).
A social dilemma game is completely defined by its normal representation, detailed by the following payoff matrix:
\begin{equation}
    \bordermatrix {
& C & D \cr
C \ \ & R & S \cr
D \ \ & T & P \cr}
\end{equation}
%
where $R$ is the \emph{reward} for mutual cooperation, $S$ is the \emph{sucker's payoff} for cooperating against a defector, $T$ is the \emph{temptation to defect}  against a cooperator, $P$ is the \emph{penalty} for mutual defection.
Being the game symmetric we report only the payoff for the row player. 

We adopt a convenient reparameterization of the above matrix, defined by two parameters \cite{tanimoto2007relationship, wang2015universal, ito2018scaling}: 
\begin{equation}
G(D_g,D_r) = 
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & -D_r \\
1+D_g & 0
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
where $D_g := T-R, \ D_r := P-S$, identified as \textit{gambling intending} 
 and \textit{risk adverting} \textit{dilemma strengths}, respectively.
In particular, higher values of $D_g$ promote defection by enhancing the temptation to defect $T$; higher values of $D_r$ promote defection penalizing betrayed cooperators by lowering $S$. 
Different combinations of the parameters $D_g,\ D_r \in [-1,1]$ encompass different games, represented in the space of dilemma strengths as a square where each quadrant defines a different social dilemma~\cite{pena2016, sadekar2024evolutionary}. Classical EGT results determine the equilibrium states in the well mixed scenario and unstructured populations, for each social dilemma: calling $\rho^*_B$ the fraction of cooperators at equilibrium for a baseline game $G_B$, we have the following picture:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Prisoner's Dilemma (PD): \\
    $D_g >0,\ D_r>0$, full defection $\rightarrow \rho^*_B = 0$ 
    \item Snowdrift Game (SD): \\
    $D_g >0,\ D_r<0$, mixed equilibrium $\rightarrow \rho^*_B \in (0,1)$
    \item Stag Hunt (SH): \\
    $D_g <0,\ D_r>0$, bistability $\rightarrow \rho^*_B = 0 \ \mathrm{or} \ 1$
    \item Harmony Game (HG): \\
    $D_g <0,\ D_r<0$, full cooperation $\rightarrow \rho^*_B = 1$ 
\end{itemize}

%\subsection{Coevolutionary game model}
We follow a co-evolutionary approach in which games are considered evolving: in our framework, both the strategies of the population and the game representing the environment change over time with reciprocal influence. Differently from previous co-evolutionary models, we simplify the assumption of the environment being driven by an external dynamical variable, instead describing the evolution of the game (representing the environmental setting) by introducing a direct dependence between the payoffs for different strategies and the level of cooperation $\rho$. Consequently, the population itself shapes the environment entirely through a simple co-evolution, in a way interpretable as a closed system with self-feedback. 

The general game dynamics of the model involves fixed baseline and target games, and the level of cooperation $\rho = \rho(t)$ which changes in time through the evolution of strategies in the population.
The full strategy-game co-dependence reads (omitting the implicit time dependence):
\begin{equation}
\label{payoff_matrix_rho}
\begin{split}
 & G(\rho, D_g,D_r,D^T_g,D^T_r) = [1-f(\rho)]G_B +f(\rho)G_T 
\\
& = [1-f(\rho)] \begin{pmatrix}
1 & -D_r \\
1+D_g & 0
\end{pmatrix} + f(\rho)
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & -D^T_r \\
1+D^T_g & 0
\end{pmatrix}, 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $f:[0,1]\rightarrow[0,1]$ can be a general mapping of the unit interval in itself, describing the strategy-payoff feedback. Natural choices of the functional form should be continuous, monotonic (for consistent feedback for increasing $\rho$), and account for synergistic effects \cite{hauert2006synergy}: %\fede{ref abbastanza volatile, però parlano di synergy}
%a basic example would be
the simplest specification is $f(\rho) = \rho^\lambda,\ \lambda \in (0,+\infty)$. Henceforth, the identical mapping $f(\rho) = \rho$, is considered, while the role of $\lambda$ is discussed in the Supplemental Material.
The \textit{feedback-driven} game structure is thus defined as a convex combination mediated by $f(\rho)$ between the baseline game $G_B = G(D_g, D_r)$ and a target game $G_T = G(D^T_g, D^T_r))$, defined by the baseline and target dilemma strengths respectively. As a consequence, the possible games lie between two extremes: the $G_B$ game, corresponding to $\rho=0$, and the $G_T$ game, corresponding to $\rho=1$.  
%Equivalently, the \textit{feedback-driven} game can be framed as a baseline payoff matrix $G_B$ ($\rho$ independent) plus an additional matrix $I$ expressing the type of feedback on the game, weighted by $f(\rho)$. The evolution from the baseline environment described by $G_B$ is governed entirely by the feedback matrix $I$, specified by four parameters: the baseline ($D_g$ and $D_r$) and target ($D^T_g$ and $D^T_r$) dilemma strengths. \\
%The above definition for game-strategy coevolution revolves on the idea that, starting from a baseline game, the payoffs defining the game shared by each agent will evolve in response to changes in the level of cooperation in the population. 
The interpretation of the strategy dynamics in our co-evolutionary model is made clear by considering two extreme choices of parameters \( D^T_g \) and \( D^T_r \) defining the target game: if $D^T_r = D^T_g = 1$, the feedback effectively acts as a driver for defection, pushing the game parameters to the most defective case of PD for higher levels of cooperation; conversely, if $D^T_r = D^T_g = -1$, the feedback is a cooperation incentive \cite{akccay2011evolution} that drives the game to the most cooperative case of HG. 
% We remark that the evolution is described at fixed game parameters only by one degree of freedom specified by $\rho$, jointly characterizing the evolution of cooperation and the game change in time elicited by the feedback on payoffs.
%\subsection{Replicator dynamics}

The interaction between agents in the model is well mixed, and the thermodynamic limit (i.e. infinite sized population) is assumed.
In this setting, the evolutionary dynamics of the level of cooperation $\rho$ of the system is given by the mean field replicator equation \cite{hofbauer2003evolutionary, hofbauer1998evolutionary, taylor1978evolutionary},
\begin{equation}
    \label{replicator}
\frac{d\rho}{dt} = \rho (1 - \rho) \Delta \Pi(\rho) \ ,
\end{equation}
where \( \Delta \Pi(\rho) = \Pi_C(\rho) - \Pi_D(\rho) \) is the expected payoff difference between cooperators and defectors. % Aside from the trivial equilibria at \( \rho = 0 \), \( \rho = 1 \), internal equilibria are found at any internal point where \( \Delta \Pi = 0 \).
Note that in EGT scenarios, where the fitness of agents depends on the abundance of strategies, the payoff term of the replicator equation does as well ~\cite{hofbauer1998evolutionary, doebeli2004evolutionary}. If nontrivial evolutionary stable states (i.e. rest points of eq.~\ref{replicator}) exist, it is due to the condition $ \Delta \Pi(\rho) = 0$ holding for specific values of $\rho$. 
The explicit expression for our model can be computed directly from eq.~\ref{payoff_matrix_rho}, and due to the mapping $f(\rho) = \rho$ we have:
\begin{equation}
\label{generaldeltapayoff}
    \Delta \Pi = \rho^2 ((D^T_r - D_r)+ (D_g - D^T_g)) + \rho (2D_r - D^T_r- D_g) - D_r \ .
\end{equation}
Upon specifying the four game parameters, the behavior of $\rho$ in time and therefore the existence of equilibria for the system is completely described by plugging eq.~\ref{generaldeltapayoff} into eq.~\ref{replicator}.
