\paragraph*{\bf Results.}\label{results}
The evolution of strategies dictates the evolution of the environment through the payoff feedback.
Depending on the target games, two scenarios are possible for an initial game $G=(1-\rho_0)G_B+\rho_0 G_T$ starting from a level of cooperation $\rho_0$ at $t=0$. 
The dynamics shifts the game to $G_{eq}=(1-\rho^*)G_B+\rho^*G_T$ while the population stabilizes to a nontrivial level of cooperation $\rho^* \in (0,1)$, or collapses to full defection/cooperation with $\rho^*=0,1$ respectively. The transition from the initial game played $G$ to the \textit{equilibrium game} $G_{eq}$ may lead to a shift in the social dilemma faced by the population. Moreover, while traditional results in the EGT formulation associate to each social dilemma specific evolutionary stable states $\rho^*_{eq}$, these do not strictly hold in our framework: depending on the social dilemma class, the system equipped with feedback may stabilize to equilibrium levels of cooperation $\rho^*$ unstable for $G_{eq}$ in the EGT formulation. We call such settings \textit{Chimera games}, as the behavior of the population is at odds with EGT results given the payoffs. %\fede{the system reaching an equilibrium $\rho^*$ ends up playing an equilibrium game $G_{eq}$ endowed with a different EGT eq. $\rho^*_{eq}$} the population playing a feedback-driven game at equilibrium (i.e. $G_{eq}$) doesn't converge to the level of cooperation stemming from classical EGT results. 
% \fede{This equilibrium might even represent the one of a social dilemma different from the one of $G_{eq}$. CHIMERA GAME} \\

