\textbf{Conclusions.}
Understanding the emergence of evolutionary stable states of cooperation, when populations evolve by interacting through a social dilemma, is a fundamental problem in evolutionary game theory~\cite{axelrod1981evolution, nowak2006five, hofbauer1998evolutionary, nowak2006evolutionary}. In real-world systems, the evolution of the environment is subject to influence from the cooperation level of the population, and several models have emerged in this direction ~\cite{weitz2016oscillating, tilman2020evolutionary, betz2024evolutionary, perc2010coevolutionary, gross2008adaptive}.
%Policy interventions are typically implemented through incremental incentives~\cite{liu2023coevolutionary, sadekar2024evolutionary}. 
Our work contributes to this growing field by detailing a minimal framework for systems endowed with an endogenous feedback between population strategies and the payoff structure of the game, without assuming any external dynamics~\cite{tilman2020evolutionary, weitz2016oscillating, ito2024complete} or stochasticity~\cite{delton2011evolution, hilbe2018evolution, sadekar2024evolutionary,civilini2024explosive}.
Results show that even deterministic endogenous feedbacks of simple specification can profoundly alter the evolutionary outcome, resulting in levels of cooperation driven by the feedback-induced change in game. In particular, a cooperative feedback may enable the population to transition from its initial cooperation level to full cooperation and a Harmony Game setting, from any baseline social dilemma class. Due to the opposite feedback favoring defection, EGT mixed equilibria are only slightly disrupted.
% \littlej{opens pathways to full cooperation}
%\littlej{In particular, the cooperative feedback enables the population to transition to full cooperation, regardless of the initial}
%If cooperation is enhanced via feedback, the system can reach full cooperation from a wide range of baseline game conditions, whereas if defection is promoted, the initial mixed equilibrium is only slightly disrupted%, consistently with recent findings in feedback-driven dynamics~\cite{tilman2020evolutionary, sadekar2024evolutionary, civilini2024explosive}
Most notably, a stability switching phenomenon is found when cooperation/defection is promoted in the SD/SH region, respectively. We referred to such scenarios as Chimera games.

These results, with no classical game theory analogue, have concrete implications for real-world systems. Policymakers must consider that dynamic responses to incentives may not just  shift the behavior of agents, but also disrupt the stability of cooperation by reshaping the very structure of strategic interactions, leading to hardly foreseeable outcomes through static payoff-based models. Incentive designs as dynamic feedback must account for these findings, recognizing that in social systems the environment is not a fixed backdrop but a malleable and co-evolving entity~\cite{weitz2016oscillating, tilman2020evolutionary, ito2024complete, sadekar2024evolutionary, gross2008adaptive, civilini2024explosive, civilini2021evolutionary, bowles2003co, sadekar2025drivers}. 

%Future work could explore how these dynamics unfold in \textit{structured populations}~\cite{szabo2007evolutionary, santos2005scale}, in \textit{higher-order interactions}~\cite{civilini2024explosive, civilini2021evolutionary}, or under \textit{stochastic and noisy feedback mechanisms}~\cite{sigdel2019convergence, tanimoto2007promotion, wang2024evolution}, thereby bridging the gap between minimal theoretical insights and complex socio-economic realities.

