\section*{\bf SUPPLEMENTAL MATERIAL}

\section{Simulations}

Here we detail the Monte Carlo simulation protocol performed to validate the analytical results.
The standard evolutionary framework adopted for simulations relies on the well mixed assumption and draws from the replicator equation \cite{szabo1998evolutionary, traulsen2006stochastic%, blume1993statistical
}. 
Consider a finite but sufficiently large population of agents, sharing a baseline game scenario determined by the payoff matrix $G_B$. The population is initialized to a cooperation level $\rho_0$, and each agent equipped with an initial strategy at random. This leads in our model to an initial feedback-driven game $\rho_0 G_B + (1-\rho_0)G_T$. 
Upon specifying the initial game dilemma strengths and the target dilemma strengths, the payoff matrix is completely defined. Note that populations endowed with different $G_B, \rho_0$ but same $\rho_0 G_B + (1-\rho_0)G_T$,  despite starting from the same game, can exhibit drastically different dynamics. \\ The system is evolved by agents updating their strategies from their interactions through an asynchronous process. In each round, a focal player $f$ and a model player $m$ are selected randomly. Each plays its strategy against $k$ randomly chosen players of the population collecting a total payoff $\Pi_f$, $\Pi_m$ respectively. A stochastic update process depending on the payoff difference drives the strategy update of the focal player: with probability given by \begin{equation}
    p = \frac{1}{1+\exp(-\omega(\Pi_m-\Pi_f))}
\end{equation}
where $\omega$ is the selection strength (chosen as $\omega = 0.1$), the focal player adopts the strategy of the model player. After each round the cooperation level is updated and thus the following round payoff matrix is defined via eq.~\ref{payoff_matrix_rho}.
The simulation steps are repeated until the system stabilizes and reaches equilibrium, either by collapsing into an absorbing state of full defection/cooperation, or by reaching a stable nontrivial level of cooperation.
The code used for simulation is available at \textit{https://github.com/GiacomoFrigerio/CoevChimeraGames}.

\section{Results on equilibria}

In the main text we showed the explicit expression for the delta payoff in our co-evolutionary framework, namely:
\begin{equation}
\label{generaldeltapayoff_supp}
    \Delta \Pi(\rho) = \rho^2 ((D^T_r - D_r)+ (D_g - D^T_g)) + \rho (2D_r - D^T_r- D_g) - D_r 
\end{equation}
We now discuss the properties of the solutions for the two target cases of target game, \textit{Prisoner's Dilemma} ($D^T_g=D^T_r=1$) and \textit{Harmony Game} ($D^T_g=D^T_r=-1$), which represent nontrivial rest points of the replicator equation
\begin{equation}
\label{replicator_supp}
    \frac{d\rho}{dt}=\rho (1-\rho)\Delta\Pi(\rho)
\end{equation}

\subsection{Prisoner's Dilemma target}
\noindent Equation ~\ref{generaldeltapayoff_supp} becomes:
\begin{equation}
     \rho^2 (D_g - D_r) + \rho (2D_r - D_g - 1) - D_r = 0 \label{deltaPD_supp}
\end{equation}
By the intermediate value theorem,
% Bolzano's theorem
at least a solution $\rho \in (0,1)$ exists in the region \( D_r < 0 \): in fact, this choice gives $\Delta\Pi(0) = -D_r >0$, while $\Delta\Pi(1) = -1 <0$. Due to the opposite value of $\Delta\Pi$ at the interval boundaries, and to the fact that the curve described by equation ~\ref{deltaPD_supp} has fixed concavity, %(polynomial of degree 2) 
it follows that this solution will also be unique.
The region of admissible equilibria, as detailed in the main text, spans the \textit{Harmony Game} (\( D_g < 0 \)) and \textit{Snowdrift Game} (\( D_g > 0 \)) class. \\
Numerically, one finds that no physical solutions $\rho \in [0,1]$ are admissible for the regions where $D_r>0$, spanning the \textit{Prisoner's Dilemma} (\( D_g < 0 \)) and \textit{Stag Hunt} (\( D_g > 0 \)). 
Being $\Delta\Pi<0$ for any physical $\rho$, in both regions full defection is always the absorbing state reached by the system governed by eq.~\ref{replicator_supp}.
\\

\noindent Concerning the stability of the solutions found in the region $D_r <0$, the Jacobian of the dynamical system $J(\rho) = \frac{\partial(d\rho/dt)}{\partial\rho}$, has to be studied at the stationary points; in fact, for our one dimensional case, it is simply the derivative. Using similar reasoning as before, it follows that a neighborhood of the root of eq.~\ref{deltaPD_supp} is always negative, therefore the equilibrium is stable independently of $D_g$.
Regardless of the initial value of cooperation level $\rho_0$, the system will reach the cooperation level given by $\rho^*$, solution of eq.~\ref{deltaPD_supp}.


\subsection{Harmony Game target}
\noindent Equation~\ref{generaldeltapayoff_supp} becomes:
\begin{equation}
     \label{deltaHG_supp}
     \rho^2 (D_g - D_r) + \rho (2D_r - D_g + 1) - D_r = 0 
\end{equation}

\noindent In this case we are guaranteed the existence of at least a solution $\rho \in (0,1)$ only in in the region \( D_r > 0 \): $\Delta\Pi(0) = -D_r <0$, while $\Delta\Pi(1) = 1 >0$. As before, such a solution will also be unique.
The region of admissible equilibria, as detailed in the main text, spans the \textit{Stag Hunt} (\( D_g < 0 \)) and \textit{Prisoner's Dilemma} (\( D_g > 0 \)) class. \\
Numerically, one finds that no physical solutions $\rho \in [0,1]$ are admissible for the regions where $D_r<0$, spanning the \textit{Harmony Game} (\( D_g < 0 \)) and \textit{Snowdrift Game} (\( D_g > 0 \)).
Being $\Delta\Pi>0$ for any physical $\rho$, in both regions full cooperation is always the absorbing state reached by the system governed by eq.~\ref{replicator_supp}. \\
\noindent Concerning the stability of the solutions found in the region $D_r >0$, using similar reasoning as before, it follows that a neighborhood of the root of eq.~\ref{deltaHG_supp} is always positive, therefore the equilibrium is unstable independently of $D_g$.
As such, depending on the relative ordering between the initial value of cooperation level $\rho_0$ and the solution of eq.~\ref{deltaHG_supp} $\rho^*$, the system will either reach full cooperation ($\rho_0>\rho^*$) or full defection ($\rho_0<\rho^*$).


\section{Chimera game characterization}

In the main text we discussed the existence of peculiar scenarios in which the dynamics brings the system towards an equilibrium for $\rho^*$, and as such for the game played $G_{eq}$, but $\rho^*\neq \rho_{eq}$. In particular, for the scenario where the target game $G_T$ is fixed to \textit{Prisoner's Dilemma} ($D_g=D_r=1$), and the baseline game $G_B$ lies in the \textit{Harmony Game} region of $D_g<D_r$, we highlighted the existence of an equilibrium game of the \textit{Stag Hunt} class, endowed with a cooperation level $\rho^*\in (0,1)$ of \textit{Snowdrift Game}-type (see Fig.~\ref{schematic_game_result_supp}).
Here, we show an explicit mapping between the SH game at equilibrium and such class of SD-type games.

