\section{Introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Poverty alleviation remains one of the most pressing policy challenges, especially in developing countries, where tight government budgets severely limit policy responses. To maximize the impact of scarce resources, governments have increasingly implemented targeted unconditional cash transfer (UCT) programs, which provide financial support to the poorest households. By 2020, UCT programs covered at least 1\% of the population in low-income countries \cite{banerjee2022social}, a reflection of their growing importance in the global policy landscape. Prominent examples of such programs include South Africa’s Child Support Grant and China’s Minimum Living Standard Scheme, each of which reaches millions of beneficiaries \cite{barrientos2013social}.

The success of targeted transfer programs hinges on the government's ability to accurately identify poor households, a task made difficult by widespread informality and administrative limitations, which often prevent reliable measurement of income or consumption. To address this, policymakers typically predict household income based on available information and allocate transfers accordingly. One common approach, offering simplicity and low implementation costs, is geographic targeting, whereby aid is delivered to all households within regions estimated to be poor \cite{baker1994poverty,elbers2007poverty}.\footnote{Geographic targeting allows policymakers to predict income and allocate transfers using only one dataset and without household-level data \cite{baker1994poverty,elbers2007poverty}. Examples of programs implementing this approach include the Productive Safety Net in Ethiopia, Challenging the Frontiers of Poverty Reduction in Bangladesh, and Chile Solidario \cite{barrientos2013social}.} Another widely used method is proxy means testing (PMT), whereby household income is predicted based on easily observable characteristics such as housing quality, education, and asset ownership.\footnote{PMT implementation involves using a representative survey to {\it train} a regression model to predict income at the household level and then applying the model to a broader dataset, typically a census or administrative registry, to target poor households. Examples of programs using PMT include SISBEN in Colombia, BISP in Pakistan, Progresa/Oportunidades in Mexico, and Takaful and Karama in Egypt \cite{barrientos2013social}.}

Targeting approaches used to identify impoverished households have been found to have high error rates in both inclusion and exclusion. Inclusion errors occur when transfers are provided to nonpoor households, while exclusion errors happen when impoverished households are not targeted. For example, \citeA{brown2018poor} find, for several African countries, that the inclusion error rate under a PMT approach is approximately 40\% and the exclusion error rate approximately 70\% when the poorest 20\% of the population is targeted.\footnote{In probabilistic terms, the inclusion error rate is the probability that a household is not poor conditional on its being identified as poor, while the exclusion error rate is the probability that a household is not identified as poor given that it is indeed poor.} Despite advancements in machine learning, more complex models have generally failed to significantly improve the precision of income targeting \cite{mcbride2018retooling, baez2019adaptive, areias2022machine, corral2025poverty}.

This paper offers a distinct yet related perspective by shifting the focus from improving prediction accuracy to optimizing the use of available data. Specifically, I take the estimates as given and focus on how policymakers can optimally allocate the antipoverty budget using these estimates. In this sense, my approach complements the literature that seeks to improve income prediction. 

Following \citeA{bourguignon1990poverty,bourguignon1997discontinuous}, I first characterize the optimal allocation of an antipoverty budget under complete information, where a benevolent planner implements an unconditional cash transfer program to assist the poorest individuals while adhering to a budget constraint. In practice, however, policymakers must rely on noisy income estimates from geographic targeting or PMT rather than true income data. This uncertainty transforms the allocation problem into a statistical decision problem: The planner must determine how to distribute transfers to maximize aid to the neediest while ensuring subsidies assistance (no taxation) and adherence to a budget constraint.

Given this setup and under the assumption of normality of the estimates, I show that the transfer assignment problem is equivalent to estimating the mean of a normally distributed variable with constraints on both the estimates (actions) and the mean (parameters). In particular, the parameter space is constrained by households' incomes being nonnegative, while the planner’s decision is restricted by the no-taxation condition (transfers must be nonnegative) and the budget constraint (total transfers cannot exceed the available budget). 

The equivalence between transfer assignment and constrained estimation allows the use of Stein’s lemma to establish that the plug-in rule is inadmissible. This rule, which is commonly used by policymakers, assigns transfers based directly on observed estimates while respecting the budget constraint. Specifically, I show that a James--Stein-type estimator achieves uniformly lower risk than the plug-in rule across the entire parameter space. While shrinkage estimators have traditionally been studied in the context of statistical estimation risk, I show that their benefits extend directly to policy decisions on transfer allocations. To my knowledge, this work is the first to make this connection. Overall, the dominance results suggest that {\it costless} improvements to the plug-in rule, such as introducing shrinkage, can enhance targeting accuracy without requiring additional data or complicating implementation.

To propose a more structured and flexible approach to shrinkage than the linear shrinkage of the James--Stein-type estimator, I present a nonparametric empirical Bayes (EB) method for poverty targeting. I begin by characterizing the oracle decision rule that minimizes Bayes risk, assuming the policymaker knows the true distribution of the parameters, namely the conditional expectation of income for each household. This yields a nonsmooth transfer rule: The optimal allocation depends on a thresholded version of the posterior mean, adjusted by a Lagrange multiplier that reflects the shadow price of budgetary resources. This transfer schedule minimizes the Bayes risk over all Borel measurable decision rules subject to the budget constraint, which means it weakly outperforms alternative methods restricted to narrower function classes \cite{JiangZhang2009}. %\footnote{EB is well suited for this setup because it enables flexible shrinkage, which can improve performance. Furthermore, in contrast to traditional Bayesian methods that require specifying a prior, EB estimates the prior from the data, which makes it more robust to misspecification and ensures strong performance under both Bayesian and frequentist criteria \cite{lehmann2006theory}. Similarly, by approximating the oracle rule without relying on a subjective prior, EB avoids concerns about subjectivity. Finally, thanks to advances in nonparametric maximum likelihood estimation \cite{koenker2014convex}, EB is computationally efficient and does not require costly simulations such as Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) simulations, which makes it practical for policymakers.} 

Since the true distribution of the conditional expectation of income is typically unknown, the oracle rule is generally infeasible and cannot be implemented directly. Hence, I follow the EB literature in approximating the optimal decision rule by estimating the prior using the nonparametric maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) \cite{kiefer1956consistency,koenker2014convex}. I then examine the theoretical statistical properties of the EB method in this setup. To measure performance, I define the Bayes regret as the difference in expected loss between the EB and oracle Bayes decision rules. Building on the results of \citeA{jiang2020general}, I show that, although the decision rule is a nonsmooth transformation of the posterior mean, the convergence rate of Bayes regret in the poverty-targeting problem is no slower than that of estimating the posterior mean under mean squared error loss. This finding highlights that optimal targeting decisions can be learned as efficiently as posterior means, even in the presence of budget and nonnegativity constraints.

Using consumption data from nine African countries collected by \citeA{brown2018poor}, I simulate a realistic targeting environment calibrated to the observed distribution of incomes and estimated noise levels. In this ideal setting, where the income estimates are assumed to be normally distributed, the empirical Bayes (EB) approach substantially outperforms conventional methods. Specifically, at a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of 0.25, the EB rule achieves 15--30\% of the loss reduction that would be possible under full-information transfers and performs nearly as well as an infeasible oracle benchmark that knows the true underlying distribution. In addition to improving aggregate performance, the EB method reduces exclusion errors by approximately 25 percentage points relative to the plug-in rule, allowing a substantially larger share of the poor population to be reached while maintaining a similar inclusion error rate.

In a more realistic exercise based on data from Mexico collected by \citeA{corral2025poverty}, I do not impose normality and instead use the actual distribution of the income estimates. In this case, the EB approach continues to outperform the plug-in rule, achieving a modest 2\% reduction in the gap relative to full-information targeting, on average. EB also delivers higher minimum targeting performance and substantially lower variability across samples. Compared to machine learning methods such as XGBoost, EB achieves better average targeting performance.

This paper contributes to several strands of the literature. In poverty targeting, most work emphasizes income prediction, often using plug-in rules under which true income is replaced with its estimate (see \citeNP{banerjee2022social}). A notable exception is \citeA{follett2023hybrid}, who introduce a Bayesian framework that blends PMT with community-based targeting, rather than directly improving targeting accuracy. In contrast, this paper adopts a statistical decision framework that moves beyond prediction to make better use of the available data. While related to work applying decision theory to policy design \cite{manski2004statistical, dehejia2005program, stoye2012minimax, tetenov2012statistical, kitagawa2018should, athey2021policy}, this paper shifts the focus from treatment assignment to targeting deprivation.

Second, this paper contributes to the predominantly statistical literature on the inadmissibility of constrained normal location estimators. While there is extensive research on the dominance of MLEs %%Editor: Please check that I have preserved your intended meaning here.
under restricted parameter spaces \cite{charras1992bayes, marchand2001improving, hartigan2004uniform, fourdrinier2018shrinkage}, these studies neither consider scenarios where the parameter space is a simplex nor address constraints on the action space. This paper extends these results by incorporating a constraint on the parameter space alongside an additional simplex constraint on the action space.

Third, this paper applies EB methods to a novel class of problems: constrained assignment problems with noisy estimates. While EB techniques have been extensively used for denoising and for ranking and selection tasks \cite{gu2023invidious, kline2022systemic, montiel2021empirical, chetty2014measuring, chetty2018impacts}, their application to constrained decision-making environments has been limited. Two recent exceptions are \citeA{moon2025optimal}, who develops an empirical Bayes approach for optimizing social welfare under policy uncertainty, and \citeA{kline2024discrimination}, who use EB methods to assign discrete grades to firms based on noisy estimates of discriminatory conduct, balancing informativeness against reliability. In this paper, I show that nonparametric empirical Bayes (NPMLE) approaches can significantly enhance performance in structured allocation problems, expanding the practical scope of EB methods to real-world scenarios where resources must be optimally deployed under uncertainty.

This paper contributes to the literature on Bayes regret for EB estimators. Early results by \citeA{zhang2009generalized} and \citeA{JiangZhang2009}, building on \citeA{kiefer1956consistency}, established regret bounds tied to the tail behavior of the prior. These were extended to multivariate and heteroskedastic settings by \citeA{Saha},  \citeA{jiang2020general}, and \citeA{soloff2021multivariate} and refined by \citeA{chen2023empirical} with the CLOSE-NPMLE. I add to this line of research by showing that a nonsmooth transformation of the posterior mean attains the same regret rates as the posterior mean itself.

The paper is organized as follows. Section \ref{sec:pov_target} introduces the poverty targeting model, starting from a full-information benchmark and extending it to the case of noisy income estimates. Section \ref{sec:inadmissible} establishes the inadmissibility of the plug-in rule and proposes a James--Stein-type shrinkage alternative. Section \ref{sec:Bayes} presents the EB framework, characterizes the oracle and feasible rules, and derives regret bounds. Section \ref{sec:sim_emp} provides simulation evidence comparing the EB rule to conventional targeting methods. Section \ref{sec:conclu} concludes.

