\section{Poverty Targeting Model \label{sec:pov_target}}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{Perfect Information \label{sec:perfect}}

\subsubsection{Setup}

In terms of notation, all vectors are assumed to be column vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^n\) unless otherwise noted. Throughout, \(\| \cdot \|\) denotes the standard Euclidean norm, i.e., \(\|\boldsymbol{x}\|^2 = \boldsymbol{x}^\top \boldsymbol{x}\) for any \(\boldsymbol{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n\). 

First, I characterize the optimal transfer assignments when the planner has perfect household income information.\footnote{Accurately measuring the income of poor households presents significant challenges in practice, and income alone often fails to provide a complete picture of household welfare. In developing countries, factors such as nonmarketed subsistence production and informal in-kind transfers play a crucial role in shaping people's livelihoods, making it difficult to capture their true economic well-being through income metrics alone. Additionally, seasonal income fluctuations further complicate efforts to measure household economic status consistently \cite{merfeld2023poverty}. For simplicity, I abstract from these complexities and assume that the planner can make perfectly informed targeting decisions based solely on household income.} I follow \citeA{bourguignon1990poverty,bourguignon1997discontinuous} to model the optimal decision of a benevolent social planner trying to minimize poverty. I assume there is a population of households with observable incomes, denoted by a column vector $\boldsymbol{y} = (y_{1},..., y_{n})^\top$.\footnote{For simplicity, I assume each household has a single member, abstracting from variation in household size.} The planner has to determine how to allocate a fixed but insufficient budget $B$ through income transfers $\boldsymbol{\tau} = (\tau_{1}, ..., \tau_{n})^\top$. I do not consider how the antipoverty budget $B$ is financed: the policymaker cares only about how to spend it (i.e., $\tau_{i} \geq 0,  i=1,...,n$). Let $z$ be the poverty line defined in terms of income.

To measure poverty, I consider the \citeA{foster1984class} measure:
\begin{align*}
	\rho_{\alpha} (y_{i} + \tau_{i}) =  \Bigg( \frac{z-y_{i}-\tau_{i}}{z} \Bigg)^{\alpha} \text{.}
\end{align*}
The power $\alpha$ can be interpreted as reflecting society's ethical preferences concerning poverty, referred to as ``aversion to poverty'' \cite{barrientos2013social}. Throughout, I assume that the planner aims to target the most deprived households and, for simplicity, set $\alpha = 2$.\footnote{\citeA{haushofer2022targeting} argue that focusing solely on deprivation is typically suboptimal for maximizing social welfare, even when redistribution is a priority.} Thus, the function $\rho_{2} (\cdot)$ is continuous, differentiable, and strictly convex. 

Unlike the planner in \citeA{bourguignon1990poverty,bourguignon1997discontinuous}, who evaluates poverty only among those below the threshold, the planner in this framework considers the poverty index of all households. With $\alpha = 2$, even households with income above the poverty line ($y_i \geq z$) contribute a positive loss, $(z - y_i)^2$, despite the fact that the planner cannot reduce this loss through taxation. This modeling choice plays a central conceptual role. It ensures that the planner directly accounts for the opportunity cost of allocating scarce resources to less poor households. While a transfer to a household just above the threshold may generate only a small inefficiency, transfers to substantially better-off households crowd out resources to those in greater need. The loss function therefore internalizes the full cost of inclusion errors and by penalizing both underpayment and overpayment, it reflects the planner’s dual concern with reaching the poor and avoiding waste.\footnote{The squared-gap loss is also analytically convenient. Its symmetry and differentiability allow the application of tools like Stein’s lemma, which underlie the admissibility results in Section~\ref{sec:inadmissible}. A monotone alternative such as $L_{+} = (z - y_i - \tau_i)_{+}^2$, which penalizes only under-transfers, breaks the quadratic orthogonality identity required for these comparisons. While the theoretical results do not directly extend to that setting, Appendix Figures~\ref{fig:sim_results1_plus} and~\ref{fig:sim_results2_plus} show that the empirical Bayes rule of Section~\ref{sec:Bayes} continues to outperform the plug-in rule under $L_{+}$, even though the James--Stein-type estimator performs slightly worse due to underspending.}

\subsubsection{Optimal Allocation}

Building on this setup, I now formalize the planner’s objective.\footnote{The model does not consider the potential impact of transfers on labor supply. This is consistent with empirical evidence suggesting that cash transfers do not significantly affect labor market behavior \cite{banerjee2017debunking,handa2018myth}.} Since $z$ is a constant and does not affect the optimal allocation, I work with the unscaled loss function. The planner seeks to allocate a limited budget $B$, which is typically smaller than the total poverty gap, $\sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbbm{1}\{y_i < z\} (z - y_i)$, through nonnegative transfers $\boldsymbol{\tau} = (\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n)^\top$ to minimize the average social loss from poverty:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq: theo_problem}
\begin{aligned}
	\min_{\boldsymbol{\tau} = (\tau_{1},..., \tau_{n})} \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Big(z-y_{i}-\tau_{i} \Big)^{2} \text{ subject to } & \text{(i) } \sum_{i}^{n} \tau_{i} \leq B, \\
	& \text{(ii) } \tau_{i} \geq 0, \forall i.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

That is, the planner aims to minimize the sum of squared poverty gaps subject to a fixed antipoverty budget $B$. Proposition~\ref{prop:full_info} states that the solution to this problem can be equivalently described in three ways: (i) through the Karush--Kuhn--Tucker (KKT) conditions, (ii) as an orthogonal projection, and (iii) using an iterative “progressive leveling-up” algorithm. The KKT conditions provide an analytical solution, the projection method offers a convenient characterization to derive the results of the next sections, and the ``leveling-up" algorithm provides an intuitive interpretation useful for policy implementation. The proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:full_info} and all subsequent proofs can be found in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}. 

\begin{proposition}
\proptitle{\hyperlink{proof:full_info}{Complete Information Policy}}
\label{prop:full_info} \hypertarget{prop:full_info}{}
The solution to optimization problem \eqref{eq: theo_problem} can be characterized equivalently in three ways:
	\begin{enumerate}

		\item Karush--Kuhn--Tucker Conditions. There exists a nonnegative Lagrange multiplier $\lambda>0$ (for the budget constraint) and nonnegative multipliers $\xi_i \geq 0$ (for the nonnegative constraints) such that 
		\begin{align}
		\label{eq: tau_star}
			\tau_{i}^{\star} & = \max \Bigg\{ 0, z- y_{i} - \frac{\lambda}{2}  \Bigg\} \text{, } i =1,...,n \text{.}
		\end{align}

		\item Projection onto Simplex. The optimal transfer is the unique Euclidean projection of $\boldsymbol{1}z - \boldsymbol{y}$ onto $\mathcal{A}$: 
		\begin{align*}
			\boldsymbol{\tau^{\star}} = \arg \min_{\boldsymbol{\tau} \in \mathcal{A}} || \boldsymbol{\tau} -(\boldsymbol{1}z - \boldsymbol{y}) ||^2,
		\end{align*}
		where  \(\boldsymbol{1} = (1, \dots, 1)^\top\) is the \(n\)-dimensional vector of ones.

		\item Progressive “Leveling-Up” Algorithm. Without loss of generality, let $y_{1} < y_{2} < ... < y_{n}$. Then, there exists an integer $p$ and a nonnegative income level $\underline{y}$ with $y_p \leq \underline{y} < y_{p+1}$ such that the optimal transfer is 
		\begin{equation*}
	\tau_{i}^{\star} =
	\begin{cases}
		\underline{y} - y_{i} & \text{ if } i =1,...,p \\
		0 & \text{ if } i = p+1,..., N 
	\end{cases}
	\text{,}
	\end{equation*}
	where $\underline{y}$ and $p$ are given by
	\begin{align*}
		\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} (y_{p} - y_{i}) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{p} (\underline{y} - y_{i}) = B \leq \sum_{i=1}^{p} (y_{p+1} - y_{i}) \text{.}
	\end{align*}
	\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

The first characterization follows directly from applying the KKT conditions to the strictly convex minimization problem. Because the objective is a sum of squared terms and the constraints are linear, the KKT system is both necessary and sufficient for optimality. Equation \eqref{eq: tau_star} implies that when a household's ``budget-adjusted'' poverty gap $z- y_{i} - \frac{\lambda}{2}$ is positive, the household receives a transfer; otherwise, it receives none. The Lagrange multiplier $\lambda$ is the shadow price of public funds: It ensures that the total resources allocated to households do not exceed the budget.\footnote{Let $\mathrm{T} (\lambda)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\max\{0,z-y_i-\lambda/2\}$. $\mathrm{T}(\lambda)$ is continuous and strictly decreasing on $[0,\infty)$ with $\mathrm{T}(0)=\sum_i(z-y_i)>0$ and $\lim{\lambda\to\infty}\mathrm{T}(\lambda)=0$. Hence, a unique $\lambda^{*}\geq 0$ solving $\mathrm{T}(\lambda^{*})=\min\{\mathrm{T}(0),B\}$ exists by the intermediate-value theorem; if $\Phi(0)\le B$, then $\lambda^{*}=0$ (budget slack); otherwise, $\lambda^{*}>0$, and the budget binds. This monotonicity argument applies verbatim whether the planner works with $y_i$, the plug-in rule, the posterior means, or their EB estimates, guaranteeing a well-defined and unique transfer vector in every case.} Intuitively, the planner chooses $\lambda$ to “trim” each individual’s poverty gap by a uniform amount, reflecting the fact that a single marginal dollar of transfers must be allocated where it reduces the overall sum of squared poverty gaps the most.

An alternative viewpoint is geometric: The feasible set $\{ \boldsymbol{\tau} : \sum_{i=1}^{n} \tau_i \leq B, \tau_i \geq 0 \}$ is a simplex in $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n}$. Because the objective function can be written as a squared euclidean distance between $ \boldsymbol{\tau}$ and the poverty-gap vector $\boldsymbol{1}z - \boldsymbol{y}$, the unique optimal solution is precisely the orthogonal projection of $\boldsymbol{1}z - \boldsymbol{y}$ onto that simplex.

A more constructive interpretation emphasizes the intuitive way in which the planner allocates transfers from the bottom of the income distribution upward. Starting with the poorest household, the planner incrementally equalizes its income with that of the next poorest, pooling resources to ``level them up'' together. This process continues, one household at a time, until the available budget is exhausted. Formally, there exists an income level \( y \in [y_p, y_{p+1}) \) and an integer \( p \) such that all households with \( i \leq p \) receive transfers bringing their income up to \( \underline{y} \) while households with \( i > p \) receive no transfers. The level \( \underline{y} \) and cutoff \( p \) are determined jointly so that the budget is exactly spent, i.e., \( \sum_{i=1}^{p} (y - y_i) = B \).

\subsection{Noisy Measurements \label{sec:noisy}}

The solution to the antipoverty budget assignment problem in Section \ref{sec:perfect} yields an optimal transfer for each household $i=1,...,n$. Nevertheless, the assignment rule is not feasible without direct knowledge of the income vector $\boldsymbol{y}$. As discussed in the introduction, it is uncommon for policymakers to have a precise measure of income or consumption for poor households in developing countries. In this subsection, I consider the problem of finding the optimal transfer when the planner observes only noisy measurements of household income. 

The targeting decisions are based on predictions of income generated by parametric or nonparametric specifications of observable variables $W_i$, such as household characteristics or geographic location. These predictions, commonly implemented via PMT or geographic targeting, are trained with separate data sources and are treated here as primitives. In other words, the planner is not involved in building the model but still must act upon its outputs.

Formally, the planner observes a noisy estimate $\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}} = (\hat{Y}_{1},..., \hat{Y}_{n})^\top$ and its standard error $\boldsymbol{\sigma} = (\sigma_{1}, ..., \sigma_{n})^\top$. The predictions $\hat{Y}_i$ are interpreted as noisy estimates of the conditional expectation $\mathbb{E}[y_i \mid W_i] = \mu_i$. Specifically, $\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}$ is a random vector taking values in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and whose realizations $\boldsymbol{\hat{y}} = (\hat{y}_{1},..., \hat{y}_{n})^\top$ are referred as {\it data}. Motivated by the central limit theorem, I assume normality of the estimates and consider them a noisy but unbiased signal of $\mu_i$. In addition, to rule out degenerate or pathological cases, I bound $\sigma_i^2$ within a minimum and maximum range.

\vspace{0.25cm}
\hypertarget{ASSUME}{} \clr{
\label{assump:normality} \hypertarget{assump:normality}{}
\noindent \assume{1}{Normality with Bounded Variance} 
Let $W_{i}$ be the vector of covariates used to predict income for each household $i=1,...,n$. The income estimates $\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}$ are conditionally normally distributed, centered at their conditional mean, and independent across $i$: 
	\begin{align}
	\label{eq: normality}
		\hat{Y}_{i} \mid \mu_{i}, \sigma_i  \overset{\mathrm{ind.}}{\sim}  \mathcal{N} ( \mu_i, \sigma_i^{2}) \text{ , } i =1,...,n\text{,}
	\end{align}
	where $\mu_i = \mathbb{E}[y_i \mid W_i] \in \mathbb{R}_{+}$ and $\sigma_i^{2} \in \mathbb{R}_{++}$ is known and constant. Furthermore, for all $i=1,...n$, $0 < \sigma_{\min}^2 \leq \sigma_i^2 \leq \sigma_{\max}^2$.
}
\vspace{0.25cm}

The following two remarks discuss why the normality assumption is a reasonable approximation under the two most common targeting approaches: geographic targeting and PMT.

\begin{titledRemark}{Geographic Targeting} \label{remark:geo} Geographic targeting is a frequently used approach in poverty alleviation programs whereby specific regions or communities with high poverty rates are identified and prioritized for assistance. Although policymakers aim to target regions rather than individual households, there is a direct mapping between the two problems. In this setting, the objective function must be redefined to depend on region-level income rather than household-level income. However, because each region can still be treated as a single unit with an associated signal and target, the structure of the problem remains unchanged in terms of the budget constraint, the action space, and the decision rule.

	The approach consists of using representative survey data to estimate the average income and then target geographic regions using that estimate. Specifically, the estimate is given by
	\begin{align*}
		\hat{y}_{j} = \frac{1}{n_{j}} \sum_{i=1}^{n_{j}} y_{ij}, \quad j=1,...,J  \text{, }
	\end{align*}
	where $j$ denotes the administrative region, $y_{ij}$ the income or consumption of households in region $j$, and  $n_{j}$ the number of households in region $j$. That is, in the regression framework, the covariates $W_i$ used for prediction are simply a set of region-specific dummy variables indicating the household’s location. 

	The normal distribution assumption is reasonable as long as the population from which the samples are drawn has a finite variance and the effective sample size for each administrative region is relatively large. That is, as the sample size grows to infinity,
	\begin{align*}
		\frac{\hat{y}_{j} - \mu_{j}}{\sigma_{j}} \xrightarrow{\;d\;} \mathcal{N}(0, 1), \quad j=1,...,J \text{,}
	\end{align*}
	where $\mu_j$ is the true average income and $\sigma_j$ is the standard error of the estimate for region $j$. The same argument extends to more sophisticated approaches that use census data, administrative datasets, or satellite information to target smaller geographic units (e.g., \citeNP{elbers2003micro,blumenstock2015predicting}). 
\end{titledRemark}

\begin{titledRemark}{Proxy Means Testing} \label{ex: pmt} Under PMT, observable household characteristics, such as household assets or housing quality, are taken as proxies for household income or consumption. These characteristics are used to construct a poverty score that, in turn, determines eligibility for the program. Specifically, on representative survey data, the following regression is estimated by ordinary least squares (OLS): 
	\begin{align}
	\label{eq:ols}
		y_{i} = W_{i}'\beta + \epsilon_{i} \text{, } \quad i =1,..., n \text{,}
	\end{align}
	where  $i$ denotes the household, $y_i$ is income or consumption, and $W_{i}$ is a vector of covariates observable for the entire population (family size, housing conditions, etc.). Then, based on a dataset of the entire relevant population (usually a census or administrative registry), the predicted income, $\hat{y}_{i} = W_{i}'\hat{\beta}$, is used for targeting.\footnote{Strictly speaking, the predicted incomes $\hat y_{i}=W_{i}'\hat\beta$ are not independent across households because they share the common first-stage estimation error $\hat\beta-\beta$ which induces pairwise covariances $\operatorname{Cov}(\hat y_{i},\hat y_{j})=W_{i}'\operatorname{Var}(\hat\beta)W_{j}=\mathcal{O}(n^{-1})$ since $\operatorname{Var}(\hat\beta)=O(n^{-1})$ under standard OLS assumptions. These $1/n$-order correlations disappear as the survey size $n$ grows, so the marginal normal approximation remain valid to first order.}


	If the support of covariates is finite $W_{i} \in \{w_{1}, ..., w_{K} \}$ and regression \eqref{eq:ols} is fully saturated, the predicted income is the average income for each of the $K$ different types of households:
	\begin{align*}
		\hat{y}_{k} = \frac{1}{n_{k}} \sum_{i=1}^{n_{k}} y_{ik} \text{, } \quad k =1,...,K \text{.}
	\end{align*}
	In this case, the planner would target households indirectly by targeting types of households, as in the previous example where it targeted geographic units. Thus, the same argument regarding the normal approximation holds. If the support of covariates is infinite or regression \eqref{eq:ols} is not saturated, the normality of $\hat{y}_{i}$ rests on the normality of the OLS estimator as the sample size grows to infinity. Specifically, under $\mathbb{E}[W'\epsilon]=0$ and the matrix $\mathbb{E}[W'W]$ having full rank, $\hat{\beta}$ is approximately normally distributed around $\beta$ with variance $\mathbb{E}[W'W]^{-1} \mathbb{E}[\epsilon^{2} W'W] \mathbb{E}[W'W]^{-1}/n$ \cite{wooldridge2010econometric}.
\end{titledRemark}


Let the vector \( \boldsymbol{\mu} = (\mu_{1}, \ldots, \mu_{n})^\top \) denote households' conditional mean income. This vector represents the parameters and is assumed to belong to the parameter space \( \Theta \), which is given by $\Theta = \{ \boldsymbol{\mu} = (\mu_{1},..., \mu_{n})^\top \mid \boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathbb{R}_{+}^{n} \}$ \footnote{I assume $\Theta$ encompasses all information relevant for evaluating the planner's actions. That is, I consider the parameter space to be equivalent to the set of possible {\it  states of the world}, which capture the planner's uncertainty about the consequences of its actions.} . 

For each household $i$, after the planner observes the data $\hat{Y}_{i} = \hat{y}_{i}$, it has to determine what the size of the transfer $\tau_{i}$ should be. Since the planner is not allowed to tax households and has to respect the budget constraint, the action space is $\mathcal{A} = \left\{ \boldsymbol{x} \in \mathbb{R}_{+}^n \;\middle|\; \boldsymbol{1}^\top \boldsymbol{x} \leq B \right\} $.


Motivated by the model in Section~\ref{sec:perfect}, the loss function \( L: \mathcal{A} \times \Theta \to \mathbb{R} \) faced by the planner, which captures the poverty index under the choice of action \( \boldsymbol{t} = (t_1, \ldots, t_n)^\top \) subject to the budget constraint, is given by
\begin{align*}
	L(\boldsymbol{t}, \boldsymbol{\mu}) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n}  \Big( z-\mu_{i}-t_{i}\Big)^{2}  \text{.} %+ \lambda \Bigg(\sum_{i}^{N} \tau_{i} - B  \Bigg) \text{,}
\end{align*}
This loss function reflects the planner's objective, which is to minimize the poverty index as determined by household conditional mean income including transfers, subject to a feasibility constraint that total transfers cannot exceed the available antipoverty budget \( B \).  That is, the planner must balance the gain from more transfers and the additional cost in terms of approaching the budget constraint. Crucially, since the planner observes only the noisy estimates of income $\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}$, there is uncertainty about the poverty index of each action. 

A statistical decision rule in this problem is a mapping $\boldsymbol{\tau}: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathcal{A}$ that, for each observed value of the data $\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$, indicates what action in space $\mathcal{A}$ to take. In other words, for each possible realization of the income {\it data}, a statistical decision rule has to specify the transfer amount each household should receive. Note that the action space does not coincide with the parameter space as in traditional estimation problems, as the planner is forced to satisfy the budget constraint. Let $\mathcal{D} = \{ \boldsymbol{\tau}:   \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathcal{A} \}$ denote the set of all measurable decision rules.

The next remark provides an important insight: Minimizing the loss with respect to $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ is equivalent (up to a constant) to minimizing it with respect to $\mathbf{y}$. 

% Here the expectation is w.r.t. the distribution of epsilon_i
\begin{titledRemark}{Loss Equivalence Between \texorpdfstring{$\boldsymbol y$}{y} and \texorpdfstring{$\boldsymbol\mu$}{mu}}
\label{remark:losses}
Let $y_i=\mu_i+\varepsilon_i$ with $\mu_i=\mathbb{E}[y_i\mid W_i]$ and
$\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i\mid W_i]=0$.  For any (measurable) decision rule
$\boldsymbol\tau(W)=(\tau_1(W_1),\ldots,\tau_n(W_n))$, the quadratic
loss satisfies, household by household,
\[
\begin{aligned}
L_i(\tau_i,y_i)
&=\mathbb{E}\bigl[(z-y_i-\tau_i(W_i))^{2}\bigr]\\
&=\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[(z-\mu_i-\tau_i(W_i))^{2}\bigr]
      -2\,\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[(z-\mu_i-\tau_i(W_i))\,\varepsilon_i\bigr]
      +\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i^{2}]\\
&=L_i(\tau_i,\mu_i)\;+\;\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i^{2}],
\end{aligned}
\]
where the cross-term vanishes by $\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i\mid W_i]=0$ and the
law of iterated expectations.  Summing over $i=1,\dots,n$ gives
\[
L(\boldsymbol\tau,\boldsymbol y)\;=\;L(\boldsymbol\tau,\boldsymbol\mu)
      \;+\;\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i^{2}],
\]
and the additive constant $\sum_i\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i^{2}]$ is independent
of $\boldsymbol\tau$.  Hence, optimizing with respect to the (unobserved) true income is
isomorphic to optimizing with respect to the conditional mean
$\boldsymbol\mu$.  Even though the planner ultimately cares about true income, all subsequent risk comparisons can therefore be
conducted with~$\boldsymbol\mu$ without loss of generality.
\end{titledRemark}

The mean loss or (frequentist) risk function $\mathcal{R}: \mathcal{D} \times \Theta \to \mathbb{R}$ of a statistical decision rule $\boldsymbol{\tau}$ is given by
\begin{align*}
	\mathcal{R} (\boldsymbol{\tau}, \boldsymbol{\mu}) = \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}} [L(\boldsymbol{\tau}, \boldsymbol{\mu})] \text{,}
\end{align*}
where the expectation is taken with respect to the probability distribution of $\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}$ so $\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{\tau}, \boldsymbol{\mu})$ represents the average loss to the planner when \( \boldsymbol{\tau} \) is used across different realizations of the data.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Inadmissibility of Plug-In Rule \label{sec:inadmissible}}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{Equivalence}

The previous section describes a statistical decision problem in which the planner allocates transfers to minimize expected poverty given noisy income observations. To facilitate the analysis and leverage existing statistical results, it is useful to recast this problem in an equivalent form. Specifically, rather than framing it as a transfer assignment problem, we can view it as the problem of estimating a Gaussian location vector under convex constraints.

Specifically, let the observed poverty gap be given by $X_i = z-\hat{Y}_i \sim N( \theta_i, \sigma_{i}^{2})$ for $i=1,...,N$, where $\theta_i = z- \mu_i$. Crucially, although this is set as a transfer assignment problem, it is equivalent to the problem of selecting an estimator $\boldsymbol{\tau}(\boldsymbol{X}) \in \mathcal{A}$ to minimize loss $||\boldsymbol{\theta}  - \boldsymbol{\tau} (\boldsymbol{X}) ||^{2}$ while observing $\boldsymbol{X} = (\boldsymbol{1}z - \boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}) \in \mathbb{R}^n$ drawn from a normal distribution with location $\boldsymbol{\theta} \in (-\infty, z]^{n}$. In other words, it is equivalent to the problem of estimating the Gaussian location vector when the estimator and the parameter are constrained to be in convex subsets of $\mathbb{R}^{n}$. Proposition \ref{prop:equivalence} formalizes the equivalence between the two problems. 

\begin{proposition}
\proptitle{\hyperlink{proof:equivalence}{Problem Equivalence}}
\label{prop:equivalence} \hypertarget{prop:equivalence}{} The problem of optimally allocating transfers to minimize expected poverty based on noisy income observations is statistically equivalent to the constrained Gaussian location estimation problem in the following sense:  
There exist bijective mappings between the parameter spaces, sample spaces, and action spaces of the two problems that:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item preserve the sampling distributions,
	\item preserve the loss incurred by each action--parameter pair, and
	\item induce a one-to-one correspondence between decision rules that preserve their risk functions at all parameter values.
\end{enumerate}
Hence, every decision rule in one problem corresponds uniquely to a rule in the other with the same risk at each parameter value.
\end{proposition}



Using this equivalence to the problem of estimating a Gaussian location vector, I show in this section that the projection of \( \boldsymbol{X} \) onto \( \mathcal{A} \), known as the plug-in rule, is dominated by a James--Stein-type estimator that achieves uniformly lower risk across the entire parameter space.

\vspace{-0.5cm}
\subsection{Plug-In Rule}

To simplify the exposition and facilitate comparison with traditional shrinkage estimators, I assume throughout this subsection that the income estimates are conditionally independent and homoskedastic, i.e., $\hat{Y}_{i} \mid \mu_i \overset{\mathrm{ind.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu_i, \sigma^2), \quad i = 1, \dots, n$, where the variance $\sigma^2 $ is known and constant across households.

A plug-in rule is a decision-making approach in which the unknown parameter of interest is replaced by its estimate when decisions are made. That is, the action that optimizes the decision criterion is chosen based on an estimated value, typically obtained through maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) or the method of moments, rather than the true, unobserved parameter \cite{hirano2019statistical}.

In the context of poverty targeting, the plug-in rule involves using estimates of household incomes to determine the optimal allocation of transfers. Specifically, the planner applies the full-information allocation rule from Proposition~\ref{prop:full_info} using predicted incomes in place of the true values. That is, transfers are computed as if the noisy estimates were fully accurate.

By Proposition~\ref{prop:full_info}, the plug-in estimator is the projection of the observed poverty gap $\boldsymbol{X} = \boldsymbol{1}z - \boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}$ onto the set $\mathcal{A}$. Formally, it can be defined as the solution to the convex optimization problem
\[
\boldsymbol{\tau}^{\text{plug}}(\boldsymbol{X}) = \arg\min_{\boldsymbol{\tau} \in \mathcal{A}} \| \boldsymbol{X} - \boldsymbol{\tau} \|^2.
\]
This corresponds to finding the vector of feasible transfers that is closest (in euclidean norm) to the observed poverty gaps. Intuitively, the solution sets to zero any components of \( \boldsymbol{X} \) that are negative. In other words, households estimated to be above the poverty line receive no transfer, and the remaining components are scaled to ensure that the budget constraint is satisfied.

The projection admits a closed-form solution based on a thresholding rule. Let $\gamma^{\star} \in \mathbb{R}$ denote the unique value such that $\sum_{i=1}^n \max\{0, X_i - \gamma^{\star}\} = B$. Then, the plug-in estimator is given by
\[
\tau^{\text{plug}}_i(X) = \max\{0, X_i - \gamma^{\star}\}, \quad i = 1, \dots, n.
\]
This estimator is piecewise linear and Lipschitz continuous; it is differentiable almost everywhere, and the associated projection map is nonexpansive. That is, for any \( X, X' \in \mathbb{R}^n \), we have \( \| \boldsymbol{\tau}^{\text{plug}}(X) - \boldsymbol{\tau}^{\text{plug}}(X') \| \leq \| X - X' \| \). These properties play a central role in the analysis of the estimator's risk and in the application of Stein-type dominance results. Appendix~\ref{app:weakdiff} provides a detailed analysis of the mathematical properties of this estimator that are necessary to study its risk and apply Stein-type results.

\subsection{Dominance Results}

The plug-in estimator \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\), while natural and interpretable, is known to be inadmissible in constrained settings. This result follows from the work of \citeA{charras1992bayes}, which shows that estimators lying on the boundary of compact parameter spaces are generally dominated under squared error loss. The intuition is rooted in complete class theorems: Admissible estimators must be Bayes estimators with respect to some prior, and Bayes estimators with nondegenerate priors rarely take boundary values.\footnote{I focus on nonrandomized estimators, as the combination of a quadratic loss and a convex constraint set implies that the class of nonrandomized rules is essentially complete; see \citeA{ferguson2014mathematical}.} Importantly, in the current proof of the inadmissibility of the plug-in rule, I do not determine which estimators dominate. 

In this subsection, I extend previous results in the statistics literature that have shown that the natural estimator with $L_{2}$ loss is dominated by a James--Stein-type shrinkage estimator when the action space is restricted to a polyhedral cone \cite{fourdrinier2018shrinkage}. Specifically, in this section, I propose and analyze a James--Stein-type estimator that improves upon \(\tau^{\text{plug}}\) in terms of risk. Classical Stein-type results rely on the availability of weak derivatives for both the estimator and its normalized direction, which are established in Appendix~\ref{app:weakdiff}.

Let \(\mathcal{K}(X) = \{ i : \tau_i^{\text{plug}}(X) > 0 \}\) denote the set of indices where the plug-in estimator is positive, and let \(s = |\mathcal{K}(X)|\) denote its cardinality. Define also
\[
g(X) := \frac{\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^2},
\]
the normalized direction of the plug-in estimator. The constrained James--Stein estimator is given by
\begin{align}
\label{eq:js}
\tau^{\text{JS}}(X) = \left( 1 - \frac{\sigma^2 (s - 3)}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^2} \right)_{\text{shrink}} \cdot \tau^{\text{plug}}(X),
\end{align}
where the truncation operator \((x)_{\text{shrink}}\) is defined as $(x)_{\text{shrink}} := x$ if $0 < x < 1$ and $(x)_{\text{shrink}} :=1$ otherwise. Note that for any $s \leq 3$, $\tau^{\text{JS}}(X) = \tau^{\text{plug}}(X)$. 

The purpose of the truncation is twofold. First, it ensures that shrinkage is applied only when it is actually expected to improve risk: If the optimal shrinkage factor \( 1 - \frac{\sigma^2 (s-3)}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^2} \) lies strictly between zero and one, then partial shrinkage is beneficial and is applied. If the factor is either negative (implying excessive shrinkage) or greater than one (implying that no shrinkage is needed), the estimator defaults to the plug-in rule with no modification. 

Second, the truncation guarantees that the estimator remains in the action space. Since \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\) belongs to the simplex by construction, any positive scaling by a factor between 0 and 1 preserves both the nonnegativity of the components and the total sum constraint. By setting the shrinkage factor to 1 when necessary, the construction avoids any possibility of the estimator moving outside the simplex.

The classical James--Stein estimator improves risk performance by shrinking the observed data toward a central point, exploiting the fact that in high dimensions, such shrinkage can yield uniformly lower risk under squared loss. However, in the poverty-targeting problem, this idea must be adapted to respect the planner’s constraints: namely, that transfers must be nonnegative and the total allocation must remain within the available budget. The plug-in estimator \( \tau^{\text{plug}}(X) \) satisfies these constraints by construction but can still exhibit high variance, particularly when many components are active.

To address this, the James--Stein-type estimator \( \tau^{\text{JS}}(X) \) applies shrinkage selectively, only when doing so is expected to reduce risk without violating feasibility. The estimator reduces the variance of \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\) by shrinking its magnitude toward zero, especially in high-dimensional settings where \(s > 3\). This mirrors the classical James--Stein insight that, for estimation of a high-dimensional mean vector, a shrunken estimate can achieve lower risk. In the constrained setting, the shrinkage intensity is governed by the ratio \( \sigma^2 (s - 3) / \| \tau^{\text{plug}}(X) \|^2 \), which captures the trade-off between variance reduction and bias.

The next proposition formalizes the risk improvement offered by the James--Stein-type estimator over the plug-in rule in high-dimensional settings.

\begin{proposition}
\proptitle{\hyperlink{proof:JamesStein}{James--Stein-Type Estimator Dominance}}
\label{prop:JamesStein} \hypertarget{prop:JamesStein}{}
	If \(\mathbb{E}[\|g(X)\|^2] < \infty\), then the James--Stein-type estimator \(\tau^{\text{JS}}(X)\) in \eqref{eq:js} strictly dominates the plug-in rule \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\) whenever \( \Pr ( s >3,  0<\sigma^2 [s -3] <  ||\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)||^2 ) >0 \).
\end{proposition}

As in the unrestricted case, Stein's lemma \cite{stein1981estimation} is key to finding a dominating estimator since it provides a mechanism to calculate the expected risk reduction when an estimator is shrunk toward a central point. By leveraging the Stein lemma, I can express one of the expectations in the risk calculation as a function of $\boldsymbol{X}$ and not of the parameters, which allows to show that the James--Stein-type estimator has lower risk than the plug-in rule. The fundamental condition for the use of Stein's lemma is that the estimator $\boldsymbol{\tau}^{\text{plug}}(X)$ and  its normalized direction $\frac{\boldsymbol{\tau}^{\text{plug}}(X)}{||  \boldsymbol{\tau}^{\text{plug}}(X) ||^{2}}$ are weakly differentiable, which is verified in Appendix~\ref{app:weakdiff}.

To clarify the distinction between the plug-in rule and the James--Stein-type shrinkage rule, I include a simple numerical illustration with five households. The households have the following income estimates $\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}} = (20, 35, 45, 60, 100)^\top$, the poverty line is $z = 100$ and the planner's budget is $B=100$. The plug-in rule projects the estimated poverty gaps onto the budget simplex, allocating \( (45, 30, 20, 5, 0)^\top \) units across the five households. It disburses the entire budget but heavily favors the household with the largest (and possibly noisiest) gap. In contrast, the James--Stein rule multiplies this allocation by a shrinkage factor to reduce overall risk. In this example, the shrinkage factor is approximately $0.731$, producing the shrunk allocation $(32.9, 21.9, 14.6, 3.7, 0)^\top$. This rule retains the ranking of needs but distributes transfers more conservatively, spending only 73\% of the budget.

\begin{figure}[h!]
  \centering
   \caption{Transfer allocations under the plug in and James--Stein--type rules.}
  \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{Figs/js_vs_plugin_plot.png}
  \label{fig:js-vs-plugin}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:js-vs-plugin} plots these allocations. The plug in estimator spends every dollar but may over-allocate to extreme, noise-driven signals. The James--Stein rule trims these values proportionally, reducing exposure to estimation error and lowering mean squared error uniformly across all states of the world. While the resulting under-spending may appear suboptimal, it is in fact \emph{optimal} under a risk minimization objective: Proposition \ref{prop:JamesStein} guarantees that, so long as at least three households receive positive transfers, the James--Stein rule achieves strictly lower risk than the plug in benchmark. Intuitively, the shrinkage mechanism dampens large, potentially spurious over-allocations, which are especially costly under the squared loss.

Even though the proposed James--Stein-type estimator dominates the plug-in rule, it is likely not admissible. In the unrestricted case, shrinkage estimators such as the James--Stein estimator or the positive-part Stein estimator are themselves inadmissible, as shown by \citeA{shao1994improving}. This suggests that when the location parameter is constrained to lie in the simplex, there may be alternative estimators that dominate the proposed $\boldsymbol{\tau}^{\text{JS}}(\boldsymbol{X})$. 


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{(Empirical) Bayes Rules \label{sec:Bayes}}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{EB Framework}

In the previous section, I established that the conventional plug-in rule is inadmissible: it can be uniformly improved upon with no additional information. However, inadmissibility, while important, provides only negative guidance. It rules out bad procedures but offers no clear path to selecting a good one. Without a principled way to choose among admissible alternatives, policymakers risk making arbitrary or inefficient decisions. To move from negative to positive guidance, I adopt the \textit{Bayes risk principle}, which prescribes selecting the decision rule that minimizes the expected loss averaged over both the data and a prior distribution on the parameters.

The Bayes risk principle formalizes the idea that a good decision rule should perform well not just on the observed data but across all possible parameter values, weighted by how plausible they are under a prior distribution. Formally, for a general nonnegative loss function that evaluates the consequences of a decision rule $\boldsymbol{\tau}$ at a parameter vector $\boldsymbol{\mu}\sim G$, the Bayes risk minimizer $\boldsymbol{\tau^{*}}$ solves:
\[
\boldsymbol{\tau^{*}} \in \arg \min_{\tau \in \mathcal{D}} \mathbb{E}_{G} \left[ \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}} \left[ L(\boldsymbol{\tau}(\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}), \boldsymbol{\mu}) \right] \right].
\]
Here, the loss is averaged first over the randomness in the observed estimates $\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}$ and then over the uncertainty in the true parameters themselves, with the prior distribution $G$ weighting different regions of the parameter space. Note that this framework conceptualizes $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ as random draws from $G$, in contrast to the framework of the previous section, where $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ was treated as fixed.

A key practical challenge arises from the need to specify a prior distribution $G$, which is rarely straightforward in applied settings. In poverty targeting, for example, policymakers typically have limited reliable information about the distribution of the conditional expectation of household incomes beyond the noisy estimates available. Fully subjective priors may therefore appear arbitrary, difficult to defend, or even politically contentious. This lack of credible prior knowledge makes direct application of the Bayes risk principle problematic. To overcome this obstacle, a widely used and practical alternative is to adopt an EB approach, whereby the prior is estimated directly from the data rather than on the basis of external assumptions.

The EB approach adopts the Bayes risk principle in spirit by seeking to approximate the oracle Bayes rule without assuming knowledge of the true prior distribution. Specifically, the objective is to construct a decision rule that, although based on an estimated prior, achieves an expected loss close to that of the true Bayes-optimal rule corresponding to the unknown prior. In the formulation of nonparametric EB, as developed by \citeA{brown2009nonparametric}, the unknown parameters are treated as realizations from an unknown distribution, and the goal is to design a data-driven estimator whose Bayes risk asymptotically matches that under the oracle Bayes rule. This framework motivates the EB procedure proposed in this paper, where the prior distribution is estimated nonparametrically from the data and then the corresponding Bayes rule applied to the observed data.\footnote{Strictly speaking, the EB rule is not a Bayes rule in Berger’s sense because the uncertainty about G is not integrated out. Proposition \ref{prop:regret} shows, however, that its risk converges to that of the oracle Bayes rule.}

To formalize the empirical Bayes framework, I impose a standard assumption consistent with this perspective. First, I treat the vector of variances $\boldsymbol{\sigma}^2 = (\sigma_1^2, \dots, \sigma_n^2)^\top$ as fixed and known. Conditional on the noise variances, the true conditional expectations of income are modeled as independent and identically distributed draws from a common probabilistic distribution $G$.  This structure enables estimation of the prior from the observed data and justifies application of an EB rule as an approximation of the oracle Bayes rule.

\vspace{0.25cm}
\hypertarget{ASSUME}{} 
\clr{
\label{assump:prior} 
\noindent \assume{2}{Prior} 
Given $\boldsymbol{\sigma} = (\sigma_{1},..., \sigma_{n})^\top$, the conditional expectation of incomes is generated by i.i.d. draws from a common probabilistic distribution $G$:
\[
\mu_{i} \mid \boldsymbol{\sigma} \overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim} G, \quad i = 1, \ldots, n.
\]
}

Thus, in this particular setup, $G$ represents an objective description of the variation in parameters rather than subjective beliefs about their likely values. Assumption 2 implies prior independence: The true conditional expectation of income and the precision of the income estimates must be uncorrelated, i.e., \(\mu_i \perp\!\!\!\perp \sigma_i\) for all \(i\). However, this assumption may be implausible in the current context. For instance, households with observable characteristics associated with higher income, such as tile roofing on their dwellings, tend to be relatively rare in developing countries. For such households, both their incomes ($\mu_i$) and the uncertainty of the respective income estimates ($\sigma_i$) are higher, which creates a positive correlation between the two. For simplicity of analysis, I focus on the case where $\sigma_i$ and $\mu_i$ are independent, and I leave an extension of the CLOSE approach proposed by \citeA{chen2023empirical} to my setup as a case for future research.

\subsection{Oracle Rule}

In this subsection, I characterize the decision rule that minimizes the Bayes risk when the true prior distribution \(G\) is known. This oracle rule provides a benchmark for optimal behavior under uncertainty, serving as a guide for the EB procedure developed later.

Because the loss function is nonnegative and measurable, Fubini’s theorem allows interchanging of the order of expectations. Thus, the Bayes risk conditional on \(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^2\) can be expressed as
\[
r(G, \boldsymbol{\tau} \mid \boldsymbol{\sigma}^2)
= \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}} \mid \boldsymbol{\sigma}^2} \left[ \mathbb{E}_{G \mid \boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}^2} \left[ L(\boldsymbol{\tau}(\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}), \boldsymbol{\mu}) \right] \right],
\]
where the outer expectation is taken with respect to the marginal distribution of \(\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}\) given \(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^2\) and the inner expectation is taken with respect to the posterior distribution of \(\boldsymbol{\mu}\).

Minimizing the Bayes risk amounts to minimizing the posterior expected loss \emph{pointwise} for each realization of the data. To formalize this, fix a realization \(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}\) of \(\boldsymbol{\hat{Y}}\). The posterior risk of an action \(\boldsymbol{t} \in \mathcal{A}\) is defined as
\[
r(G, \boldsymbol{t} \mid \boldsymbol{\hat{y}}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}^2) := \mathbb{E}_{G \mid \boldsymbol{\hat{y}}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}^2} \left[ L(\boldsymbol{t}, \boldsymbol{\mu}) \right].
\]
Thus, for each realization \(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}\), the optimal action satisfies
\[
\boldsymbol{t}^{*}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}) \in \arg\min_{\boldsymbol{t} \in \mathcal{A}} \, r(G, \boldsymbol{t} \mid \boldsymbol{\hat{y}}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}^2).
\]
Assembling these optimal actions across all realizations yields the oracle Bayes rule \(\boldsymbol{\tau}^*\).

To characterize the optimal action for a given realization \(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}\), observe that the posterior risk admits a simple decomposition. Because the prior \(G\) implies that the \(\mu_i\)s are conditionally independent given \(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}\) and the loss is additive across components, the posterior risk is decomposed as
\begin{align*}
r(G, \boldsymbol{t} \mid \boldsymbol{\hat{y}}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}^2)
& = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbb{E}_{G \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2} \left[ (z - \mu_i - t_i)^2 \right] \\
& = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left[ (z - \mathbb{E}_G[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2] - t_i)^2 + \mathrm{Var}_G(\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2) \right].
\end{align*}
Since the variance terms \(\mathrm{Var}_G(\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2)\) do not depend on the actions \(\boldsymbol{t}\), they can be ignored for optimization purposes. Minimizing the posterior risk thus reduces to a constrained least squares problem with the following Lagrangian:
\[
\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{t}, \lambda, \boldsymbol{\xi}) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( z - \mathbb{E}_G[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2] - t_i \right)^2 + \lambda \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} t_i - B \right) - \sum_{i=1}^{n} \xi_i t_i,
\]
where \(\lambda \geq 0\) and \(\xi_i \geq 0\) are the Lagrange multipliers corresponding to the budget and nonnegativity constraints, respectively. The solution to this optimization problem is characterized in the following proposition.

\begin{proposition} 
\proptitle{\hyperlink{proof:oracle_bayes_rule}{Oracle Bayes Rule}}
\label{prop:oracle_bayes_rule} \hypertarget{prop:oracle_bayes_rule}{}
	Under Assumptions 1 and 2, for each data realization $\boldsymbol{\hat{y}} = (\hat{y}_1, \dots, \hat{y}_n)^\top$, the oracle Bayes action is given componentwise by
\[
t_i^{*}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}; \lambda)
= \max\left\{ 0, \, z - \mathbb{E}_G[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2] - \frac{\lambda}{2} \right\},
\qquad i = 1, \dots, n,
\]
where the Lagrange multiplier $\lambda$ is chosen to satisfy the budget constraint:
\[
\lambda^{*} = \inf\left\{ \lambda \geq 0 \;:\; \sum_{i=1}^{n} t_i^{*}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}; \lambda) \leq B \right\}.
\]
The oracle decision rule is the mapping
\[
\boldsymbol{\tau}^{*} : \boldsymbol{\hat{y}} \longmapsto \left( t_1^{*}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}; \lambda^{*}), \dots, t_n^{*}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}; \lambda^{*}) \right)^\top,
\]
which uniquely minimizes the Bayes risk $r(G, \boldsymbol{\tau} \mid \boldsymbol{\sigma}^2)$ over the class of decision rules $\mathcal{D}$.
\end{proposition}

The Bayesian planner’s behavior fundamentally differs from planner behavior in the perfect information case (Proposition~\ref{prop:full_info}). Under perfect information, the planner simply ranks households by their true incomes and transfers resources to the poorest until the budget is exhausted. When only noisy income estimates are available, however, it is no longer obvious which households should be prioritized or how much they should receive. A household with a low estimated income either could be truly poor or could merely appear poor due to random variation in the estimation process, and the planner must account for this uncertainty when making allocation decisions.

The oracle Bayes rule addresses this challenge by allocating transfers based on each household’s posterior expected poverty gap, adjusted downward by the shadow price of budgetary resources. Households with low posterior expected incomes are prioritized, while those with sufficiently high posterior means receive no transfers. This approach ensures that resources are directed toward households where the expected marginal benefit, measured in terms of posterior risk reduction, is greatest.

A key feature of the Bayesian allocation rule is its use of shrinkage, a correction mechanism formalized by Tweedie’s formula. In this setting, Tweedie’s formula states that the posterior mean can be expressed as
\[
\mathbb{E}[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2] = \hat{y}_i + \sigma_i^2 \frac{\partial}{\partial \hat{y}_i} \log f(\hat{y}_i),
\]
where \(f(\hat{y}_i)\) denotes the marginal density of \(\hat{y}_i\) under the prior. The posterior mean corrects the raw estimate \(\hat{y}_i\) by a term that depends on the local shape of the data distribution and the noise level \(\sigma_i^2\), effectively shrinking extreme or highly uncertain observations toward more plausible values.

This shrinkage mechanism plays a crucial role in minimizing costly misallocations. Rather than taking noisy estimates at face value, the Bayesian planner adjusts the observed data for uncertainty. As a result, transfers to households with highly uncertain or extreme estimates are moderated, reducing the risk of large, costly errors even if this means many smaller mistakes end up being accepted.

More broadly, the Bayesian rule explicitly balances the trade-off between bias and variance inherent in the risk function. The plug-in rule minimizes bias conditional on the observed data but ignores variance, leading to overly aggressive decision-making prone to costly errors. In contrast, the Bayesian rule tempers bias reduction by controlling variance, yielding decisions that are more stable and less sensitive to estimation noise \cite{brown1978portfolio}.


\subsection{Empirical Bayes Procedure}

In practice, the oracle Bayes rule \(\boldsymbol{\tau}^{*}\) cannot be implemented because the true prior distribution \(G\) is unknown. The EB approach addresses this challenge by approximating the oracle rule: \(G\) is estimated directly from the observed data, and this estimate is used to construct a feasible decision rule. In this way, nonparametric EB procedures aim at the smaller benchmark risk, which means they are greedier than traditional methods such as linear shrinkage or thresholding rules \cite{JiangZhang2009}. 

Throughout this paper, admissibility has been a central concern: Good decision rules should not be uniformly dominated by others. Although EB estimators are not necessarily admissible, they approximate the performance of the oracle Bayes procedure, which is itself likely to be admissible. Thus, EB rules inherit much of the oracle’s desirable structure, particularly in large samples. Moreover, as demonstrated by the simulation results in Section~\ref{sec:sim_emp}, EB rules consistently outperform both the plug-in rule and the James--Stein-type estimator across a wide range of realistic settings, achieving substantial loss reductions, even though they are not necessarily admissible.

\subsubsection{Prior Estimation \label{sec:npmle}}

Following the recent literature, and rather than assuming a specific parametric form for the latent distribution \(G\), I estimate the unknown prior nonparametrically using the nonparametric maximum likelihood estimator (NPMLE) of \citeA{kiefer1956consistency}. An NPMLE is any \( \hat{G} \in \mathcal{G} \), the set of all probability distributions, that maximizes the marginal log-likelihood of the observations \( (\hat{y}_{i})_{i=1}^{n} \). Specifically, \(\hat{G}\) solves
\begin{align*}
	\hat{G} \in \arg\max_{G \in \mathcal{G}} \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \log \int \varphi\left( \frac{\hat{y}_{i} - \mu}{\sigma_{i}} \right) \frac{1}{\sigma_{i}} \, dG(\mu),
\end{align*}
where \(\varphi(\cdot)\) denotes the probability density function of a standard normal random variable. In other words, the NPMLE searches over all possible distributions \(G\) to find the one that best explains the observed, noisy income estimates, without imposing restrictive parametric assumptions \cite{soloff2021multivariate}. 

The resulting optimization problem is infinite-dimensional but convex, with a strictly concave objective and linear constraints. Thanks to this structure, modern interior-point algorithms can efficiently compute an approximate solution \cite{koenker2014convex}. In practice, the likelihood is maximized over a discretized grid of candidate support points. As shown by \citeA{dicker2016high}, a good approximation can be achieved provided that the grid includes at least \(\sqrt{n}\) support points.

The NPMLE exhibits several properties that make it particularly attractive for shrinkage estimation. First, the estimated prior \(\hat{G}\) is discrete, supported on at most \(n\) points \cite{koenker2017rebayes}, which facilitates efficient computation of posterior quantities such as the posterior mean. Second, \(\hat{G}\) is a consistent estimator of the true prior \(G\) under mild regularity conditions \cite{kiefer1956consistency}. Third, \citeA{polyanskiy2020self} show that with high probability, the number of support points selected by the optimization grows only logarithmically with the sample size, \(\mathcal{O}(\log n)\), despite allowing for arbitrary complexity. This ``self-regularization" property eliminates the need for external tuning parameters, allowing the NPMLE to adapt flexibly to the underlying distribution while controlling model complexity automatically.

\subsubsection{Feasible Rule}

Having estimated the prior distribution nonparametrically using the NPMLE \( \hat{G} \), I next construct a feasible decision rule that approximates the oracle Bayes rule. The empirical Bayes (EB) procedure replaces the unknown prior \( G \) with the estimate \( \hat{G} \) and sets transfers accordingly.

For each realization of the data \( \boldsymbol{\hat{y}} = (\hat{y}_1, \dots, \hat{y}_n)^\top \), define the empirical Bayes decision rule as
\begin{align*}
\hat{t}_i^{\text{EB}}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}; \lambda)
&= \max\left\{ 0, \, z - \mathbb{E}_{\hat{G}}[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2] - \frac{\lambda}{2} \right\},
\qquad i = 1, \dots, n,
\end{align*}
with the Lagrange multiplier \( \hat{\lambda}^{*} \) solving the budget constraint:
\begin{align*}
\hat{\lambda}^{*} = \inf\left\{ \lambda \geq 0 \;:\; \sum_{i=1}^{n} \hat{t}_i^{\text{EB}}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}; \lambda) \leq B \right\}.
\end{align*}
The final EB allocation is then given by
\begin{align*}
\boldsymbol{\hat{\tau}}^{\text{EB}}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}) 
= \left( \hat{t}_1^{\text{EB}}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}; \hat{\lambda}^{*}), \dots, \hat{t}_n^{\text{EB}}(\boldsymbol{\hat{y}}; \hat{\lambda}^{*}) \right)^\top.
\end{align*}

This rule mirrors the structure of the oracle Bayes rule but substitutes the unknown posterior mean \( \mathbb{E}_G[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2] \) with its empirical counterpart under \( \hat{G} \). The term \( \mathbb{E}_{\hat{G}}[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i^2] \) shrinks each noisy estimate toward more plausible values inferred from the observed data, correcting for estimation uncertainty in a fully data-driven way. As the sample size increases, \( \hat{G} \) consistently approximates \( G \), so the EB decision rule \( \boldsymbol{\hat{\tau}}^{\text{EB}} \) converges to the oracle rule \( \boldsymbol{\tau}^{*} \).

To further illustrate the construction and advantages of the feasible rule, the next two remarks provide concrete examples and conceptual comparisons. First, the following example shows how the EB rule operates in a concrete parametric setting:

\begin{titledRemark}{Truncated Normal} \label{remark:trunc_norm}  Assume homoskedastic noise $\sigma_i = 1$ for all $i = 1,\dots,n$, and let the prior $G$ for the conditional expectation $\mu_i$ be a left-truncated normal distribution with mean $\alpha$ and variance $\gamma^2$, supported on $[0, \infty)$. Its density is
\begin{align*}
    g(\mu) = \frac{1}{\gamma} \cdot \frac{\varphi\left( \frac{\mu - \alpha}{\gamma} \right)}{1 - \Phi\left( -\frac{\alpha}{\gamma} \right)},
\end{align*}
where $\varphi$ and $\Phi$ denote the standard normal probability density and cumulative distribution functions, respectively.

Ignoring the truncation, standard normal--normal conjugacy yields the posterior mean and variance:
\begin{align*}
    \delta(\hat{y}_i) &= \left( \frac{\gamma^2}{\gamma^2 + 1} \right) \hat{y}_i + \left( \frac{1}{\gamma^2 + 1} \right) \alpha 
    = \alpha + \frac{\gamma^2}{\gamma^2 + 1}(\hat{y}_i - \alpha), \\
    \nu^2 &= \frac{\gamma^2}{\gamma^2 + 1}.
\end{align*}
Since the prior is truncated, the posterior distribution $\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i$ is also a left-truncated normal. Its posterior mean is
\begin{align*}
    m(\hat{y}_i) = \delta(\hat{y}_i) + \nu \cdot \kappa\left( \frac{-\delta(\hat{y}_i)}{\nu} \right), \quad \text{where } \kappa(x) = \frac{\varphi(x)}{1 - \Phi(x)}.
\end{align*}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:oracle_bayes_rule}, the Bayes-optimal transfer is given by
\begin{align}
\label{eq:truncnorm_bayes_rule}
    \tau_i^* = \max \left\{ 0, z - m(\hat{y}_i) - \frac{\lambda^*}{2} \right\},
\end{align}
where $\lambda^*$ is the Lagrange multiplier ensuring feasibility.

The EB approach approximates \eqref{eq:truncnorm_bayes_rule} by estimating the prior parameters using maximum likelihood or the method of moments:
\begin{align*}
    \hat{\alpha} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \hat{y}_i, \quad 
    \hat{\gamma}^2 = \max\left\{ 0, \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (\hat{y}_i - \hat{\alpha})^2 - 1 \right\}, \quad
    \hat{\nu}^2 = \frac{\hat{\gamma}^2}{\hat{\gamma}^2 + 1}.
\end{align*}
Define the empirical shrinkage term and posterior mean:
\begin{align*}
    \hat{\delta}(\hat{y}_i) &= \hat{\alpha} + \frac{\hat{\gamma}^2}{\hat{\gamma}^2 + 1} (\hat{y}_i - \hat{\alpha}), \\
    \hat{m}(\hat{y}_i) &= \hat{\delta}(\hat{y}_i) + \hat{\nu} \cdot \kappa\left( \frac{-\hat{\delta}(\hat{y}_i)}{\hat{\nu}} \right),
\end{align*}
and compute the empirical Bayes rule:
\begin{align*}
    \hat{\tau}_i^* = \max \left\{ 0, z - \hat{m}(\hat{y}_i) - \frac{\hat{\lambda}^*}{2} \right\}.
\end{align*}

Thus, the optimal transfer is a decreasing function of the posterior mean, which combines a linear shrinkage toward the prior mean $\alpha$ and an adjustment term due to the truncation of $G$. Assuming a Gaussian (nontruncated) prior or using a James--Stein-type rule leads to the elegant closed-form expression $\hat{\delta}(\hat{y}_i)$, which is analytically convenient but restrictive. Notably, such approaches would omit the truncation adjustment, which can seriously affect the performance of the rule.
\end{titledRemark}

Second, the next remark clarifies the conceptual differences between the EB and standard machine learning approaches.

\begin{titledRemark}{Nonparametric EB vs. Machine Learning} \label{remark:eb_vs_ml}  This paper adopts a nonparametric EB (NPEB) approach to shrink noisy income estimates prior to the allocation of transfers. While shrinkage also underlies standard regularization techniques in machine learning, such as ridge and lasso regression, these approaches rely on parametric priors, with normal in the case of ridge and Laplace in the case of lasso, which impose a rigid structure on the shrinkage pattern \cite{walters2024empirical}. In contrast, NPEB estimates the prior distribution flexibly from the data, allowing the degree and shape of shrinkage to be determined by the data. This data-adaptive property is especially useful in environments, such as poverty targeting, where the true distribution of latent parameters is unknown and potentially far from the assumptions embedded in parametric shrinkage.

As shown in \citeA{abadie2019choosing}, NPEB dominates parametric alternatives when the number of parameters is large, there is no sparsity, and the true parameter distribution is highly dispersed. These are precisely the conditions that characterize the poverty targeting problem. In poverty targeting, income is a continuous variable that exhibits wide dispersion across households, with few, if any, observations concentrated near zero. Moreover, the planner typically works with large administrative datasets, ensuring a high-dimensional setting where the advantages of NPEB become most pronounced. Thus, both the theoretical properties and the simulation evidence from Abadie and Kasy support the use of NPEB methods in this context.

Other machine learning methods such as regression trees, random forests, and XGBoost can improve predictive accuracy but offer little guidance on how to account for estimation uncertainty in subsequent allocation decisions. Similarly to the plug-in rule, they treat point estimates as if they were known with certainty, without adjusting for the noise inherent in the income predictions. As a result, they suffer from the same fundamental limitation: an inability to optimally balance the trade-off between bias and variance in allocations of scarce resources under uncertainty. In contrast, the EB approach explicitly incorporates this uncertainty into the decision rule, producing better targeting outcomes.

\end{titledRemark}

\subsection{Statistical Properties}

In this subsection, I discuss the regret rates associated with the nonsmooth transformation of the posterior mean used to compute the feasible rule.

\subsubsection{Assumptions}

Following \citeA{jiang2020general}, I allow \( \hat{G} \) to be an approximate solution to the NPMLE problem.

\vspace{0.25cm}
\hypertarget{ASSUME}{} \clr{
\label{assump:approx} \hypertarget{assump:approx}{}
\noindent \assume{3}{Approximate Solution} 
Let \(f_G(\hat{y}_i) = \int \varphi\left( \frac{\hat{y}_i - \mu}{\sigma_i} \right) \frac{1}{\sigma_i} \, dG(\mu)\) denote the marginal density of \(\hat{y}_i\) induced by a distribution \(G\) over \(\mathbb{R}_{+}\), where \(\varphi(\cdot)\) is the standard normal probability density function.  
The NPMLE \(\hat{G}\) satisfies
\begin{align*}
	\prod_{i=1}^{n} f_{\hat{G}} (\hat{y}_i) \geq q_n \sup_{G \in \mathcal{G}}  \prod_{i=1}^{n} f_{G} (\hat{y}_i),
\end{align*}
where \(\mathcal{G}\) is the family of all distribution functions on \(\mathbb{R}_{+}\) and \(q_n = (e \sqrt{2\pi}/n^2)\land 1\).
}
\vspace{0.25cm}



Assumption 3 allows the EB estimator $\hat{G}$ to approximately maximize the marginal likelihood instead of requiring exact maximization. %%Editor: Please check that I have preserved the intended meaning here.
Specifically, it demands that the likelihood achieved by $\hat{G}$ be within a factor $q_n$ of the global maximum likelihood across all possible priors, where $q_n$ shrinks slowly at the rate $1/n^2$ as the sample size grows. This condition serves two purposes. First, it responds to the computational challenges of solving an infinite-dimensional optimization problem exactly, especially as $n$ becomes large. Second, it guarantees that the approximation error is sufficiently small to preserve the key statistical properties of $\hat{G}$. In particular, by allowing a small slack in optimization, the assumption enables practical computation while ensuring that $\hat{G}$ remains ``good enough" for the EB procedure to attain near-optimal regret rates. 

I also impose a moment condition on \( G \). 

\vspace{0.25cm}
\hypertarget{ASSUME}{} \clr{
\label{assump:moment} \hypertarget{assump:moment}{}
\noindent \assume{4}{Moment Condition} 
Let \( \mathcal{M}_{p}(G) = \left\{ \int |y|^{p} \, dG(y) \right\}^{1/p} \) denote the \( p \)-th absolute moment of the prior distribution \( G \).  
The prior distribution $G$ satisfies $\mathcal{M}_{p}(G) = \mathcal{O}(1)$ for some $p \geq 2$.
}
\vspace{0.25cm}

Assumption 4 imposes a mild regularity condition on the true prior \( G \), requiring that its \( p \)-th absolute moment remain uniformly bounded as the sample size increases. This assumption rules out extremely heavy-tailed priors, such as the Cauchy distribution or Pareto distributions with shape parameters less than \( p \), since these would lead to infinite moments. In contrast to \citeA{jiang2020general}, who assumes only the existence of the first absolute moment (i.e., $p \geq 1$), I impose a slightly stronger condition by requiring $p \geq 2$, which ensures that the prior $G$ has finite variance. 

\subsubsection{Bayes Regret}

In evaluating the performance of EB procedures, it is natural to compare them to the oracle Bayes rule, which has access to the true prior distribution \( G \). To do so, I adopt the notion of \textit{Bayes regret}, which quantifies the additional expected loss incurred by using the EB rule \(\hat{\tau}^{*}\) instead of the oracle Bayes rule \(\tau^{*}\). This regret reflects the cost of estimating the prior rather than knowing it.

To formalize this comparison, I introduce the following notation. Let \( P_{\mathcal{A}}(\cdot) \) denote the euclidean projection onto the action space \( \mathcal{A} \). Define the oracle posterior mean as $m_i^* = \mathbb{E}_G[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i]$ and the EB posterior mean as $\hat{m}_i = \mathbb{E}_{\hat{G}}[\mu_i \mid \hat{y}_i, \sigma_i]$. The corresponding transfer rules are given by:
\[
\tau_i^* = P_{\mathcal{A}}(\boldsymbol{1}z - \mathbf{m}^*)_i, \quad \hat{\tau}_i = P_{\mathcal{A}}(\boldsymbol{1}z - \boldsymbol{\hat{m}})_i.
\]
Following \citeA{soloff2021multivariate} and \citeA{chen2023empirical}, I define the Bayes regret as:
\[
\text{Bayes Regret}(\boldsymbol{\hat{\tau}}, \boldsymbol{\tau}^*) = \mathbb{E}\left[ L(\boldsymbol{\hat{\tau}}, \boldsymbol{\mu}) \right] - \mathbb{E}\left[ L(\boldsymbol{\tau}^*, \boldsymbol{\mu}) \right],
\]
where \( L(\cdot, \cdot) \) is the loss function and the expectation is taken over both the prior draw \( \boldsymbol{\mu} \sim G \) and the observed data \( \hat{\boldsymbol{Y}} \mid \boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\sigma} \), conditional on the known noise levels \( \boldsymbol{\sigma} \).

Intuitively, the Bayes regret isolates the performance gap due solely to the use of an estimated prior. Clearly, a Bayes regret value close to zero indicates that the EB rule successfully mimics the oracle rule, yielding near-optimal performance even without full knowledge of \( G \).

Using the framework outlined above, the following proposition establishes a finite-sample upper bound on the regret incurred by the feasible EB rule. The result builds on the convergence rates derived by \citeA{jiang2020general} for NPMLEs under finite moment conditions.

\begin{proposition}
\proptitle{\hyperlink{proof:regret}{Regret Rates}}
\label{prop:regret} \hypertarget{prop:regret}{}
Let Assumptions 1--4 hold and fix $p>0$. Then there exist universal constants $M_0, C_0>0$ (independent of $n$) such that for all $n$ satisfying $\log n >1/p$,
\begin{align*}
	\text{Bayes Regret } (\boldsymbol{\hat{\tau}}, \boldsymbol{\tau^{*}})   \leq C_0 C_n + M_0 C_n n^{-p/(2+2p)} (\log n)^{(7p+8)/(4+4p)} \text{.}
\end{align*}
Because $C_0 = \mathcal{O}(1)$ and $C_n = \mathcal{O}(n^{-1/2})$, the inequality implies
\begin{align*}
	\text{Bayes Regret } (\boldsymbol{\hat{\tau}}, \boldsymbol{\tau^{*}})  = \mathcal{O}(n^{-p/(2+2p)} (\log n)^{(7p+8)/(4+4p)} ) +  \mathcal{O}(n^{-1/2})  \text{.}
\end{align*}
Hence the Bayes regret of the empirical‑Bayes projection estimator converges to zero, and its dominant asymptotic rate is $n^{-p/(2+2p)} (\log n)^{(7p+8)/(4+4p)}$.
\end{proposition}

The main idea behind the proof is that projecting a vector onto the simplex cannot increase the estimation error. In other words, this projection step is stable: It preserves or reduces differences between the estimated and true quantities. This property ensures that any error from estimating the underlying means is not made worse by enforcing feasibility. As a result, the analysis can focus entirely on bounding the error in the initial plug-in estimates, without worrying about additional complications introduced by the projection. This allows the regret of the empirical-Bayes targeting rule to inherit the same convergence rates as the unconstrained posterior mean estimator.

Proposition~\ref{prop:regret} formalizes this result. For any fixed value of the tail parameter $p > 0$, the Bayes regret of the empirical-Bayes projection rule converges to zero and admits a non-asymptotic bound with two distinct components: one decaying at the familiar $n^{-1/2}$ parametric rate, and a second term that reflects the difficulty of learning the prior distribution. The latter dominates when the prior is heavy-tailed and improves with smoother priors. In particular, when the prior has finite second moments (i.e., $p = 2$), the regret rate is approximately $n^{-1/3}$ up to logarithmic corrections. As the prior becomes smoother, meaning it has lighter tails, the rate accelerates and approaches the ideal \( n^{-1/2} \) as \( p \) tends to infinity.

This result highlights two key insights. First, even without knowing the true prior, the empirical-Bayes regret  decays at a known rate, determined by how difficult the prior is to learn. Second, the projection step, which ensures that the resulting targeting vector is feasible (e.g., respects the budget and nonnegativity constraints), does not degrade performance. Its non-expansiveness guarantees that estimation error is not amplified in the process.

In short, Proposition~\ref{prop:regret} shows that the empirical-Bayes projection rule delivers theoretically sound performance guarantees. It adapts to the unknown prior distribution, enforces feasibility, and matches the convergence rates for nonparametric posterior mean estimation.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Simulation Evidence \label{sec:sim_emp}}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

I conduct two simulation studies to develop a richer quantitative understanding of the performance of different decision rules. First, I design a data-generating process calibrated to consumption data from nine African countries: Burkina Faso, Ethiopia, Ghana, Malawi, Mali, Niger, Nigeria, Tanzania, and Uganda, as collected by \citeA{brown2018poor}. This setup represents an \textit{ideal} scenario in which income estimates are assumed to be normally distributed around the conditional expectation, with variances known and independent of the prior distribution. Second, to assess the EB method in a more realistic context, I adapt the simulation framework of \citeA{corral2025poverty}, treating the estimated incomes as given.

\subsection{African Countries \label{sec:sim_emp_africa}}

\subsubsection{Setup}
I consider a setting where the policymaker observes noisy estimates of the conditional expectation of true consumption for each household \(i\) in country \(c\), denoted by \(\hat{Y}_{ic}\), which are generated as follows:
\[
\hat{Y}_{ic} \sim \mathcal{N} \left( \mathbb{E}[y_{ic} \mid W_{ic}], \sigma_{ic}^{2} \right),
\]
where \(\mathbb{E}[y_{ic} \mid W_{ic}]\) is the conditional expectation of consumption based on observable characteristics \(W_{ic}\).\footnote{I estimate \(\mathbb{E}[y_{ic} \mid W_{ic}]\) using extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost) separately for each country, based on a rich set of household-level predictors including demographic characteristics, housing conditions, and asset ownership.} The standard deviation \( \sigma_{ic} \) is drawn uniformly from the interval \( [\sigma_c/2, 1.5\sigma_c] \), where \( \sigma_c \) is calibrated to achieve a target signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), defined as the ratio of the variance of true consumption to the average variance of the measurement noise, of \( 1 \), \( 0.75 \), \( 0.5 \), \( 0.25 \), or \( 0.1 \). For each combination of country and SNR level, \(500\) simulation replications are conducted.

For each country, the poverty line \(z_c\) is set to the median consumption level, \(z_c = \text{Med}(y_{ic})\), and the antipoverty budget \(B_c\) is set to \(10\%\) of the amount needed to eliminate poverty under full information:\footnote{The budget is set to \(10\%\) to ensure that the James--Stein estimator performs meaningful shrinkage relative to the plug-in rule; with larger budgets, the James--Stein estimator often defaults to the plug-in rule because shrinkage becomes negligible. For the other methods, the results are virtually unchanged when the budget share is varied.}
\[
B_c =  \sum_{i=1}^{n_c} \max\{z_c - y_{ic}, 0\} / 10,
\]
where \(n_c\) denotes the number of households in country \(c\).

I compare the performance of five approaches to targeting the budget \(B_c\): (i) the plug-in approach, which allocates transfers based directly on the noisy estimates \(\hat{Y}_{ic}\); (ii) the James--Stein-type shrinkage correction applied to the plug-in rule;\footnote{The James--Stein correction uses \(\sigma_c\), assuming homoskedastic noise across households within each country.} (iii) the oracle Bayes rule, where the planner knows the distribution of the conditional expectation of income for each country \(G_c\); (iv) the feasible nonparametric EB rule using an estimated \(\hat{G}_c\) via nonparametric maximum likelihood; and (v) the no-data rule (universal basic income) that assigns \(B_c/n_c\) to every household. 

I measure performance using the loss ratio, defined as
\begin{align}
\label{eq:performance}
	\text{Loss Ratio} (\boldsymbol{\tau}) = \frac{L(\boldsymbol{\tau}, \boldsymbol{y}) - L(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{y})}{L(\boldsymbol{\tau^{\star}}, \boldsymbol{y}) - L(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{y})} \text{ ,}
\end{align}
where \(L(\boldsymbol{\tau}, \boldsymbol{y})\) denotes the poverty loss associated with transfer schedule \(\boldsymbol{\tau}\) given true realized incomes \(\boldsymbol{y}\) and \(L(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{y})\) is the loss under no transfers.\footnote{All welfare evaluations are conducted with respect to realized incomes \(\boldsymbol{y}\), not conditional expectations \(\boldsymbol{\mu} = \mathbb{E}[y \mid W]\).} The loss ratio is a random variable that captures the proportion of the maximum achievable loss reduction attained by the estimated transfer schedule \(\boldsymbol{\tau}\).

A loss ratio of 1 indicates that decision rule \(\boldsymbol{\tau}\) achieves the same reduction in poverty as the full-information optimum \(\boldsymbol{\tau^{\star}}\). A value between 0 and 1 implies that \(\boldsymbol{\tau}\) improves upon the no-transfer baseline but falls short of achieving the full potential of \(\boldsymbol{\tau^{\star}}\). A value of 0 indicates that \(\boldsymbol{\tau}\) yields no improvement relative to no transfer, while negative values suggest that \(\boldsymbol{\tau}\) increases the loss relative to provision of no transfer at all.

\subsubsection{Results}
Figure~\ref{fig:sim_results1} presents the distribution of the loss ratio across the nine African countries for 200 replications under an SNR of \(0.25\). The UBI method is represented as a horizontal dashed line because its loss ratio is constant across replications. 

 \begin{figure}[h!]
    \begin{center}
    \caption{Distributions of Loss Ratio}
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{./Figs/Sims_Plot1.png}
    \label{fig:sim_results1}
    \end{center}
    \footnotesize{{\bf Notes}: Distribution of performance (defined by \eqref{eq:performance}) under a signal-to-noise ratio of 0.25 for the uniform, plug-in, James--Stein, empirical Bayes, and oracle rules under the different income distributions. The loss ratio is measured relative to the theoretical optimal, with a higher value indicating performance closer to that of the optimal transfer schedule for reducing poverty. Each boxplot represents the distribution of performance over 200 replications for each rule under the different income distributions. See the text for details.}
\end{figure}

Several patterns emerge consistently across countries. First, EB and oracle Bayes clearly outperform the other approaches, achieving significantly higher loss ratios. Second, the EB method performs very close to the oracle benchmark, indicating that the Bayes regret—the gap between the feasible EB method and the infeasible oracle benchmark—is small in this setting. 

Third, the plug-in method performs very poorly, yielding negative loss ratios across all countries. This suggests that naively targeting based on noisy consumption estimates can be counterproductive in terms of poverty loss minimization. Fourth, the James--Stein correction improves upon the plug-in method in every country, but the improvement is modest: While James--Stein shifts the distribution of the loss ratio upward, it still falls far short of the performance achieved by the EB and oracle methods. Overall, the results demonstrate the substantial gains available from application of empirical Bayes corrections in targeted transfer programs, relative to simpler heuristic approaches.


Figure~\ref{fig:sim_results2} illustrates the mean loss ratio across simulations as a function of the SNR. The results show again that the EB and oracle methods consistently outperform the other approaches across all SNR levels. Both methods achieve very similar performance, with the EB method closely approximating the oracle benchmark even as the SNR decreases.

