To clarify the differences between targeting approaches, Table \ref{tab: mechanisms} summarizes the average transfers (conditional on their being positive), average percentage of the population reached, average inclusion error, and average exclusion error across replications for each country.

The plug-in rule stands out for its aggressive approach, characterized by larger average transfers (e.g., 11.58 in Burkina Faso) but very small percentage of population reached (e.g., only 2\% in Burkina Faso). This method leads to a high exclusion error, of approximately 96--97\% across countries, meaning a significant portion of the poor population receives no transfers. While it achieves slightly lower inclusion errors (e.g., 30\% in Burkina Faso), this comes at the cost of resources being inefficiently concentrated on very few households, making mistakes very costly.

In contrast, the oracle and EB methods adopt a much more conservative approach. The transfers are smaller on average (e.g., 1.30 in Burkina Faso) and reach a larger percentage of the population (e.g., 22\% in Burkina Faso). This broader allocation reduces the exclusion error significantly—from approximately 96\% under the plug-in rule to approximately 70--72\% under EB and oracle. The inclusion error under these methods remains comparable to that of the plug-in rule (e.g., 25–32\% across countries for EB and oracle in most cases). This shows how both the EB and oracle Bayes approaches spread resources more evenly, avoiding costly large mistakes.

The UBI approach provides uniform transfers, resulting in the smallest average transfer (e.g., 0.28 in Burkina Faso) while reaching 100\% of the population. This approach eliminates exclusion errors entirely but leads to a very high inclusion error, diverting resources from where they are needed most.

