Overall, both the oracle and EB rules achieve superior performance across all settings by avoiding extreme allocations and spreading transfers more broadly across the income distribution. This smoothing effect allows them to reduce exclusion errors significantly, reaching a larger share of the truly poor, while keeping inclusion errors on par with the rate under the plug-in rule.

\subsection{Mexico}

\subsubsection{Setup}
This section builds directly on the simulation framework proposed by \citeA{corral2025poverty}, who use the 2015 Mexican Inter-Censal Survey to construct a high-quality pseudo-census and evaluate poverty mapping methods under repeated sampling. Following their approach, I use the same pseudo-census dataset, comprising approximately 3.9 million households, and adopt their sampling procedure to generate 500 survey samples. Each sample is drawn using a two-stage clustered design, with primary sampling units (PSUs) selected with probability proportional to size and 10 households drawn per selected PSU.

While I replicate the underlying data and sampling structure of \citeA{corral2025poverty}, my focus diverges from theirs. Rather than predicting poverty status as a binary indicator, I focus on predicting \textit{true household income}, treating income as a continuous outcome. For each of the 500 samples, I estimate a predictive model where the dependent variable is the observed income in the census. The predictors include a rich set of household-, PSU-, and municipality-level covariates. I implement two predictive models: an OLS regression and an XGBoost model trained via Bayesian hyperparameter optimization. The OLS model is estimated with weighted least squares with sampling weights from the survey, and I compute heteroskedasticity-consistent (HC1) standard errors for all coefficients. These variances are used to construct standard errors for out-of-sample predictions. For the XGBoost model, I use the same set of covariates and allow for hyperparameter tuning.

After training each model on the 500 samples, I generate income predictions for all households in the full census. For the OLS model, I assume the prediction errors are approximately unbiased and normally distributed, which permits the use of an EB procedure to adjust the estimates via posterior means. In contrast, XGBoost predictions are treated as fixed point estimates without any distributional assumptions and thus are not subjected to posterior correction.

I then implement three targeting strategies based on these estimates. The first is a plug-in rule using the raw OLS predictions. The second applies the EB procedure to the OLS estimates and allocates transfers based on the resulting posterior means. The third uses the XGBoost predictions directly as the basis for the transfer decisions. All strategies are evaluated within the poverty alleviation framework of \citeA{corral2025poverty}, in which the poverty line is defined as the 25th percentile of the true income distribution in the census and the total transfer budget is given by the total poverty gap.


\subsubsection{Results}

To evaluate targeting performance, I compute the loss ratio defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:performance}, with higher values (closer to one) reflective of better targeting outcomes. Figure~\ref{fig:boxplot_lossratio} visualizes the distribution of the loss ratio across the 500 simulated samples for each method: plug-in OLS, EB OLS, and XGBoost. 

  \begin{figure}[h!]
    \begin{center}
    \caption{Loss Ratio Comparison Across Methods}
   \includegraphics[width=0.875\textwidth]{./Figs/performance_comparison_plot.png}
    \label{fig:boxplot_lossratio}
    \end{center}
    \footnotesize{{\bf Notes}: Performance (defined by \eqref{eq:performance}) for the plug-in, empirical Bayes, and XGBoost rules across 500 samples. The loss ratio is measured relative to the theoretical optimal, with a higher value indicating performance closer to that of the optimal transfer schedule for reducing poverty. Diamonds indicate means. See the text for details.}
\end{figure}

All three methods perform poorly relative to the full-information benchmark, underscoring the challenge of targeting transfers under realistic information constraints. Nonetheless, the EB approach consistently achieves the best outcomes among the three. On average, EB attains a loss ratio of $-0.528$, outperforming both plug-in OLS ($-0.558$) and XGBoost ($-0.553$). This corresponds to a nearly 2\% reduction in the remaining gap to optimality with respect to the plug-in rule.

Importantly, the EB method also demonstrates greater performance in worst-case scenarios. Its minimum loss ratio is $-0.629$, substantially higher than that of plug-in OLS ($-0.721$) and XGBoost ($-0.782$). In terms of dispersion, EB yields the most stable performance, with a standard deviation of $0.0225$, compared to $0.0381$ for plug-in OLS and $0.0402$ for XGBoost.

Pairwise comparisons further confirm these patterns: EB outperforms plug-in OLS in 499 out of the 500 samples and beats XGBoost in 74.2\% of the cases. These results highlight that, even though no method performs well in absolute terms, applying EB shrinkage to noisy linear predictions consistently yields modest yet reliable improvements over both classical and machine learning--based alternatives.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Conclusion \label{sec:conclu}}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The application here of shrinkage and EB methods for allocation of antipoverty budgets has demonstrated the considerable promise of these approaches for addressing the challenges posed by noisy income measurements. My analysis establishes several key theoretical and practical advances in this domain. First, I prove the inadmissibility of traditional plug-in approaches relative to James--Stein-type estimators, providing a theoretical foundation for improved allocation methods. Furthermore, I develop a nonsmooth empirical Bayes rule that achieves the same Bayes regret bound as the posterior mean. Simulation results confirm these theoretical insights, showing that the EB approach significantly outperforms conventional plug-in estimators across various scenarios.

These methodological advances yield meaningful policy implications. The framework provides policymakers with a straightforward yet effective method for targeting subsidies to the poorest households, addressing a practical and potentially high-impact policy issue. This enhancement in targeting efficiency not only allows more judicious use of limited antipoverty resources but also potentially increases the overall impact of poverty alleviation programs.

The broader implications of this work extend beyond poverty alleviation. The setup and methods presented here can be readily adapted to other policy domains where decision-makers must contend with noisy measurements, such as in optimizing healthcare interventions with imperfect patient data, targeting educational resources based on school or teacher value added, or allocating resources for crime prevention based on noisy crime statistics. As policymakers increasingly face the challenge of making data-driven decisions with imperfect information, the EB approach offers a theoretically grounded way to improve policy targeting and resource allocation.

