\section{Appendix: Geometric and Analytical Properties of the plug-in Estimator}
\label{app:weakdiff}

\subsection{Geometric Decomposition of the Projection Map}

The plug-in estimator \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\) is defined as the projection of \(X\) onto the convex polyhedron \(\mathcal{A} = \{ \tau \in \mathbb{R}_+^n : \sum_{i=1}^n \tau_i \leq B \}\). This set is a truncated \(n\)-dimensional simplex, and the projection map inherits a rich geometric structure from this constraint set.

We begin by decomposing \(\mathcal{A}\) into its relative interiors. Let \(\mathcal{A}_0\) denote the interior of the simplex, and let \(\mathcal{A}_j\), for \(j = 1, \ldots, m\), denote the relative interiors of the proper faces of \(\mathcal{A}\). \footnote{A full \(n\)-dimensional simplex has \(2^{n+1} - 1\) nonempty faces, including the full-dimensional interior and all lower-dimensional faces. Since we label \(\mathcal{A}_0\) as the interior, we index the remaining faces by \(j = 1, \ldots, m = 2^{n+1} - 2\).} For each face \(\mathcal{A}_j\), define the region \(D_j = (\tau^{\text{plug}})^{-1}(\mathcal{A}_j)\), which is the pre-image of \(\mathcal{A}_j\) under the projection map. That is, \(D_j\) consists of all points in \(\mathbb{R}^n\) whose projection lands in the relative interior of face \(\mathcal{A}_j\). These sets \(D_j\) form a partition of \(\mathbb{R}^n\):
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{R}^n = \bigcup_{j = 0}^{2^{n+1}-2} D_j.
\end{align*}

Within each region \(D_j\), the plug-in estimator behaves as a linear projection onto the affine span of face \(\mathcal{A}_j\). That is,
\begin{align*}
    \tau^{\text{plug}}(X) = \sum_{j=0}^{2^{n+1}-2} \mathbbm{1}\{X \in D_j\} \cdot \tau^{j,\text{plug}}(X),
\end{align*}
where \(\tau^{j,\text{plug}}\) denotes the orthogonal projection onto the \(s_j\)-dimensional affine subspace spanned by \(\mathcal{A}_j\), and \(s_j = \dim(\mathcal{A}_j) + 1\) is the number of active (positive) coordinates in that face.

To build intuition, consider the 2-dimensional simplex with budget \(B\), whose feasible region is the triangle with vertices at \((0,0),\ (0,B),\ (B,0)\). Figure~\ref{fig:triangle} illustrates the faces \(\mathcal{A}_0\) through \(\mathcal{A}_6\), along with several example projections. 

\begin{itemize}
    \item \(\mathcal{A}_0\): Interior of the triangle.
    \item \(\mathcal{A}_1\)--\(\mathcal{A}_3\): Vertices (0-faces).
    \item \(\mathcal{A}_4\)--\(\mathcal{A}_6\): Edges (1-faces).
\end{itemize}

For instance, if \(X \in D_6 = \{ (x_1, x_2) : 0 < x_1 < B,\ x_2 < 0 \}\), the projection lands on edge \(\mathcal{A}_6\), assigning a transfer to the first individual only. Similarly, if \(X \in D_5\), which lies above and along edge \(\mathcal{A}_5\), both individuals receive partial transfers. If \(X \in D_0\), then \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X) = X\), since \(X\) is already feasible.

