\subsection{Weak Differentiability of \(\tau^{\text{plug}}\)}

Weak differentiability generalizes the classical notion of differentiability by allowing derivatives to be interpreted in a distributional sense. Hence, it allows one to meaningfully talk about derivatives for functions that may not be differentiable in the classical sense due to kinks or discontinuities on sets of measure zero. Intuitively, a function is weakly differentiable if its derivative exists "on average," as formalized by an integration-by-parts identity. 

Let \(\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n\) be an open set. A function \(u: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}\) is said to be \emph{weakly differentiable} with respect to variable \(x_i\) if there exists a function \(v \in L^1_{\text{loc}}(\Omega)\) such that the following integration-by-parts identity holds:
\[
\int_{\Omega} u(x) \frac{\partial \varphi}{\partial x_i}(x) \, dx = - \int_{\Omega} v(x) \varphi(x) \, dx \quad \text{for all } \varphi \in C_c^\infty(\Omega),
\]
where \(C_c^\infty(\Omega)\) is the space of smooth functions with compact support, and \(L^1_{\text{loc}}(\Omega)\) denotes the space of functions that are integrable on every compact subset of \(\Omega\).

We now prove that each component \(\tau_i^{\text{plug}}\) of the plug-in estimator is weakly differentiable. 

\paragraph{Step 1: Lipschitz continuity implies local integrability.} 
The estimator \(\tau^{\text{plug}}\) is defined as a projection onto a convex set, and projections onto closed convex sets in Euclidean space are non-expansive and Lipschitz continuous. In particular, \(\tau^{\text{plug}}\) is constructed from max and affine functions and is known to be Lipschitz. Therefore, each component \(\tau_i^{\text{plug}}\) is locally integrable on \(\mathbb{R}^n\).

\paragraph{Step 2: Differentiability within each region.}
Let \(\mathcal{K}(X) = \{ i : X_i > \lambda^*(X) \}\) denote the active set where \(\tau_i^{\text{plug}}(X) > 0\), and let \(s = |\mathcal{K}(X)|\). Fix a region where \(\mathcal{K}(X)\) remains constant; define
\[
\Omega_{\mathcal{K}} = \{ X \in \mathbb{R}^n : \mathcal{K}(X) = \mathcal{K} \}.
\]
Within \(\Omega_{\mathcal{K}}\), the projection is affine. More precisely, for \(i \in \mathcal{K}\),
\[
\tau_i^{\text{plug}}(X) = X_i - \lambda^*(X), \quad \text{where } \lambda^*(X) = \frac{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{K}} X_i - B}{s}.
\]
Differentiating this expression with respect to each component \(X_l\), we obtain:
\[
\frac{\partial \tau_i^{\text{plug}}}{\partial X_l} = 
\begin{cases}
1 - \frac{1}{s} & \text{if } i = l \in \mathcal{K}, \\
-\frac{1}{s} & \text{if } i \neq l \in \mathcal{K}, \\
0 & \text{if } i \notin \mathcal{K}.
\end{cases}
\]
These partial derivatives are constant within \(\Omega_{\mathcal{K}}\), hence they are measurable and bounded. Thus, they are integrable over any compact subset.

\paragraph{Step 3: Boundaries and measure zero sets.}
The boundaries where \(\mathcal{K}(X)\) changes are hyperplanes defined by \(X_i = \lambda^*(X)\). These boundaries are unions of lower-dimensional affine subspaces in \(\mathbb{R}^n\) and therefore have Lebesgue measure zero. Since classical differentiability holds on a full-measure subset, and \(\tau_i^{\text{plug}}\) is locally integrable and its weak partials are integrable, weak differentiability follows \cite{ziemer1989functions,bressan2012lecture}.
\begin{lemma}[Weak Differentiability Plug-in Rule]
\label{lemma:plug}
	Each component \(\tau_i^{\text{plug}}(X)\) of the plug-in estimator is weakly differentiable on \(\mathbb{R}^n\).
\end{lemma}

\subsection*{B.3 Weak Differentiability and Divergence of \(g(X)\)}

Let \(g(X) := \tau^{\text{plug}}(X)/\|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^2\). This normalized direction vector plays a central role in the Stein-type shrinkage estimator. In this section, we establish that \(g(X)\) is weakly differentiable and derive an explicit expression for its divergence.

\paragraph{Step 1: Domain of \(g\).} 
Observe that \(g(X)\) is only undefined at points \(X\) where \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X) = 0\), which occurs only if \(X_i \leq \lambda^*(X)\) for all \(i\). Since \(\tau^{\text{plug}}\) satisfies \(\sum_{i=1}^n \tau_i = B > 0\), this situation occurs on a measure-zero subset of \(\mathbb{R}^n\). Thus, \(g(X)\) is well-defined almost everywhere.

\paragraph{Step 2: Quotient rule for weak derivatives.} 
Within any region \(\Omega_{\mathcal{K}}\) where the active set \(\mathcal{K}\) is fixed, both \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\) and its partial derivatives \(\partial \tau_i^{\text{plug}} / \partial X_j\) are constant. The function \(\|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^2\) is a smooth, positive function on \(\Omega_{\mathcal{K}}\), hence differentiable. Using the quotient rule for derivatives of vector-valued functions, we obtain:
\[
\frac{\partial g_i}{\partial X_j} = \frac{1}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}\|^2} \cdot \frac{\partial \tau_i^{\text{plug}}}{\partial X_j} - \frac{2\tau_i^{\text{plug}}}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}\|^4} \cdot \sum_{k=1}^n \tau_k^{\text{plug}} \cdot \frac{\partial \tau_k^{\text{plug}}}{\partial X_j}.
\]

\paragraph{Step 3: Computing divergence.}
Let \(s\) denote the number of positive entries in \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\), i.e., \(s = |\mathcal{K}|\). Recall from B.2 that, for \(i \in \mathcal{K}\), we have \(\tau_i^{\text{plug}} = X_i - \lambda^*\), and \(\partial \tau_i^{\text{plug}} / \partial X_j\) is constant. The divergence of \(g(X)\), defined as \(\sum_{i=1}^n \partial g_i / \partial X_i\), becomes:
\[
\text{div } g(X) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{K}} \left[ \frac{1 - 1/s}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}\|^2} - \frac{2 \tau_i^{2}}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}\|^4} + \frac{2 \tau_i B}{s \cdot \|\tau^{\text{plug}}\|^4} \right].
\]
After simplification, this yields:
\[
\text{div } g(X) = \frac{s - 3}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^2} + \frac{2B^2}{s \cdot \|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^4},
\]
where \(s = |\mathcal{K}(X)|\).

\begin{lemma}[Weak Differentiability and Divergence of \(g(X)\)]
\label{lemma:divg}
	The function \(g(X) = \tau^{\text{plug}}(X)/\|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^2\) is weakly differentiable on \(\mathbb{R}^n\), and its divergence is given by:
\[
\text{div } g(X) = \frac{s - 3}{\|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^2} + \frac{2B^2}{s \cdot \|\tau^{\text{plug}}(X)\|^4},
\]
for all \(X \in \mathbb{R}^n\) such that \(\tau^{\text{plug}}(X) \neq 0\). The divergence is strictly positive when $s\geq 3$.
\end{lemma}

