\section{Introduction}
% ----------------------------- %
% ----------------------------- %
% General intro
Identifying the spiking activity of individual alpha motor neurons (MNs) non-invasively is possible by decomposing signals from activated muscles, e.g., obtained with multichannel surface electromyography (EMG) {\citep{Holobar2007a}} or ultrasound {\citep{Rohlen2020}}. 
This indirect motor neuron identification from compound muscle activity is possible because each motor neuron innervates tens up to several hundreds of muscle fibres, amplifying the motor neuron activity on a one-to-one basis \citep{Heckman2012}. 
The motor neuron and all the muscle fibres it innervates are defined as a motor unit (MU).
The activity of each MU can be modelled as a convolution of an impulse response (related to the muscle fibre activity) with the neural discharges of the motor neuron. Thus, template-matching {\citep{DeLuca2006, Nawab2010}} and convolutive blind source separation (BSS) {\citep{Holobar2007b, Negro2016}} are used to blindly estimate the discharges of a (sub)population of motor neurons. 
Although template-matching methods are effective for decomposing intramuscular EMG signals \citep{McGill2005} or under low force levels, these methods face challenges at higher force levels due to increased interference {\citep{Zhou2004}}. 
Convolutive BSS methods are reliable for extracting populations of motor neurons at low to high contraction levels {\citep{Avrillon2024}}. Although there are (slight) differences between state-of-the-art convolutive BSS-based motor neuron identification algorithms, they are built on the same theoretical background, most importantly, independent component analysis (ICA) {\citep{Hyvarinen2001}}.

Four main steps summarise ICA-based motor neuron identification: 1) reformulate the convolutive mixture model to an instantaneous mixture model by adding delayed versions of the observations (referred to as the extension step), 2) decorrelate the extended data matrix through a whitening transformation, 3) source estimation by solving an optimisation problem, and 4) post-processing of estimated spike trains and projection vectors.
The first three steps (extension, whitening, and optimisation algorithm) are well described in the seminal Independent Component Analysis (ICA) book published in \citeyear{Hyvarinen2001} by {\citeauthor*{Hyvarinen2001}}. 
The novelty of the EMG-based decomposition methods to generic convolution BSS methods lies in post-processing (fourth step). This includes spike clustering, source quality estimation and (manual) refinement of the predicted spike trains {\citep[e.g.,][]{Negro2016, DelVecchio2020, Avrillon2024b}}.
Although variants of convolutive BSS, e.g., gradient convolution kernel compensation (gCKC) {\citep{Holobar2007b}} and fastICA {\citep{Chen2015, Negro2016}}, are widely used and validated for the decomposition of surface EMG or ultrasound data, the selected parameters of the algorithms are often built on empirical observations rather than theory.
Since surface EMG has been the first and still is the most important experimental modality for the non-invasive detection of motor neuron spike trains, many decomposition methods have been tailored for EMG signals. 
Yet, empirical observations, e.g., from the decomposition of ultrasound-based image sequences {\citep{Lubel2024, Rohlen2025}}, challenge some assumptions and guidelines of algorithms used for motor neuron identification.

%Multichannel surface EMG is the most mature technique for motor neuron identification non-invasively, where the commonly used convolutive BSS methods are gradient convolution kernel compensation (gCKC) {\citep{Holobar2007b}}, a fastICA-based method with {\citep{Chen2015}} and without {\citep{Negro2016}} a peel-off step. 

%Moreover, empirical evidence from the decomposition of ultrasound-based image sequences {\citep{Lubel2024,Rohlen2025}} challenges the assumptions of methods used for EMG signal decomposition. Therefore, it is necessary to revisit the convolutive BSS model to improve the understanding of the effect of various noise sources and the assumptions required for motor neuron identifiability, which is important for developing better-performing methods and translational capabilities.

%These methods reformulate the convolutive mixture model to an instantaneous mixture model by adding delayed versions of the matrices in the model (referred to as the extension step), followed by a whitening step, making the rows of the whitened extended observation matrix orthonormal. After that, the learning rule for finding a projection vector for one spike train at a time is identical up to a normalisation factor {\citep{Negro2016}}.  

%In other words, the EMG decomposition methods are based on these existing ICA methods available in the literature from and before the early 2000s. 

%Independence and sparsity aside, the novelty of the EMG-based decomposition methods with respect to the standard ICA methods lies in post-processing (fourth step) after applying methods such as fastICA. This post-processing step includes, e.g., peak detection method, refinement stages, quality metrics for the estimated sources, and, most importantly, editing the estimated spike trains (sources). The latter means adjusting the estimated spike trains and the projection vectors to remove likely false and missed spikes. This need for editing is described to be due to the contributions of other MUs, changes in skin-electrode contact noise, instrument noise, and artefacts {\citep{DelVecchio2020}}.

% Specific issues/open questions:
% 1) Spike train statistics (independence)
A fundamental requirement for the application of ICA is mutually independent sources, which motor neuron spike trains violate due to having delayed sources in the extension step {\citep{Farina2016a}} and common synaptic input(s). 
Nevertheless, independent motor neuron spike trains have been a widely used assumption in the literature for separation purposes {\citep{Merletti2008, Chen2015, Meng2022, Zheng2023, Grison2024, Clarke2024}}.
While ICA is known to be robust against modest violations of the independence assumption \citep{Hyvarinen2000}, the independence violation has also been explicitly acknowledged for surface EMG decomposition {\citep{Farina2016a}}.
It has been argued that due to the sparsity of the MU spike trains, the sum of spike trains is more Gaussian than a single spike train, making non-Gaussianity (a common optimization goal in ICA) also a tractable choice for separating dependent but sparse sources.
Indeed, sparse coding, that is, finding sparse representations in the data, is closely related to ICA {\citep{Hyvarinen1998a, Hyvarinen1998b}}. 
\textit{
Nevertheless, the role of latent source dependencies is not fully understood in convolutive BSS-based motor neuron identification methods.}
%The motor neurons comprise zeros and ones and are, per definition, sparse. Indeed, the independence vs sparsity discussion has been debated in other fields, such as fMRI {\citep{Daubechies2009, Calhoun2013}}. 

% 2) Identifiability, MUAP uniqueness
The decomposition problem is typically ill-conditioned. 
This means that the mixing matrix is not invertible, and even if the forward model is known, it is impossible to recover the activity of a complete motor neuron pool {\citep[e.g.,][]{Klotz2023}}.
Typically, low amplitude MUAPs (either due to size or depth) and MUs with non-unique MUAPs are considered non-detectable based on empirical rules {\citep[e.g.,][]{Farina2008}}.
The condition of the mixing matrix can theoretically be improved by treating non-detectable MUs as noise {\citep{Farina2016a}}. 
However, the role of noise has received little attention in the existing literature. 
If noise is explicitly modelled, it is often assumed to be white Gaussian noise {\citep{Chen2015}}.  
Noise-correcting methods assuming white noise are considered in a few original method papers {\citep[e.g.,][]{Holobar2007a, Negro2016, Lubel2024}} by truncating the eigenvalues of the signal's covariance matrix in the construction of the whitening matrix {\citep{Belouchrani1997}}. 
Yet, a white noise assumption is an oversimplification, particularly considering that non-detectable MUs are treated as noise.
\textit{
Thus, the existence and identifiability of inverse solutions and the corresponding estimation errors are not fully understood.} 

% 3) Stationarity of MU responses
%This is particularly true if fundamental assumptions of the ICA model are violated.
Most EMG-based decomposition methods assume the mixing matrix is stationary {\citep{Negro2016}}. 
For surface EMG, this approximately holds for isometric and non-fatiguing contractions. 
In contrast, mechanically, like in ultrasound recordings, it is known that the twitch shapes may vary from spike to spike in isometric contractions {\citep{Raikova2007}}. 
Indeed, the ultrasound-based decomposition method can still identify the motor neuron discharges where the MU twitches cannot be considered stationary {\citep{Rohlen2025}}.
Moreover, decomposition methods that can robustly handle non-stationarities are essential for many research questions in basic physiology and prosthetic control through neural interfaces.  
\textit{
Therefore, the limits of the linear convolution BSS model in the presence of non-stationarities must be systematically characterised.}

% 4) Algorithm parameters (extension factor):
%Recently, a convolutive BSS method that decomposes ultrasound-based image sequences into individual motor neuron spike trains was proposed {\citep{Lubel2024}}, which is based on the same foundation as the EMG-based decomposition methods (extension, whitening, optimisation algorithm, and post-processing). This approach can identify individual motor neuron spike trains by decomposing the intramuscular compound mechanical displacements. 
Guidelines for EMG-based decomposition suggest that the impulse response duration must be shorter than the inter-spike interval {\citep{Chen2015}}. 
Although the mechanical impulse response after a neural discharge (MU twitch) is approximately 10 times longer than the electrical impulse response (MUAP) and at least two to three times larger than typical inter-spike interval durations {\citep{McNulty2000, Raikova2008}}, a convolutive BSS method applied to sequences of ultrasound images could still identify motor neuron spike trains \citep{Lubel2024}. 
\textit{This example demonstrates that the guidelines for selecting appropriate decomposition parameters must be refined.}

 %On the other hand, MUAP uniqueness in EMG signals has been a core assumption in the separability of MUs, where pairs of MUAPs with a mean square difference of $< 5$ \% are considered identical and indistinguishable {\citep{Farina2008}}. It is unclear whether this criterion holds by separation using a convolutive BSS method. 

%All these discrepancies between theoretical assumptions and empirical evidence based on the results of the convolutive BSS method suggest the need to revisit to improve the understanding and assumptions required for motor neuron identifiability.
This study aims to update the understanding of the EMG-based convolutive BSS method for motor neuron identification. 
We summarize state-of-the-art convolution BSS methods used for motor neuron identification and derive new theoretical tools that allow studying the existence and identifiability of physiological solutions given the convolutive BSS problem.
The theory is underlined by computer simulations combining a realistic motor neuron pool model and a database of MUAPs extracted from experimental recordings.
In this way, we intend to bridge the gap from theory to application, which is important for translating motor neuron identification methods to other signal modalities like ultrasound and clinical applications. 

