%\mathbf{\tilde{z}}(t) \ &= \ \mathbf{\tilde{H}}_\mathrm{d}^w \mathbf{\tilde{s}}_\mathrm{d}(t) \, + \, \mathbf{\tilde{H}}_\mathrm{nd}^\mathrm{w} \mathbf{\tilde{s}}_\mathrm{nd}(t) \, + \, \bm{\tilde{\varepsilon}}^\mathrm{w}(t) \ ,
%\end{split}
%\end{equation}
%where $\mathbf{\tilde{z}}(t)$ is the whitened extended observations matrix, $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_\mathrm{d}^\mathrm{w}$ and $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_\mathrm{nd}^\mathrm{w}$ are the whitened and extended impulse responses of the detectable and non-detectable MUs, and $\bm{\tilde{\varepsilon}}^\mathrm{w}(t)$ is the whitened (coloured) noise. 
Thus, the $k$th spike train is then estimated by applying a suitable projection vector $\mathbf{w}_k^T$ to the extended and whitened observations:
