\subsection{Identification errors}\label{sec:results_accuracy}
% ----------------------------- %
To investigate how a reconstructed motor neuron spike train is affected by different contributions of the total EMG signal, we simulated an exemplary 64-channel surface EMG signal. 
We used a trapezoid-shaped drive with a maximal amplitude of \SI{10}{nA} (\SI{10}{s} ramping up, \SI{40}{s} plateau and \SI{10}{s} ramping down).
The common noise had a CoV of \SI{20}{\%} and the independent noise a CoV of \SI{5}{\%}.
This resulted in an active pool of 132 MUs (mean firing rate $10.9 \pm 4.1\,$Hz).
Finally, we added coloured Gaussian noise to the EMG signal such that we obtained a SNR of \SI{20}{dB}. 
After selecting an exemplary MU (i.e., \#105), we applied the optimal projection vector to the isolated contribution of MU \#105 {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}A)}, the contribution of the remaining 131 MUs {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}B)}, the additive coloured noise {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}C)}, and finally, to the total EMG signal with additive coloured noise {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}D)}. 
It can be observed that the projection of the single MU signal closely approximates a Dirac delta function {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}A)}.
The spike amplitude was $6.74$, which was equivalent to the norm of the corresponding column in the extended and whitened mixing matrix (i.e., as predicted by Equation~\ref{eqn:source_amplitude_estimate}).
The approximation error is because the delayed versions of the extended and whitened MUAP are not fully independent {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}E)}. 
Considering a window of plus/minus one sample, the observed spike amplitude was, on average, \SI{66}{\%} of the spike's peak height.
The cross-projection error was below \SI{15}{\%} for all other delays.
Notably, this allows the identification of doublets if two consecutive spikes are separated by at least three samples; see {Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:doublets}}.
The amplitude of the largest peak due to the activity of other MUs was $6.55$ {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}B and F)}, nearly two times larger than the expected amplitude of the most dominant (delayed) MUAP from all other MUs, with a value of $3.80$.
Further, the variance of the projected noise  {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}C)} was $0.28$, i.e., considerably lower than the worst-case estimate, which is a variance equal to one.
Finally, we note that the reconstructed MU source from the full EMG signal {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}D)} is equal to the superposition of the isolated projected signal components {(Figure~\ref{fig:reconstruction_accuracy}A-C)}, thus verifying Equation~\eqref{eqn:convBSSallerrors}.

