\begin{remark}\label{r6}
As we shall see in Section \ref{sec 6}, Theorem \ref{d2} is sharp for all possible $\dim Q$ of the orthonormal system. However, in the final section, we will show that the sharpness of Theorem \ref{codim2} and Theorem \ref{codim1} holds only for a certain, although wide, range of $\dim Q$, which in itself is an interesting phenomenon.
On the other hand, as stated in Theorem \ref{codim1}, we do not know whether $\alpha(d-1,d,p)$ is sharp for $2<p<4$. Compared to Theorem \ref{d2}, it seems that our bounds might not be the best possible.
\end{remark}




Similar to \cite{frank2017}, a crucial point of our result is that the exponent $1/\alpha(k,d,p)<1$ for all $p>2$. Indeed, applying the triangle inequality to the right‐hand side of \eqref{3} and estimating each $f_j$ using \eqref{lp restriction} yields
\begin{equation}\label{30}
\Biggl\|\sum_{j\in J}|f_j|^2\Biggr\|_{L^{p/2}(\Sigma)}
\le C\,\lambda^{2\delta(k,d,p)}\,\dim Q,
\end{equation}
which is far from optimal in light of our results.

Moreover, the exponent $\alpha(k,d,p)=\tfrac p2$ that appear mutiple times in our theorems is natural. To see this, consider any orthogonal (but not necessarily normalized) subset $\{f_j\}_{j\in J}\subset E_\lambda$. Then by our reults \footnote{Possibly modulo a $\log\lambda$–loss.},
\begin{equation}\label{Minkowski}
\Big\|\|\{f_j\}\|_{l^2(J)}\Big\|_{L^p(\Sigma)}
\le C\,\lambda^{\delta(k,d,p)}\,
\Big\|\{\|f_j\|_{L^2(M)}\}\Big\|_{l^p(J)}.
\end{equation}
This shows that Theorems \ref{codim2}, \ref{d2} and \ref{codim1} allow us to interchange the underlying space in the Lebesgue norm. On the other hand, a direct application of Minkowski’s inequality together with \eqref{lp restriction} gives
\begin{equation}\label{Minkowski2}
\Big\|\|\{f_j\}\|_{l^2(J)}\Big\|_{L^p(\Sigma)}
\le C\,\lambda^{\delta(k,d,p)}\,
\Big\|\{\|f_j\|_{L^2(M)}\}\Big\|_{l^2(J)}.
\end{equation}
Clearly \eqref{Minkowski} improves \eqref{Minkowski2} for all $p>2$.

The optimality of our results can be understood from a different point of view. Let us illustrate this in the surface case. When $\dim M=2$, we have the following corollary.

\begin{corollary}
Let $d=2$, $k=1$. For any orthonormal system $\{f_j\}_{j\in J}\subset E_{\lambda}$ with $\#J\sim\lambda^{\beta}$ for some $0\le\beta\le1$, we have
\begin{align}\label{ss1}
\Biggl\|\sum_{j\in J}|f_j|^2\Biggr\|_{L^{p/2}(\Sigma)}
\le C\,(\log\lambda)^{h(1,2,p)}\,\lambda^{\gamma(p)},
\end{align}
where
\begin{align*}
\gamma(p) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{\beta + 1}{2}, & 2 \le p \le 4,\\
1 - \dfrac{2(1-\beta)}{p}, & 4 \le p \le \infty.
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The work of Burq--Gérard--Tzvetkov shows that \eqref{ss1} is sharp when
$\beta=0$ (equivalently $\#J\sim1$), up to a single $\log\lambda$ loss.
For $\beta=1$—that is, for $Q=E_\lambda$—we have $\gamma(p)=1$, so
\eqref{ss1} is already optimally sharp by the pointwise Weyl law on the
sphere.  Moreover, as noted earlier, Section~\ref{sec 6} proves the
stronger fact that \eqref{ss1} is saturated on the two-sphere for every
$\beta\in(0,1)$.
\end{remark}


The proof of our result rests on three key ingredients: a duality principle in Schatten spaces, a parametrix for the wave equation, and certain oscillatory integral estimates from \cite{bgt2007}. As in Frank–Sabin, we use the duality principle to reduce the estimation of $L^p$ norms for an orthonormal system of eigenfunctions to appropriate Schatten‐type bounds. We then follow the strategy of Burq–Gérard–Tzvetkov \cite{bgt2007}: they employ Sogge’s trick to rewrite the spectral projection operator via the half‐wave operator and use a parametrix to convert it into an oscillatory integral operator. A standard $TT^*$ argument and a dyadic decomposition of the resulting kernel 
\[
K(z,z')=\sum_j K_j(z,z'),\qquad |z-z'|\sim 2^j,
\]
together with a formula for the Schatten‐2 norm of the corresponding operator, then completes the proof of Theorem \ref{codim2}. Unlike in \cite{frank2017}, we do not employ complex interpolation.

This paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec 2} recalls the definition of Schatten classes and the
duality principle from \cite{sabin2017}.  In Section~\ref{sec 3} we
carry out the standard reduction.  Sections~\ref{sec 4} and
\ref{sec 5} assemble these ingredients to prove our main theorems.
Section~\ref{sec 6} shows that \eqref{ss1} is essentially sharp on the
two-sphere for every $\beta\in(0,1)$.  Finally,
Section~\ref{sec 7} establishes the sharpness parts of
Theorems~\ref{codim2} and~\ref{codim1}.


