\section{Approach and Motivation}

% \ac{first paragraph is very broad could be avoided for this super short paper}
% The integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) into network management is increasingly recognized as a critical component of future network infrastructures. Beyond mobile networks and emerging 6G architectures, AI is expected to play a central role in enabling intent-based, adaptive, and autonomous behaviors across a broad range of networking domains, from data center fabrics to optical and edge networks \cite{cui2025overview, zhou2024large}. \ac{until here}

Recent research has demonstrated the effectiveness of Artificial Intelligence (AI), and more specifically, Large Language Models (LLMs), in supporting network configuration synthesis~\cite{wang2024netconfeval} and automating network diagnosis~\cite{wang2024netassistant} tasks, among others. 
In this preliminary work, we restrict our focus to the application of AI agents to network troubleshooting
%\footnote{we refer to observability as the combination of monitoring and diagnosis capabilities} 
and elaborate on \emph{the need for a standardized, reproducible, and open benchmarking platform, where to build and evaluate AI agents with low operational effort}.
This platform primarily aims to \emph{standardize} and \emph{democratize} the experimentation with AI agents, by enabling researchers and practitioners -- including non domain-experts such as ML engineers and data scientists -- to focus on the evaluation of AI agents on curated problem sets, without concern for underlying operational complexities. 
Custom AI agents can be easily plugged through a single Application Programming Interface~(API) and rapidly evaluated. 
We present a modular and extensible benchmarking framework that supports widely adopted network emulators~\cite{bonofiglio2018kathara, mininet, containernet, containerlab}. 
It targets an extensible set of network issues in diverse real-world scenarios -- \emph{e.g.}, data centers, access, WAN, etc. -- and orchestrates the end-to-end evaluation workflows, including failure injection, telemetry instrumentation and collection, and agent performance evaluation. 


\smartparagraph{The natural quest for LLMs for network troubleshooting.}
Given a network problem, network engineers need to undertake mechanical yet cumbersome steps to diagnose and mitigate the issue~\cite{wang2024netassistant,netseer}. 
%across multiple dimensions and domains to perform root cause analysis (RCA) and mitigation~\cite{wang2024netassistant,netseer}. 
They can be summarized as \emph{(i)} identifying the right telemetry signals to collect, \emph{(ii)} navigating dashboards and interpreting the collected data, 
% \emph{(iii)} performing trials with Command-Line Interfaces~(CLIs) 
\emph{(iii)} taking corrective actions based on the insights derived from the telemetry data \emph{(iv)} iterating on the previous speculating about root-causes and \emph{what-if} hypothesis. 
% These tools remain highly configurable and manual effort is still necessary to use them efficiently for tasks like root cause analysis (RCA) and mitigation. 
% Atop this abundance of data, 
Typically, engineers need to switch on-the-fly across different monitoring configurations, as network issues are ongoing and escalating. Common examples include probing the network to explain packet drops~\cite{netseer, bufscope} or refining the detection logic -- \emph{e.g.}, 
%adjust sketch configuration/instance, 
request the collection of more fine-grained queue-length data to zoom into an ongoing incident~\cite{zoom2net}, enable debug-level diagnostics, etc.
This manual process is still complex, slow and error-prone, as it requires expert operators to reason across multiple dimensions.
% In short, human capacity still bounds the rate at which monitoring reconfigurations are pushed to the network.
Furthermore, modern network telemetry paradigms over programmable data planes -- such as sketches~\cite{sketchovsky, dta} and in-band network telemetry (INT)~\cite{int} -- %have enabled fine-grained observability and enriched 
% have enabled more fine-grained visibility and 
have expanded the range of available measurement strategies. The increased expressiveness may introduce additional degrees of freedom and come at the cost of greater operational complexity.
%provided network operators with a richer set of network measurement alternatives. 
% Today's networks can export significantly large volumes of heterogeneous telemetry data~\cite{dta}. 
Thus, we observe that while programmable data planes and new telemetry techniques have enhanced operators' visibility on the network, human intervention still remains a primary bottleneck in network triaging. %of diagnosing and resolving network issues.


Thanks to their ability to parse multimodal data and, more recently, engage in natural-language-driven reasoning~\cite{wang2024netconfeval, wang2024netassistant, netllmbench}, LLMs -- as a breakthrough category of AI models -- hold a special promise for assisting network operators. % to automate this process. 
As a result, our community has begun to explore how traditional approaches can evolve into more automated, LLM-assisted \emph{intent-based} network monitoring and diagnosis solutions~\cite{angi2025llnet, habib2025llm, li2025llmsketch, moghadas2025stradallm}.
%that require reasoning over domain-specific knowledge and context.

% this requires a platform/benchmark to automatically provision the setup of network topologies (diverse networks), traffic generation, failure injection, instrumentation for telemetry collection.

\smartparagraph{Lack of holistic platforms and benchmarks.}
Nevertheless, existing experimentation environments~\cite{bonofiglio2018kathara, containerlab} are often limited in scope, lacking standardized and reproducible benchmarks.
% \ac{following are details (also implementation) about what we do that we partially already said.. not sure it goes here. Maybe at the end of intro when we state the contribution. Here I would speak more extensively why we need a benchmark and why a playground. Just need to reorder a bit}
Recently, benchmarks for LLM agents have been proposed in the area of network configuration, such as NetConfEval~\cite{wang2024netconfeval}. 
% \ac{next sentence is also out of place and not useful to this discussion}
% NetConfEval~\cite{wang2024netconfeval} has shown that LLMs can automate the translation of human-readable intents into device configurations, alleviating the need for operators to master multiple vendor-specific syntaxes.
This work assumes that LLM agents can be evaluated on static benchmarks, with one-shot and offline executions. % operator issues an intent to LLMs, that interpret and deploy to the devices accordingly. 
While this assumption holds true for many problem instances in network configuration, \emph{network troubleshooting} is an inherently more dynamic and interactive problem space. It requires real-time feedback loops with the network, where AI agents must not only observe but also probe, react, and refine based on the evolving system conditions. 
%\ac{next sentence we already partially said before and breaks flow, would cut}For example, diagnosing packet drops or performance anomalies may involve iterative troubleshooting steps~\cite{netseer,wang2024netassistant} with connectivity checks, live port counters inspection, or reconfiguring telemetry granularity. 
Thus, it is essential to evaluate AI agents in environments that allow for such interactive, closed-loop operations, where they can dynamically adapt their strategies based on real-time telemetry and network state.
Unfortunately, engineers wishing to experiment with AI agents for network troubleshooting must still shoulder the significant burden of developing custom infrastructure code to enable this agent-environment interaction, as no modular, low-effort platform -- abstracting network environments (e.g., cloud, edge, mobile, satellite), protocols, failure modes, and tooling -- exists to date.
% Such gaps poses a barrier to the development, evaluation and cross-comparison of AI-driven observability solutions~\cite{angi2025llnet, habib2025llm, li2025llmsketch, moghadas2025stradallm}. 


We draw attention to the risk that, in the absence of standardized evaluation practices, research in this area remains fragmented and difficult to compare.
In response, we argue for a holistic, universally accepted platform that combines an extensible benchmark suite and a flexible, plug-and-play network playground -- e.g., based on existing network emulators. We develop a preliminary Proof-of-Concept~(PoC) and validate it with a ReAct agent for a toy-case failure scenario. We then outline challenges and future directions towards our vision.

