Based on this design, we prototype an initial PoC\footnote{codebase will be open-sourced} on top of Kathara~\cite{bonofiglio2018kathara} and validate an end-to-end network failure scenario, which we triage using a ReAct~\cite{yao2023react} AI agent.
The user (e.g., ML engineer) selects one of the pre-defined network issues \circled{A} through a declarative interface.
% plugs the code implementing the agent logic by rewriting a callback \texttt{execute\_agent}, 
The user can implement the AI agent logic simply by rewriting a callback \texttt{execute\_agent}. Then, the user can plug the code into our platform and kickoff the evaluation workflow in a single command. The playground is responsible for instantiating the experiment and orchestrating the underlying operations such as traffic generation, fault (or misconfiguration) injection \circled{B}, telemetry instrumentation and telemetry collection \circled{C}. 
%The experiment starts when the playground invokes the callback function that initiates the execution of the AI agent. 
The agent can then interact with the network environment through a set of MCP-based~\cite{mcp2025} tools \circled{D} 
that expand its capabilities. Our design follows related work~\cite{aiopslab} in microservice applications.


