\begin{abstract}
This paper shows that linearizing the transverse discontinuity mapping (TDM) in Filippov systems can produce inaccurate predictions of the dynamics in impact oscillators operating near a pre-stressed soft barrier.
%when compared to those obtained via direct numerical simulations and their corresponding electronic experiments.
%carries out stability analyses of impact oscillators with a single soft barrier, discusses the issues around linearizing the transverse discontinuity maps when compared to the dynamics obtained via numerical integration, as well as electronic experiments.
This discrepancy arises from the limitations of the linearized saltation matrix, which inaccurately predicts impacts in the local neighborhood of the discontinuity boundary. To address this issue, a higher-order approximation of the TDM is derived, which accurately captures the onset of impacts and closely matches the results obtained from both numerical simulations and electronic experiments. The proposed higher-order TDM results in a quadratic estimation of flight time for impacts. Geometrically, real-valued impact events are only feasible when the discriminant of this quadratic equation is positive. The differences in the predicted higher-order flight times and mapping estimates become more pronounced for low-velocity impacts close to grazing. Subsequently, a numerical approximation of the higher-order saltation matrix and its consequent Floquet multipliers and Lyapunov exponents for stability analysis is proposed and demonstrated on a pre-stressed, forced, damped, soft impact oscillator. To validate the numerically observed discontinuity-induced bifurcations, an analog electronic circuit is proposed that models a soft mechanical prestressed oscillator. This inductor-less circuit overcomes the limitations of typical LCR-based circuits, which are used to design such oscillators but cannot accommodate low stiffness ratios. The experimentally obtained limit cycles, finger-shaped Poincar\'e sections, and bifurcation diagrams match the predictions of the higher-order TDM, validating that the proposed circuit accurately models the soft-impact oscillator for both low and high stiffness ratios.
%A representative case with a mass ratio of 1 has been presented for validation. 
%The resulting stability analyses match with the forward and backward bifurcation diagrams obtained numerically and experimentally, confirming that the proposed higher-order TDM accurately predicts the transition of orbits from stable to unstable behaviour in the hysteretic region, leading to discontinuity-induced bifurcations.
\end{abstract}

