\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec 6}
%A closed-form expression of a higher-order transverse discontinuity mapping (TDM) for piecewise-smooth Filippov systems has been derived. The higher-order TDM relies on an improved higher-order flight time. This higher-order flight time estimates when perturbations in the local neighborhood of an impacting orbit reach the discontinuity boundary. The resulting expression is a quadratic equation and implies that perturbations reach the discontinuity boundary for which the discriminant is positive. This provides an analytical estimate of a range of perturbations for which impacts occur - perturbations larger than this range result in an imaginary root with a negative discriminant, implying that impacts do not occur. On the contrary, the first-order flight time results in a real fraction and predicts impacts for all perturbations. Hence, the first-order saltation matrix cannot capture when perturbations do not reach the discontinuity barrier. Additionally, the higher-order flight time and TDM comprise correction terms in their denominator that ensure that orbits do not diverge for low-velocity impacts near grazing. This results in an accurate mapping of perturbations and avoids overestimating the mapped state inherent in the first-order saltation matrix.

