\noindent
The inclusion–exclusion reduction produces the formula:
\[
\psi =
       (\neg\text{\d{$c_1$}}\vee a)
\wedge (\neg\text{\d{$c_1$}}\vee \neg b)
\wedge (\neg\text{\d{$c_1$}}\vee \neg c)
\wedge (\neg\text{\d{$c_2$}}\vee \neg a)\useieee{\]\vspace{-1.75em}\[}
\wedge (\neg\text{\d{$c_2$}}\vee b)
\wedge  (\neg\text{\d{$c_2$}}\vee \neg c)
\wedge (\neg\text{\d{$c_3$}}\vee c).
\]
with weight $w(\text{\d{$c_1$}})=w(\text{\d{$c_2$}})=w(\text{\d{$c_3$}})=-1$ and
$w(a)=w(b)=w(c)=1$. The number of
assignments that falsify $c_1$ is $1$, for $c_2$ is $1$, and $4$ for
$c_3$. There are no assignments that falsify $c_1$ and $c_2$ or $c_1$
and $c_3$ simultaneously, respectively; there is also no
assignment that falsifies $c_2$ and $c_3$. Since, finally, there is no
assignment that falsifies all three clauses, we obtain
$\#_w(\psi)=2^3-1-1-4+0+0+0-0=2=\#(\phi)$. 
\end{example}


