\title{From Mass-Shell Factorisation to Spin: An Attempt at a Matrix-Valued Liouville Framework for Relativistic Classical and Quantum Phase-Spacetime}
%Emergence of a Classical Dirac-Type Spin from On-Shell Factorisation and Liouvillian Evolution}

\author{Mark Everitt}
\affiliation{Quantalytics, Loughborough, UK}
\email{m.j.everitt@physics.org}
\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}
While Liouville's theorem is first-order in time for the phase-space distribution itself, the relativistic mass-shell 
constraint $p^\mu p_\mu = m^2c^2$ is naively second-order in energy. 
We argue that it is reasonable to unify both energy branches within a single Hamiltonian by factorizing $(p^2 - m^2)$ in analogy with Dirac's approach in relativistic quantum mechanics.
We show the resulting matrix-based Liouville equation remains first order and naturally yields a $4\times4$ matrix-valued probability density function in phase space as a classical analogue of a relativistic spin-half Wigner function. We investigate its classical physics and deformation quantisation. 
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\newcommand{\Gam}[1]
{{\sqrt{1 - \dfrac{{#1}^2}{c^2}}}}
\newcommand{\Lgn}{\mathcal{L}}
\newcommand{\Ham}{{H}}

\section{Motivation}
From a philosophical perspective, it is valid to argue that, at least empirically, physics should always be framed as a probabilistic theory due to intrinsic uncertainties in initial conditions, physical parameters, and measured values. 
Even if one does not hold this view, it seems natural to argue that any physical theory should allow statistical descriptions of ensembles.
Consequently, Liouville's Hamiltonian theorem merits particular consideration. 
From a pedagogical viewpoint, there is considerable value in teaching quantum physics through its phase-space formulation, wherein quantum mechanics naturally emerges as a deformation quantization of classical physics; replacing the Poisson bracket with the Moyal bracket and conventional distribution multiplication with the star product. 
However, a persistent gap in this approach has been the difficulty of naturally introducing spin, since quantum mechanical spin-half has no direct analogue in classical mechanics. 
Despite significant prior efforts using spin phase-space representations via SU(2) Wigner functions formulated in terms of a displaced-parity operator, a satisfying quantum-classical analogy remained elusive. 
Recently, an alternative strategy suggested itself after revisiting the standard derivation of the Dirac equation. 
The argument presented here closely follows Dirac's original reasoning, modified to utilize the relativistic Liouville equation instead of Schr\"odinger's equation. 
For simplicity, we limit the discussion to the single-particle free-space case and a particle in an electromagnetic field.

For a relativistic extension of Schr\"odinger's equation, Dirac argued that (a) it should make use of the mass shell, (b) the dynamical equation, like the Schr\"odinger equation, should be first-order in time but also (inspired by issues with the Klein-Gordon equation) that the Hamiltonian should be first-order in space, and (c) the four-momentum components should be replaced with their operator counterparts.
Unlike Dirac's argument, we will use the Liouville equation as the basis for our discussion but apart from this, and not needing to introduce operators, the assumptions (a) and (b) will be retained. 

We will then argue for a matrix relativistic Liouville equation and a matrix probability density that we will term a spinor-matrix distribution function. 

\section{Background}
As a starting point for our argument, recall that in classical mechanics a single 
particle with Hamiltonian $H(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{p},t)$ has a phase-space probability distribution $\rho(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{p},t)$ that evolves according to the Liouville equation
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} + \{\rho,H\} _3\;=\; 0,
\label{eq:liouville}
\end{equation}
where $\{A,B\}_3$ is the usual Poisson bracket in three dimensional space. 
Importantly note that, as with the Schr\"odinger equation,
eq.~\eqref{eq:liouville} is \emph{first-order} in time and, as for the Dirac constraints, the Poisson bracket is first order in phase-space variables. 

The Dirac equation resulted from seeking a relativistic generalization of Schr\"odinger's equation.
We will therefore seek relativistic generalizations of this equation with the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item First-order in space and time [instead we might equivalently require that our dynamical framework does  (i) not  reduce the dimension of the phase-space or (ii) exclude any solutions].
	\item Consistent with the mass-shell condition.
\end{enumerate} 
The only difference from the argument for the Dirac equation is that (i) we will not replace energy and time by their operator counterparts; (ii) by first-order in space we mean all phase-space derivatives.
The relativistic Liouville equation is given by the extended relativistic Poisson bracket vanishing~\cite{MARSDEN198629,hakim2011introduction}
\begin{equation}
	\PD{\Ham(x,p)}{p_\nu}\PD{W(x,p)}{x^\nu} -
	\PD{W(x,p)}{p_\nu}\PD{\Ham(x,p)}{x^\nu} \equiv \PB{\Ham}{W} = 0. \label{eq:relL}
\end{equation}
As with the non-relativistic Liouville equation this is also consistent with our constraint of being first order in phase-spacetime variables. At this stage, we might consider that there is no more to be done. However, when we consider the mass-shell, we will see that this brings with it second-order constraints that need to be dealt with. 
Specifically, the mass-shell constrains the phase-space to a seven-dimensional hypersurface where eliminating the momenta converts the Liouville equation into a second-order equation (both of which we wish to avoid).
We will now continue the argument and derive the free-particle Hamiltonian, reproducing standard textbook arguments~\cite{Landau:1975pou}.
The four-position and -velocity are given by:
\begin{align}
	x = 
	\begin{pmatrix}
	ct \\
	q^x \\	
	q^y \\	
	q^z \\	
	\end{pmatrix},
	v = \TD{x^\mu}{\tau} =
	\gamma
	\begin{pmatrix}
	c \\
	v_x \\	
	v_y \\	
	v_z \\	
	\end{pmatrix},	
\end{align}
where $\tau=\ud s/c$ is the proper time.
For a free particle the action has the form:
\begin{align}
	S=-mc \int_a^b \ud s.
\end{align}
The four momentum is then
\begin{align}\label{eq:4m}
	p_i = -\PD{S}{x^i}= mcv_i,
\end{align}
so 
\begin{align} \label{eq:shell}
	p^\mu p_\mu = m^2c^2,
\end{align}
yielding the so-called mass-shell condition. The Lagrangian will be
\begin{align}
	\Lgn =-mc^2 \Gam{\Vec{v}} \label{eq:rel:L},
\end{align}
so that the three-momentum is
\begin{align}
	\Vec{p} = \PD{\Lgn}{\Vec{v}}=\frac{ m \vec{v}}{\Gam{\Vec{v}}}. \label{eq:rel:3p}
\end{align}
Which is in agreement with the spatial components of eq.~\ref{eq:4m}. The Hamiltonian is 
\begin{align}
	\Ham =\Vec{p}\cdot \Vec{v} -\Lgn
	= \frac{mc^2}{\Gam{\Vec{v}}} \label{eq:rel:H}.
\end{align}
Squaring eqs.~\ref{eq:rel:3p} %(note this also $\gamma = \sqrt{1+p^2/(mc)^2}$) 
and \ref{eq:rel:H} and eliminating the velocity yields:
\begin{align}
	\Ham^2 = (m c^2 )^2 + (\Vec{p} c)^2.
	\label{eq:ham2}
\end{align}
Now using eq.~\ref{eq:shell} we have
\begin{align}
	\Ham^2 &= (m c^2 )^2 + (\Vec{p} c)^2, \\
	&= (m c )^2c^2 + (\Vec{p} c)^2, \\
	&= (p^\mu p_\mu)c^2 + \Vec{p}\,^2 c^2, \\
	\implies
	\frac{\Ham^2}{c^2} &= p^\mu p_\mu + \Vec{p}\,^2, \\
	\implies 
	p_0^2 &=  \left(\frac{\Ham}{c}\right)^2.
\end{align}
But this implies both positive and negative energy solutions. What if we want to capture both at the same time?

In textbook treatments of a single relativistic particle, one picks 
the positive-energy solution $p^0 = +\sqrt{\mathbf{p}^2c^2 + m^2c^4}$ 
\emph{a priori} to avoid negative energies and obtains a Hamiltonian $H(\mathbf{p}) = +\sqrt{\mathbf{p}^2 + m^2}$. This yields a first-order Liouville equation for the (scalar) phase-space density 
$\rho(t,\mathbf{x},\mathbf{p})$. Crucially, here we observe that such a choice \emph{excludes} the negative energy branch. 
If we wish to unify both energy branches in a single formalism, or simply include the mass-shell constraint as second order in 
$p^0$ without selecting a solution, there is an apparent mismatch: 
Liouville's theorem is first order in time, whereas the constraint 
$E^2 - \mathbf{p}^2 = m^2$ is second order.

\section{Relativistic Liouville equation and the spinor-matrix distribution function}
So let us return to the mass shell
$$
p^\mu p_\mu = p_0^2 - p_x^2-p_y^2-p_z^2= 
\left(\frac{\Ham}{c}\right)^2 - p_x^2-p_y^2-p_z^2
=
m^2c^2.
$$
Following Dirac let us seek a $p$ satisfying
$$
\gamma^0\left(\frac{\Ham}{c}\right) + \gamma^1 p_x+ \gamma^2 p_y+ \gamma^3 p_z = \sqrt{\left(\frac{\Ham}{c}\right)^2 - p_x^2-p_y^2-p_z^2}
$$
for some constants $\{\gamma^\mu\}$.
In 3+1 dimensions, the minimal Clifford algebra representation has 
dimension $4\times4$. 
If one wants to factorize the above expression a linear \emph{4-component} structure is forced. 
The solution that Dirac found in relativistic quantum mechanics, where these $\gamma^\mu$ are the same $\gamma$-matrices of tat work is also a solution here (the $p_i$ do not need to be operators to force the same solution). Inspired by the Dirac equation we might look for an equation of the form 
\begin{equation}
    \left(\gamma^\mu p_\mu - mc 1_4 \right) \psi = 0_4. \label{eq:df}
\end{equation}
Writing $p_{0}=\Ham/c$ and multiplying on the left by $\gamma^{0}$ gives
\begin{align}
\left(\frac{\Ham}{c} - \Vec{\alpha}\cdot \Vec{p} - \gamma^0 m c \right) \psi=0_4,	
\end{align}
with
\begin{align}
\alpha^{i} &= \gamma^{0}\gamma^{i}, \\
\COM{\alpha^{i}}{\alpha^{j}}_+&=2\delta^{ij},\\
\COM{\alpha^{i}}{\gamma^0}_+&=0_4.
\end{align}
where $\COM{\cdot}{\cdot}_+$ is the anti-commutator. Hence the matrix-valued Hamiltonian
\newcommand{\uHam}{\mathbf{\Ham}}
\newcommand{\uW}{\mathbf{W}}
\newcommand{\uF}{\mathbf{F}}
\newcommand{\uG}{\mathbf{G}}
\newcommand{\uK}{\mathbf{K}}
\newcommand{\uO}{\mathbf{O}}
\newcommand{\uP}{\mathbf{P}}

\begin{equation}
\uHam = c\,\Vec{\alpha}\cdot\Vec{p} + \gamma^0 m c^2
\end{equation}
%satisfies
%\begin{equation}
%\uHam^{2} = c^{2}\Vec{p}\,^{2} + m^{2}c^{4},
%\end{equation}
%which reproduces the mass-shell condition.
This Matrix Hamiltonian is however defined in terms of the three spatial momenta only and is not covariant. Our argument has gone too far. Let us return to the Dirac factorisation and define the LHS of eq.~\ref{eq:df} as a matrix super-Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
    \uK_\mathrm{free}(x,p)= c\gamma^\mu p_\mu - mc^2 1_4
\end{equation}
which is also first order in every momentum component, and (by definition) covariant. 
An alternative to consider is $c \gamma^0 (\gamma^\mu p_\mu - mc 1_4)$ which expands to
\newcommand{\aK}{{\cal{K}}}
\begin{align}
	\aK_\mathrm{free}= \Ham - c\Vec{\alpha}\cdot \Vec{p} - \gamma^0 m c^2 
\end{align} 
%Importantly note that while the free particle $\uK=0$ its derivatives do not.
We will use $\uK(x,p)$  instead of $\uHam$ in the relativistic Liouville equation. 
For our revised equation
\begin{align}
	\PD{\uK(x,p)}{p_\nu}\PD{\uW(x,p)}{x^\nu} -
	\PD{\uW(x,p)}{p_\nu} \PD{\uK(x,p)}{x^\nu}   
	 \equiv \PB{\uK}{\uW} = 0, \label{MLE}
\end{align}
to make sense $\uW$ must be a $4 \times 4$ matrix because it must live in the same non-commutative algebra as the Hamiltonian, $\uK(x,p)$, that drives its dynamics.



We will refer to it as a spinor-matrix distribution function.
Also note that the order in which $\uK$ and $\uW$ appears is now important and is chosen to be consistent with the underlying Lie algebra of the Poisson bracket, which ensures appropriate behaviour of the infinitesimal transformation of evolution.
While this extended Poisson bracket satisfies the axioms of a Lie algebra (bilinear, alternating and Jacobi identities) it fails the Leibniz rule and is therefore not a derivation with respect to matrix multiplication (but the trace of the bracket is). It is not clear to me if the trace being a derivation is enough to ensure any resulting theory is physically reasonable. Exploring the importance of this observation is beyond the scope of this work.

\subsection{Free particle example}

Let us now evaluate the relativistic Liouville equation~(\ref{MLE} for the free particle). As $\uK_\mathrm{free}(x,p)$ in this example has no explicit $x$ dependence and it is also linear in momentum we have:
\begin{equation}
\PD{\uK_\mathrm{free}(x,p)}{x^\nu}=\mathrm{0} \text{ and }
\PD{\uK_\mathrm{free}(x,p)}{p_\nu}=c\gamma^\nu
\end{equation}
so
\begin{equation}
\PB{\uK_\mathrm{free}}{\uW}=c\gamma^\nu \PD{\uW}{x^\nu}=0
\end{equation}
or (after dividing through by $c$)
\begin{equation}
\frac{\gamma^0}{c}\PD{\uW}{t}
+\gamma^1\PD{\uW}{x}
+\gamma^2\PD{\uW}{y}
+\gamma^3\PD{\uW}{z} \label{2562}
=0.
\end{equation}
For the alternative Hamiltonian $\aK$ we have
\begin{equation}
\PD{\uK_\mathrm{free}(x,p)}{x^\nu}=\mathrm{0} \text{ and }
\PD{\uK_\mathrm{free}(x,p)}{p_\nu}=c \gamma^0\gamma^\nu
\end{equation}
which leads to
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{c}\PD{\uW}{t}
+\alpha^1\PD{\uW}{x}
+\alpha^2\PD{\uW}{y}
+\alpha^3\PD{\uW}{z}
=0.
\end{equation}
where we have again divided by $c$ to make this version and eq.~\ref{2562} directly comparable.
\subsection{Charged particle in an electromagnetic field}
Let us add the electromagnetic field by replacing the canonical
four-momentum $p_\mu$ by the gauge-covariant
\begin{equation}
  \pi_\mu(x,p)=p_\mu-\frac{q}{c}\,A_\mu(x),
  \qquad
  F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu.	
\end{equation}
The \(4\times4\) covariant Hamiltonian is
\begin{align}
  \uK(x,p) &=
  c\gamma^\mu \pi_\mu(x,p)-mc^2\, 1_{4} \\
  &=\gamma^\mu (c p_\mu-q\,A_\mu(x))-mc^2\, 1_{4}\\
&= \uK_\mathrm{free} - q\gamma^\mu \,A_\mu(x)
\end{align}
%It satisfies \(K^2=\pi^2-m^{2}c^{2}\), i.e.\ the Klein-Gordon
%constraint and $\uK_\mathrm{free} =0$ is the mass-shell constraint.
The derivatives of the covariant Hamiltonian are:
\begin{align}
  \frac{\partial \uK}{\partial p_\nu}&=c\gamma^\nu,\\
  \frac{\partial \uK}{\partial x^\nu}
    &=-q\,\gamma^\mu\PD{ A_\mu}{x^\nu}\\
    &=-q\gamma^\mu\left( F_{\nu\mu}
      +\partial_\mu A_\nu\right).
\end{align}
The relativistic Liouville bracket is
\begin{align}
    \PB{\uK}{\uW}
    &=
    c\gamma^\nu 
    \PD \uW {x^\nu}
    +q\,\PD \uW {p_\nu} \gamma^\mu
      \PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}  \\
    &=
    c\gamma^\nu 
    \PD \uW {x^\nu}
    +q\,\PD \uW {p_\nu} \gamma^\mu
           \left( F_{\nu\mu}
      +\PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu} \right) \\&
      = 0.
\end{align}
Now for the alternative Hamiltonian
\begin{align}
	\aK &= c\gamma^0[\gamma^\mu (p_\mu-\frac{q}{c}\,A_\mu(x))-mc\, 1_{4}]\\
&= \aK_\mathrm{free} - q\gamma^0\gamma^\mu \,A_\mu(x)
\end{align}
so
\begin{align}
	  \PB{\aK}{\uW}
    &=
    c\gamma^0\gamma^\nu 
    \PD \uW {x^\nu}
    +q\PD \uW {p_\nu} \gamma^0\gamma^\mu
      \PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}=0
\end{align}
\subsection{An alternative bracket}
We also note that an alternative to the previous Poisson bracket might be
\begin{align}
%		  \frac{1}{2}\left[
&\MMB{\uK}{\uW}	= \label{extendedbracket} \\ 
&\nonumber \left(
	  \PD{\uK}{p_\nu}
	  \PD{\uW}{x^\nu}
	  +
	  \PD{\uW}{x^\nu}
	  \PD{\uK}{p_\nu}
	  \right)
-
	  \left(
	  \PD{\uK}{x^\nu}
	  \PD{\uW}{p_\nu}
	  +
	  \PD{\uW}{p_\nu}
	  \PD{\uK}{x^\nu}
	  \right)
%	  \right]
\end{align}
as (up to an irrelevant factor of $2$) this equation would reproduce the normal Poisson bracket if $\uK$ and $\uW$ commute.
Algebraically, this is not even a Lie bracket nor a derivation except in its trace, but we consider it for reasons that will become clear when we discuss deformation quantisation. 
Here the free particle dynamics would be given by:
\begin{align}
	\gamma^\nu 
    c\PD \uW {x^\nu}+c\PD \uW {x^\nu}\gamma^\nu 
    &=0\\
	c\PD {}{x^\nu}\COM{\gamma^\nu}{\uW}_+ 
    &=0 \label{reftoremarks}
\end{align}
The charged particle in an electromagnetic field would have dynamics given by:
\begin{align}
		c\gamma^\nu 
    \PD \uW {x^\nu}+c\PD \uW {x^\nu}\gamma^\nu 
    -
    q\PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}\left(\gamma^\mu \PD \uW {p_\nu}  +\PD \uW {p_\nu} \gamma^\mu \right)
       =0
       \\
       c\PD {}{x^\nu}\COM{\gamma^\nu}{\uW}_+
       -
       q\PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}\COM{\gamma^\nu}{\uW}_+
       =0
\end{align}
where $\COM{\cdot}{\cdot}_+$ is the anti-commutator.
For $\aK$ we have for the free particle
\begin{align}
	\PD {}{x^\nu}\COM{c \gamma^0\gamma^\nu}{\uW}_+ 
    &=0 
\end{align}
and for a charged particle in an electromagnetic field
\begin{align}
       c\PD {}{x^\nu}\COM{\gamma^0\gamma^\nu}{\uW}_+
       -
       q\PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}\COM{\gamma^0\gamma^\nu}{\uW}_+
       =0.
\end{align}
\subsection{Remarks}
To keep the relativistic Liouville equation linear in derivatives, and treat both energy signs at once, $\uHam$ must be matrix-valued. 
Once $\uHam$ lives in the Clifford algebra, consistency requires $\uW$ to live in the \emph{same} algebra so that the Poisson bracket $\{\uHam,\uW\}$ is well defined.
Moreover, Lorentz rotation or boosts acts/transforms in the expected way $\mathbf{S}(\Lambda)\uW \mathbf{S}^{-1}(\Lambda)$.
In relativistic quantum mechanics we can make a density matrix in this Clifford algebra from the Dirac bi-spinor $\psi \psi^\dag$ or $\psi \bar \psi$. The spinor-matrix distribution function $\uW$ is, I believe, the classical counterpart. 
If this is the case, then spin is not a quantum phenomenon per se. Rather it is a consequence of demanding that a theory is relativistically covariant, contains both energy solutions, and allows a statistical or probabilistic description.

An alternative to the relativistic Poisson bracket was also considered and, for the free particle, we found
$$
\MMB{\uK_\mathrm{free}}{\uW} =c\PD {}{x^\nu}\COM{\gamma^\nu}{\uW}_+ =0.
$$
Now if we were to define $\dW=\psi \bar \psi$ from Dirac bi-spinors then we would have
$$
\partial_\nu \dW = (\partial_\nu \psi)\bar \psi + \psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi 
$$
now
\begin{align}
\partial_\nu \COM{\gamma^\nu}{\dW}_+
&= {\gamma^\nu}{\partial_\nu \dW}+ {\partial_\nu \dW}{\gamma^\nu}
 \\ &= \nonumber
{\gamma^\nu}\left[(\partial_\nu \psi)\bar \psi + \psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi \right]+ \left[(\partial_\nu \psi)\bar \psi + \psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi \right]{\gamma^\nu}
\end{align}
Now the Dirac/Weyl equation in chiral (Weyl) representation is
$$
\gamma^\nu \partial_\nu \psi = 0 \text{\ \ \ and\ \ \  }  \partial_\nu \bar \psi \gamma^\nu = 0
$$
and so 
\begin{align}
\partial_\nu \COM{\gamma^\nu}{\dW}_+
 &= \nonumber
{\gamma^\nu}\left[\cancel{(\partial_\nu \psi)\bar \psi} + \psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi \right]+ \left[(\partial_\nu \psi)\bar \psi + \cancel{\psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi }\right]{\gamma^\nu}
\\
&= \gamma^\nu \psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi + (\partial_\nu \psi)\bar \psi\gamma^\nu
\\
&= A + B
\end{align}
where $A= \gamma^\nu \psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi$ and $B= (\partial_\nu \psi)\bar \psi\gamma^\nu$
Now $\gamma^\nu$ and $\dW$ are both Dirac Hermitian and so their anti-commutator will also be too. This means that the LHS and RHS of the above equation are both Dirac Hermitian. Now
\begin{align}
A^\dag&=(\gamma^\nu \psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi )^\dag
\\ &=
\gamma^0(\partial_\nu \psi)
\bar \psi
\gamma^\nu \gamma^0
\\ &=\gamma^0(\partial_\nu \psi)
\bar \psi
(\gamma^0\gamma^0)
\gamma^\nu \gamma^0
\\ &=\gamma^0(\partial_\nu \psi)
\bar \psi
\gamma^0(\gamma^0
\gamma^\nu \gamma^0)
\\ &=\gamma^0(\partial_\nu \psi)
\bar \psi
\gamma^0(\eta^{\nu \nu}\gamma^\nu)
\\ &=\eta^{\nu \nu}
\gamma^0(\partial_\nu \psi)
\bar \psi
\gamma^0\gamma^\nu
\\ &=
\eta^{\nu \nu}
(\partial_\nu \psi)
\bar \psi
\gamma^0\gamma^0\gamma^\nu
\\ &=
\eta^{\nu \nu}
(\partial_\nu \psi)
\bar \psi
\gamma^\nu
\\ &=\eta^{\nu \nu} B
\end{align}
where we have used $\gamma^0 \gamma^\nu \gamma^0 = \eta^{\nu \nu}\gamma^\nu$ as a corollary of $\COM{\gamma^\mu}{\gamma^\nu}=2\eta^{\mu\nu}$ where $\eta^{\mu\nu}=\mathrm{diag}(+1,-1,-1,-1)$.
We also have
\begin{equation}
B^\dag =	[(\partial_\nu \psi)\bar \psi\gamma^\nu]^\dag=\eta^{\nu \nu}\gamma^\nu \psi \partial_\nu \bar \psi
=   \eta^{\nu \nu} A
\end{equation}
Now
\begin{align}
\gamma^0 (A+B)^\dag \gamma^0
&= \gamma^0 (A^\dag+B^\dag) \gamma^0
\\
&=\eta^{\nu\nu} (A+B) 
\\
&= (A+B) 
\end{align}
The spatial parts of $A+B$ vanish algebraically and substituting back into the Dirac/Weyl equation implies that the temporal component must also vanish. And so finally we see that 
\begin{equation}
	\partial_\nu \COM{\gamma^\nu}{\dW}_+ = 0
\end{equation}
which, up to an overall factor of $c$, is consistent with eq.~\ref{reftoremarks} provided $\psi$ is a solution to, and therefore is consistent with, the Dirac/Weyl equation. This is an encouraging observation that adds a degree of legitimacy to eq.~\ref{extendedbracket}.

The analogy between $\uW$ and $\dW$ may turn out to be deeper still if it can be shown that relativistic Wigner functions, such as those discussed in \cite{PhysRevD.100.056021,KOWALSKI20161,refid0}, result from a deformation quantisation of the Poisson bracket to a Moyal bracket.
An outline of a potential argument is given next.
%That, however, is a subject for future work which, if successful, would open an approach to explaining much of quantum physics as a natural extension of classical physics.

\section{A minimal argument for quantum mechanics with issues}

Inspired once more by Dirac, but this time from his argument for the Heisenberg matrix formulation ~\cite{dirac1981principles} adapted for phase space arguments~\cite{QM}.
We start by noticing both the relativistic and non-relativistic Poisson bracket satisfy the definition of a Lie algebra:
\begin{align}
\PB{u}{v} &= - \PB{v}{u}, \\
\PB{a u_1 + b u_2}{v} &=  a\PB{u_1}{v} + b\PB{u_2}{v}, \\
%\PB{h}{a f + b g} =  a\PB{h}{f} + b\PB{h}{f}
\PB{u_1 u_2}{v} &= \PB{u_1}{v}u_2 + u_1\PB{u_2}{v}, \label{eq:PB:key1} \\
\PB{u}{v_1 v_2} &= \PB{u}{v_1}v_2 + v_1\PB{u}{v_2}, \label{eq:PB:key2} \\
\PB{\PB u v}{w} &+\PB{\PB w u}{v} + \PB{\PB v w}{u}  = 0.
\end{align}
where $a$ and $b$ are just numbers. 
These are the rules of infinitesimal transformations, such as rotation or translation. 

Dirac's argument for commutators was that for any dynamical theory, we would like to retain the structure that the state at time $t$ going to $t+\delta t$ is an infinitesimal transformation. 
That argument begins by considering $\QPB{u_1 u_2}{v_1 v_2}$. Apply equation~\ref{eq:PB:key1} to obtain:
\begin{equation}
\QPB{u_1 u_2}{v_1 v_2} = \QPB{u_1}{v_1 v_2}u_2 + u_1\QPB{u_2}{v_1 v_2},
\end{equation}
and then equation~\ref{eq:PB:key2} to obtain:
\begin{equation}
\QPB{u_1 u_2}{v_1 v_2} = \QPB{u_1 u_2}{v_1}v_2 + v_1\QPB{u_1 u_2}{v_2},
\end{equation}
equating these yields
\begin{equation}
\QPB{u_1}{v_1 v_2}u_2 + u_1\QPB{u_2}{v_1 v_2} = \QPB{u_1 u_2}{v_1}v_2 + v_1\QPB{u_1 u_2}{v_2},
\end{equation}
making use of the other relations and simplifying we find:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:COM_PB}
\QPB{u_1}{v_1}\COM{u_2}{v_2} = \COM{u_1}{v_1}\QPB{u_2}{v_2},
\end{equation}
where $\COM{a}{b}=ab-ba$. In this way, the argument for operators observables and commutation relations was made. 
With some work, one arrives at $\COM{\cdot}{\cdot} = \mathrm{i} \hbar\PB{\cdot}{\cdot}$ (where $\hbar$ is some constant to be determined through experiment) as a scheme for quantisation.
This is an imperfect scheme, as seen from Groenewold's theorem.

Phase-space provides an alternative approach. 
Here we seek to replace the relativistic matrix Poisson Bracket with a quantum counterpart which we will denote $\MB{\cdot}{\cdot}$ after the Moyal Bracket. that must form a Lie algebra and also demand that 
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{\hbar \Tends 0} \MB{\cdot}{\cdot} = \PB{\cdot}{\cdot},
  %\implies \MB{\cdot}{\cdot} = \PB{\cdot}{\cdot} + \mathcal{O}(\hbar^2)
\end{equation}
in terms of some yet to be determined constant $\hbar$.
This bracket is thus a continuous deformation of the Poisson bracket.
The bracket must also satisfy the Lie algebra conditions:
\begin{align}
\MB{u}{v} &= - \MB{v}{u}, \\
\MB{a u_1 + b u_2}{v} &=  a\MB{u_1}{v} + b\MB{u_2}{v}, \\
%\PB{h}{a f + b g} =  a\PB{h}{f} + b\PB{h}{f}
\MB{u_1 u_2}{v} &= \MB{u_1}{v}u_2 + u_1\MB{u_2}{v}, \label{eq:MB:key1} \\
\MB{u}{v_1 v_2} &= \MB{u}{v_1}v_2 + v_1\MB{u}{v_2}, \label{eq:MB:key2} \\
\MB{\MB u v}{w} &+\MB{\MB w u}{v} + \MB{\MB v w}{u}  = 0.
\end{align}
over the same phase space as the Poisson bracket. The non-relativistic version yields the normal Moyal equation for the dynamics of the Wigner function.
In this work, I propose a relativistic Liouville equation in terms of the matrices $\uK$ and $\uW$ to generate system dynamics.

The challenge is nevertheless the same: to build into the nature of the phase space-time dynamics the non-commutative nature of quantum mechanics.
The matrix approach in the main body nearly meets our need as $\hbar \Tends 0$.
Importantly, this approach fails to meet all
the Leibniz rules [equations (\ref{eq:MB:key1}) and (\ref{eq:MB:key2})] in matrix form. The trace does satisfy the Leibniz conditions and can be viewed as a derivative (again, it is not clear to me if this is enough to ensure any resulting theory is physically reasonable).
Following standard phase-space logic, we now seek a redefinition of spinor-matrix distribution functions, $\mathbf{F}$ and $\mathbf{G}$ in terms of a star product $\mathbf{F} \star \mathbf{G}$. Where  
\begin{align}
	  \MB{\mathbf{F}}{\mathbf{G}} =  \frac{1}{\mathrm{i}\hbar}[\mathbf{F} \star \mathbf{G} - \mathbf{G} \star \mathbf{F} ].
\end{align}
The $1/\mathrm{i}\hbar$ allows the non-relativistic approach to achieve this limit in an elegant way.
As we shall soon see, this approach does not work for the matrix extension.
Continuing the argument we look to define a new product of phase space functions of the form
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{F} \star \mathbf{G} = \sum_{n=0}^\infty \hbar^n,\Pi^n(\mathbf{F},\mathbf{G})
\end{equation}
where $\Pi^n(\mathbf{F},\mathbf{G})$ is some satisfactory function of the phase space distributions $\mathbf{F}$ and $\mathbf{G}$. 
If we set the zeroth-order term in this expression as $\mathbf{F}\mathbf{G}$ then we have
$$\lim_{\hbar \Tends 0} \mathbf{F} \star \mathbf{G} = \mathbf{F}\mathbf{G}
$$ 
and we recover usual multiplication of matrices of functions. 
To recover the usual Poisson bracket the next term must be:
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{F} \star \mathbf{G} = 
  \mathbf{F}\mathbf{G} + \frac{\mathrm{i} \hbar}{2}
  \left(
  \PD{\mathbf{F}}{x^\nu}\PD{\mathbf{G}}{p_\nu}
- \PD{\mathbf{F}}{p_\nu}\PD{\mathbf{G}}{x^\nu}
  \right)
   + \Order{\hbar^2},
\end{equation}
We will assume that the same solution for $\mathbf{F} \star \mathbf{G}$ as found in textbook treatments of the phase space formulation quantum mechanics which is
\begin{equation}
  \uF\star \uG = \sum_{i=1}^N {1 \over n!}\left( {\ui \hbar \over 2}\right)^n\Pi^n(\uF,\uG)
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
  \Pi^n(\uF,\uG)= \sum_{k=0}^n(-1)^k {n\choose k} 
  \left[\HPD{}{p}{k}\HPD{}{q}{(n-k)}\uF \right]
  \left[\HPD{}{p}{(n-k)}\HPD{}{q}{k}\uG \right]
\end{equation}
which can be more compactly written as
\begin{equation}
  \uF \star \uG = \uF \sum_{i=1}^N \exp \left[\ui \hbar 
  \left(\overset{\leftarrow}{\PD{}{q_i}}\overset{\rightarrow}{\PD{}{p_i}}- \overset{\leftarrow}{\PD{}{q_i}}\overset{\rightarrow}{\PD{}{p_i}}\right)\right] \uG
\end{equation}
where the arrow's indicate the direction in which the derivative is to act. 
Unlike the scalar case
\begin{align}
  \MB{\uF}{\uG} 
  &= \frac{1}{\ui \hbar}\left( \uF \star \uG - \uG \star \uF \right)
  \\&\neq  \frac{2}{\hbar}
   \sum_{i=1}^N \uF \sin \left[ \frac{\hbar}{2} 
  \left(\overset{\leftarrow}{\PD{}{q_i}}\overset{\rightarrow}{\PD{}{p_i}}- \overset{\leftarrow}{\PD{}{q_i}}\overset{\rightarrow}{\PD{}{p_i}}\right)\right]  \uG .
\end{align}
as derivatives of $\uF$ and $\uG$ may not commute.
And so we identify our first issue as
\begin{align}
	  &\MB{\mathbf{F}}{\mathbf{G}} =  \frac{1}{\mathrm{i} \hbar}\COM{\mathbf{F}}{\mathbf{G}} +
	  \nonumber \\ & 
	  \frac{1}{2}\left[
	  \left(
	  \PD{\uK}{x^\nu}
	  \PD{\uW}{p_\nu}
	  +
	  \PD{\uW}{p_\nu}
	  \PD{\uK}{x^\nu}
	  \right)
	  -
	  \left(
	  \PD{\uK}{p_\nu}
	  \PD{\uW}{x^\nu}
	  +
	  \PD{\uW}{x^\nu}
	  \PD{\uK}{p_\nu}
	  \right)
	  \right]
\nonumber \\ &+\Order{\hbar}.
\end{align}
And as such this will only reproduces the normal Moyal bracket (and Poisson bracket in the second term) if $\uK$ and $\uW$ commute.

The challenge will be to see if there is a resolution to such difficulties and verify the assumption that the higher-order corrections follow the pattern as for the non-relativistic case. Let us assume this will work and press on. The relativistic Moyal equation should be of the form:
\begin{equation}
\MB{\uK}{\uW} = 0.    
\end{equation}
Noting that 
$
\MB{\uK}{\uW} = 0
$
if
\begin{equation}
\uK \star \uW = \epsilon \uW
= \uW \star \uK
\end{equation}
which is of the same form as the time independent Schr\"odinger stargenvalue equations of non-relativistic quantum mechanics but in phase spacetime and, encouragingly, both of these sets of equations contain all powers of $\hbar$.

\subsection{Free particle}
\subsubsection{Results for $\uK_\mathrm{free}$}
Only the first-order momentum derivatives do not vanish so we have
\begin{align}
\uK_\mathrm{free} \star \uW
&=
\left(c\gamma^\mu p_\mu-mc^2 \right)  \uW - \frac{\ui \hbar}{2}c\gamma^\nu \PD{\uW}{x^\nu}\\
\uW \star \uK_\mathrm{free}
&=
\uW \left(c\gamma^\mu p_\mu-mc^2 \right) + \frac{\ui \hbar}{2}\PD{\uW}{x^\nu}c\gamma^\nu     
\end{align}
so the stargenvalue equations are
\begin{align}
\left(c\gamma^\mu p_\mu-mc^2 \right)  \uW - \frac{\ui \hbar}{2}c\gamma^\nu \PD{\uW}{x^\nu}
&=\varepsilon\uW
\\
\uW \left(c\gamma^\mu p_\mu-mc^2 \right) + \frac{\ui \hbar}{2}\PD{\uW}{x^\nu}c\gamma^\nu     & = \varepsilon\uW
\end{align}
and the covariant matrix Moyal bracket would be
\begin{equation}
 \MB{\uK_\mathrm{free}}{\uW}  = 
 \frac{c}{\ui \hbar}p_\mu
 \COM{\gamma^\mu}{W}
 -
 \frac{c}{2} \PD{}{x^\nu}\left(\gamma^\mu\uW+\uW\gamma^\mu\right)=0
\end{equation}
or
\begin{equation} 
 p_\mu
 \COM{\gamma^\mu}{W}
 -\frac{\ui \hbar}{2}
  \PD{}{x^\mu}\COM{\gamma^\mu}{\uW}_+=0
\end{equation}
%which is the same as the classical case.
%This is similar to classical situation where nothing quantum happens in the dynamical equations until potentials greater that the quartic potential are considered (this is not true for the stargenvalue equations where quantum effects arise sooner).
%To see if anything interesting happens dynamically a more complex example is needed.
\subsubsection{Results for $\aK_\mathrm{free}$}

Again, only the first-order momentum derivatives do not vanish so we have
\begin{align}
\aK_\mathrm{free} \star \uW
&=
\left(\Ham - c\alpha^i p^i - \gamma^0 m c^2  \right)  \uW - \frac{\ui \hbar}{2}c\gamma^0\gamma^\nu \PD{\uW}{x^\nu}
\nonumber
\\ &=
\epsilon \uW
\\
\uW \star \uK_\mathrm{free}
&=
\uW \left(\Ham - c\alpha^i p^i - \gamma^0 m c^2 \right) + \frac{\ui \hbar}{2}c\PD{\uW}{x^\nu}\gamma^0\gamma^\nu     
\nonumber
\\ &=
\epsilon \uW\end{align}
and the covariant matrix Moyal bracket would be
\begin{equation} 
 c \, p_i
 \COM{\alpha^i}{W}
+
 mc^2\COM{\gamma^0}{W}
 +\frac{\ui \hbar}{2}c
  \PD{}{x^\mu}\COM{\gamma^0\gamma^\mu}{\uW}_+=0
\end{equation}
where we have multiplied the entire equation by $-1$.
It is clear that this result is materially very different from that found for $\uK_\mathrm{free}$ (the implications of which will be discussed in a future version of this manuscript).
%which is the same as the classical case.
%This is similar to classical situation where nothing quantum happens in the dynamical equations until potentials greater that the quartic potential are considered (this is not true for the stargenvalue equations where quantum effects arise sooner).
%To see if anything interesting happens dynamically a more complex example is needed.


\subsection{Charged particle in an electromagnetic field}
Again the covariant Hamiltonian is
\begin{align}
  \uK(x,p) &=
  c\gamma^\mu \pi_\mu(x,p)-mc^2\, 1_{4}  \nonumber \\
  &=\gamma^\mu \left(cp_\mu-q\,A_\mu(x)\right)-mc^2\, 1_{4} \nonumber \\
&= \uK_\mathrm{free} - q\gamma^\mu \,A_\mu(x) \nonumber 
\end{align}
Because $\uK$ is linear in $\Pi_\mu$ the only surviving momentum derivative is:
\begin{align}
  \frac{\partial \uK}{\partial p_\nu}&=q\gamma^\nu.
\end{align}
However the position derivatives will be 
\begin{align}
  \partial_\nu^n \uK
    &=-q\,\gamma^\mu\partial^n_\nu{ A_\mu}
\end{align}
and will only vanish when $\partial^n_\nu{ A_\mu}=0$.
\begin{widetext}
The left stargenvalue equation is:
\begin{align*}
{\uK}\star{\uW} = \varepsilon \uW
\\
{\uK}{\uW} 
+\frac{\ui \hbar}{2}
\left(\PD{\uK}{x^\nu}\PD{\uW}{p_\nu}-\PD{\uK}{p_\nu}\PD{\uW}{x^\nu}\right)
+ \frac{1}{2!}
  \left(
    \frac{i\hbar}{2}
  \right)^{2}
  \left(
        \HPD{\uK}{{x^\nu}}{2}
        \HPD{\uW}{p_\nu}{2}
        - 
        2
        \frac{\partial^2\uK}{\partial x^\nu\partial p_\nu}
        \frac{\partial^2\uW}{\partial x^\nu\partial p_\nu}
                  + 
        \HPD{\uK}{{p_\nu}}{2}
        \HPD{\uW}{x^\nu}{2}    \right) \\
    + \frac{1}{3!}\!\left(\frac{i\hbar}{2}\right)^{\!3}
        \left(
        \HPD{\uK}{{x^\nu}}{3}
        \HPD{\uW}{p_\nu}{3} 
         -3\frac{\partial^3\uK}{\partial {x^\nu}^2\partial p_\nu}
        \frac{\partial^3\uW}{\partial x^\nu\partial p_\nu^2}         +
         3
         \frac{\partial^3\uK}{\partial x^\nu\partial p_\nu^2}
        \frac{\partial^3\uW}{\partial {x^\nu}^2\partial p_\nu}
          -       \HPD{\uK}{{p_\nu}}{3}
        \HPD{\uW}{{x^\nu}}{3}    
        \right) 
    + \mathcal{O}\left(\hbar^{4}\right)
= \varepsilon \uW
\\
\left[{\gamma^\mu \left(cp_\mu-q\, A_\mu(x)\right)-mc^2 1_{4}}\right]{\uW}+\frac{\ui \hbar}{2}
\left(
-q\gamma^\mu\PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}\PD{\uW}{p_\nu}-c\gamma^\mu\PD{\uW}{x^\nu}\right)
+
\frac{1}{2!}
  \left(
    \frac{i\hbar}{2}
  \right)^{2}
  \left(
  -q\gamma^\mu\HPD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}{2}        \HPD{\uW}{p_\nu}{2}
   \right)  +\mathcal{O}\left(\hbar^{3}\right)
=\varepsilon \uW
\end{align*}
\end{widetext}
we omit the other stargenvalue equation for brevity.
As an aside note that looking for non-trivial solutions to this stargenvalue equation when $\varepsilon=0$ would mean solving
\begin{align*}
&\left[{\gamma^\mu \left(cp_\mu-q\, A_\mu(x)\right)-mc^2 1_{4}}\right]{\uW}
\\
&\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ +\frac{\ui \hbar}{2}
\left(
-q\gamma^\mu\PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}\PD{\uW}{p_\nu}- c\gamma^\mu\PD{\uW}{x^\nu}\right)
+ \mathcal{O}\left(\hbar^{2}\right)
=0.
\end{align*}
This appears to similar in form to the Dirac equation in phase space seen in the literature~\cite{D1,D2,D3,D4}.

The Moyal bracket will be
\begin{align}
    \MB{\uK}{\uW}
    =&
    \frac{1}{\ui \hbar} \left(cp_\mu-q\, A_\mu(x)\right)\COM{\gamma^\mu}{\uW}
    -
    \nonumber \\& 
    \frac{1}{2}
    \left(c
    \PD{}{x^\nu}
     +
     q\PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}\PD{}{p_\nu}
     \right)	
     \COM{\gamma^\mu}{\uW}_+ -
     \nonumber \\
     &
     \frac{\ui \hbar}{8}
     \left(
     q
     \HPD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}{2}
     \HPD{}{p_\nu}{2}
     \right)
     \COM{\gamma^\mu}{\uW}_+     +\mathcal{O}(\hbar^2)=0
\end{align}
so we have
\begin{align}
& \left(c\,p_\mu-q A_\mu(x)\right)\COM{\gamma^\mu}{\uW}
    \nonumber 
    \\ &
    - 
    \frac{\ui \hbar}{2}
    \left(
    c\,\PD{}{x^\nu}
     +
     q\PD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}\PD{}{p_\nu}
     \right)	
     \COM{\gamma^\mu}{\uW}_+ 
     \nonumber 
     \\
     &
     +\frac{\hbar^2}{8}
     \left(
     q\,
     \HPD{A_\mu}{x^\nu}{2}
     \HPD{}{p_\nu}{2}
     \right)
     \COM{\gamma^\mu}{\uW}_+ 
     \nonumber 
     \\
     & +\mathcal{O}(\hbar^3)
     =0
\end{align}
Note that in quantum mechanics higher momentum derivatives of $\uW$ enter the equation. but importantly it remains first order in time.

To save unnecessary duplication of material we will discuss $\aK$ only as a specific example.

\subsection{Specific example: the Landau gauge ($\uK$)}
We will set $A=(0,0,Bx,0)^T$ that equates to a constant magnetic field, $B$ in the $z$ direction. 
The left stargenvalue equation is
\begin{align*}
&\left[{c\gamma^\mu p_\mu-q\gamma^2 Bx-mc^2 1_{4}}\right]{\uW}
\\
&\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ +\frac{\ui \hbar}{2}
\left(
-qB\gamma^2\PD{\uW}{p_2}- c\gamma^\nu\PD{\uW}{x^\nu}\right)
=\varepsilon \uW.
\end{align*}
The equation of motion will then be
\begin{align}   
c\,  \PD {} {x^\nu} \COM{	\gamma^\nu }{\uW}_+ \nonumber
     %\\
  -\ui \frac{qB}{\hbar}   x\COM{\gamma^2}{\uW}
-    \frac{c}{2}\,\PD{}{p_y}  \COM{\gamma^2}{\uW}_+       
\nonumber =0.
\end{align}

\subsection{Specific example: the Landau gauge ($\aK$)}
The left stargenvalue equation is now
\begin{align*}
&\left[{c\gamma^0\gamma^\mu p_\mu-{q\alpha^2} Bx-mc^2 \gamma^0}\right]{\uW}
\\
&\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ +\frac{\ui \hbar}{2}
\left(
-{qB\alpha^2}\PD{\uW}{p_2}- c\gamma^0\gamma^\nu\PD{\uW}{x^\nu}\right)
%\right)
=\varepsilon \uW.
\end{align*}
The equation of motion will then be
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\ui\hbar}\left(c\, p_i \COM{\alpha^i}{W}
-qBx\COM{\alpha^i}{W}
-mc^2\COM{\gamma^0}{W}\right)
\ \  \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ &
\nonumber
\\
-\frac{1}{2}\left(
c \PD{}{x^\nu}\COM{\gamma^0\gamma^\nu}{W}_+
+qB\alpha^2 \PD{}{p_2}\COM{\alpha^2}{W}_+ 
\right)=0.\nonumber &
\end{align}
or
\begin{align}
 \PD{}{t} W
=&
\frac{1}{\ui \hbar}\left(
c\, p_i \COM{\alpha^i}{W}
-qBx\COM{\alpha^2}{W}
-mc^2\COM{\gamma^0}{W}\right)
\nonumber
\\
&-\frac{1}{2}\left(
c \PD{}{x^i}\COM{\alpha^i}{W}_+
+qB\alpha^2 \PD{}{p_2}\COM{\alpha^2}{W}_+ 
\right).\nonumber 
\end{align}
