\subsection{The Bayesian invention}

There have been debates on whether scientific tools such as logic, mathematics, and statistics belong to science. This is particularly relevant because the proposed TB framework we consider here is based on empirical science. Our simple solution is to
consider the content for which the statistical method is designed. To be specific, consider the scientific problem of \cite{bayes1763essay} from an experiment consisting of $n$ independent and identically distributed Bernoulli trials with the probability of success $\theta \in [0,1]$. From this experiment, we have observed $X$ successes, $X\leq n$. We want to update our knowledge on $\theta$ or the probability calculations of future such Bernoulli events, assuming the unknown probability of success $\theta$ follows the standard uniform distribution $\mbox{Uniform}(0,1)$ {\it a priori}. %\addText{\footnote{Three issues: Updating, Finite-sample result, and large-sample result.}}
The result is the Bayes theorem: given $X$, $\theta$ has the posterior distribution
\[
\pi(\theta|X) = \frac{\pi(\theta)f_\theta(X)}{\int_0^1 \pi(\theta)f_\theta(X) d\theta},
\]
where $\pi(\theta)$ is the probability density function of the prior distribution $\mbox{Uniform}(0,1)$, $\pi(\theta)=1$ for all $\theta \in [0,1]$, and $f_\theta(X)$ is the probability mass function of $X$, $\theta^X(1-\theta)^{n-X}.$ 

Bayes' theorem and Bayesian inference developed over the last 260 years, including the work of Pierr-Simon Laplace \citep[see, {\it e.g.}][]{jaynes_probability_2003,stigler1986laplace} and neo-Bayesian revival \citep[see, {\it e.g.}][]{fienberg2006did}, have profoundly shaped mathematics, statistics, and numerous other disciplines and, thereby,  gone through a careful process of {\it TB-\articulation} since their inception or {\it creation}. %Many creative and impactful developments have extended and applied Bayesian thinking in innovative ways. Bayesian inference has evolved from theoretical concept to a versatile framework applied across disciplines. Its creative developments --- ranging from computational breakthroughs like MCMC to applications in AI, cognitive science, and quantum technologies --- demonstrate its adaptability and far-reaching impact. 
Nevertheless, while Bayesian remains as a popular school of thought, the dominant school of thought in scientific inference is frequentist, which started to surface a century ago. Here, we take a critical look the Bayesian theory from a perspective of {TB-evaluation}.

%It is arguably a simple task to evaluate the updating mechanism alone because we understand that the Bayes theorem is a mathematical consequence, for example, in the modern probability theory, and that when applications are in the large sample context, sensible working prior distributions have no significant effect. Our TB evaluation thus focuses on finite-sample situations, which become important in modern high-dimensional inference problems \citep[see, {\it e.g.}][and references therein]{jiang2024finite}.

Perhaps, the most acceptable evaluation scheme is to evaluate the predicted future observations from the Bayes model against the outcome that will be actually observed. Mathematically, this ultimately reduces to the {\it frequentist evaluation} of the posterior distribution when a specific prior distribution $\pi(\theta)$ is used, even though the data analyst has no prior knowledge about the value of $\theta$ {\it a priori}. The failure of the Bayes theory on frequency evaluation (TB-evaluation), especially on constrained parameter and multi-parameter problems, inspired paradigm shift research for alternative ways of scientific inference. %This is discussed further in Section \ref{ss:fiducial}.

