\subsection{The Dempster-Shafer discovery}\label{ss:DS}

As (the creation of) both a successor fiducial and a generalization of Bayes (%a personal conversation with A. P. Dempster, 2008; 
see \cite{dempster2008dempster}), the Dempster-Shafer theory builds upon Dempster's discovery of the need of using upper and lower probabilities for inference and Shafer's development of a broader framework \cite{shafer1976mathematical} to define and manipulate belief functions. Its dual notions of belief and plausibility offer a nuanced way to assess confidence in propositions when full probabilistic information is unavailable. %\addText{Viewed as TB-evaluation,} the most recent work on Dempster-Shafer theory includes \cite{martin2010dempster} and \cite{zhang2011dempster}. 

Here, from the TB perspective, consider the running example of inference with a binomial count. A natural way of extending Fisher's fiducial approach to continuous examples to the discrete cases would lead to considering the set-valued mapping \eqref{eq:binomial-ims}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:DS-set-value}
\Theta_{x}(u) \equiv \{\theta: \; \mbox{Beta}^{-1}_{x, n-x+1}(1-u)
 < \theta \leq \mbox{Beta}^{-1}_{x+1, n-x}(1-u)\}.
\end{equation}
Using a predictive interval for the unobserved auxiliary variable $U$, for example,
\[\mathcal{U}_{\alpha} = \left[\frac{\alpha}{2}, 1-\frac{\alpha}{2}\right],\qquad \alpha \in(0,1),\]
we can construct a $(1-\alpha)100\%$ frequentist confidence interval
\[
\Theta_{x}(\alpha) = \cup_{u\in \mathcal{U}_{\alpha}}\Theta_{x}(u) = \left[\mbox{Beta}^{-1}_{x, n-x+1}(\alpha/2),\; \mbox{Beta}^{-1}_{x+1, n-x}(1-\alpha/2)\right]
\]
for $\theta$, which corresponds to the method of \cite{pearson1920fundamental}. Interestingly, this supports the perception that frequentist ideas are ``in the air'' when R. A. Fisher became what we now consider as frequentist. 

In addition to the discovery of the necessity of using set-valued inverse mapping and, thereby, lower-and-upper or imprecise probabilities, other innovations of the Dempster-Shafer theory include combining information, Dempster's rule of combination, and the mathematical theory of evidence \citep{shafer1976mathematical}. From the TB perspective, everything seems to have come together to form a satisfactory logic of science, except for the frequency evaluation that the majority of scientists apparently considered important logically by the nature of science. Viewed as TB-evaluation, this is discussed in depth in \cite{martin2010dempster} and \cite{zhang2011dempster}, which eventually lead to the work discussed next in Section \ref{ss:ims}. For an extensive review of the Dempster-Shafer theory and more discussion of its frequency properties, see \cite{yager2008classic}, \cite{liu2015frameworks}, \cite{denzux201640}, and \cite{denoeux2018frequency}.

