As a computational procedure, IMs consists of three steps, association, prediction, and combination. The association step represents the sampling model by \eqref{eq:binomial-ims}, which
associates the observed data $X$, the unknown parameter $\theta$, and the auxiliary variable $U$ that is missing but predictable. The prediction step predicts the missing value $U$ using a so-called valid predictive random set $\mathcal{S}$, resulting in \textit{epistemic probabilities}. For example, for a general purpose inference, we can take 
\[
\mathcal{S} = [U/2, 1-U/2],\qquad U\sim \mbox{Uniform}(0,1),
\]
which is valid \citep{martin2013inferential}. %\footnote{Shall we elaborate the math detail a bit here?}. 
The combination step combines  the predictive random set $\mathcal{S}$ and the observed data to push $\mathcal{S}$ into a random set on $\Theta$ based on the association \eqref{eq:binomial-ims}:
\[
\Theta_{X}(\mathcal{S}) = \cup_{u\in \mathcal{S}}\Theta_X(u),
\]
where $\Theta_X(u)$ is defined in \eqref{eq:DS-set-value}.
For any assertion or hypothesis of interest $A$, this IM model produces the lower probability called {\it belief}
\[
\mbox{Bel}_X(A) = \mbox{Prob}(\Theta_{X}(\mathcal{S}) \subseteq A),
\]
the probability of the truth of $A$,
and the upper probability called {\it plausibility}
\[
\mbox{Pl}_X(A) = 1-\mbox{Prob}(\Theta_{X}(\mathcal{S}) \subseteq A^c),
\]
the plausibility for the truth of $A$. For example, for all $\theta\in [0,1]$, we have
\begin{eqnarray}
\mbox{Pl}_X(\{\theta\}) &=& 1-\mbox{Prob}(\Theta_{X}(\mathcal{S}) \subseteq \{\xi: 0\leq \xi\leq 1, \xi\neq \theta\}) \nonumber\\
&=& \left\{\begin{array}{lcl}
2\mbox{Beta}(\theta, X, n-X+1)),&&\mbox{if $\theta < \mbox{Beta}^{-1}(0.5, X, n-X+1)$;}\\
2(1-\mbox{Beta}(\theta, X+1, n-X)),&&\mbox{if $\theta > \mbox{Beta}^{-1}(0.5, X+1, n-X)$;}\\
1, && \mbox{otherwise.}
\end{array}
\right.
\label{eq:pl-binom}
\end{eqnarray}
