Reflecting on the advances in developing inferential methods for the logic of scientific reasoning, as we did in the last section under the term 'inferology,' we find that by the 1950s, Neyman-Pearson's frequentist framework—comprising confidence intervals for inference and the reject-or-fail-to-reject approach for hypothesis testing—was widely accepted in scientific practice. The critical techniques of maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) and the associated large-sample theory-based approximation were developed in a manner that might be described, as \cite{kuhn1970structure} calls it, as 'normal science.' In this context, the less-than-ideal finite-sample performance of MLE was recognized within the research community. This led to  weakened beliefs in Fisher's theory of MLE, and thus the problem to be solved was clearly established. From a scientific creativity perspective, as far as the authors can trace, this issue has since undergone several exploratory and transformational shifts. We outline below the key reactivities within the proposed weak belief framework for analyzing scientific reactivities, spanning developments from the {\it Jackknife} to the {\it Bootstrap} and to the most recent inquiries into {\it finite-sample exact inference}.




