\subsubsection{Jackknife}

Jackknife marks the transformational change of considering what has been nowadays understood as cross-validation analysis by \cite{quenouille1949approximate,quenouille1956notes}
and the exploratory advance to considering variance estimations as well about a decade later by \cite{tukey1958bias}; See \cite{shao2012jackknife}, \cite{lahiri2013resampling}, and references therein for more discussion.
Suppose we have a sample $x=(x_1, ..., x_n)$ and an estimator $\hat{\theta} = s(x)$. The jackknife
focuses on the samples that {\it leave out one observation at a time}:
\[x_{(i)}=(x_1,...,x_{(i-1)},x_{(i+1)},...,x_n)\]
for $i=1,..., n$, called jackknife samples. The $i$-th jackknife sample consists of the data set
with the i$i$-h observation removed. Let $\hat{\theta}_{(i)}=s(x_{(i)})$ be the $i$-th jackknife replication of $\hat{\theta}$.
The jackknife estimate of sqaured standard error is defined as the sample variance of the jackknife replicates adjusted by the factor of $(n-1)$.


The jackknife resampling method proved effective primarily for linear statistics, such as the mean, but encountered limitations when applied to non-smooth statistics (e.g., the median) or nonlinear statistics (e.g., the correlation coefficient). The jackknife's accuracy issues with these cases stemmed from inconsistencies between predicted and observed values when applied to estimands beyond the mean. These limitations prompted the development of improved techniques, aimed at extending resampling accuracy across a broader range of statistical measures.


\subsubsection{Bootstrap}


To improve the performance of jackknife in the realm of statistical inference and prediction,
bootstrap methods are discovered to estimate the sample distribution of the statistic of interest by resampling the observed data. Since the seminal paper of \cite{efron1979bootstrap}, %\citep[see also][]{efron2003second}, 
these methods have made the paradigm shit in the field of resampling methods \citep{efron1993introduction,shao2012jackknife,lahiri2013resampling}.
% often served as an indispensable tool in the realm of statistical inference and prediction, due to their simplicity and efficiency \cite[see, {\it e.g.}][]{efron1993introduction}. 
In addition to the development of bootstrap itself, it has soon motivated relevant research in other contexts, including its Bayesian counterpart \citep[see][and references therein]{rubin1981bayesian,efron1982jackknife,newton1994approximate,efron2012bayesian,newton2021weighted}.

Approximation methods often lack theoretical support for finite-sample problems, and this popular bootstrap is no exception. Theoretically, bootstrap methods primarily focus on asymptotic validity \citep[see, {\it e.g.},][ and references therein]{bickel1981some,singh1981asymptotic, wellner1996bootstrapping,vanderVaart1996weak,kosorok2008introduction,efron1993introduction}. However, even within large-sample contexts, the bootstrap can fail \citep[see, {\it e.g.,}][and references therein]{bickel1983bootstrapping,abrevaya2005bootstrap, chernozhukov2023high}. For instance, in high-dimensional linear regression where $n \to \infty$ and $p/n \to c > 0$ for $p$ predictors,  %n→∞n→∞ and p/n→c>0p/n→c>0 for pp predictors, 
the bootstrap has been found to yield inconsistent estimates of the true sampling distribution of regression coefficients, with poor numerical inference results for the vector of coefficients $\beta$  \citep{el2018can}. Most importantly, these works offer warnings about the application of bootstrap in high-dimensional settings. From the perspective of our \weak~belief framework, these {\it thought experiments} provide evidence as a way of verification, leading to further investigations.

To address limitations of the standard $n$-out-of-$n$ bootstrap method in certain cases, researchers have explored alternative resampling methods, such as $m$-out-of-$n$ resampling with or without replacement (known as subset sampling), originally suggested by \cite{bretagnolle1983lois} and further developed in works like \cite{politis1994large, bickel2008choice, bickel2012resampling}. Despite these efforts and their asymptotic backing, a significant gap remains in developing theoretically supported 
bootstrap methods for high-dimensional inference problems.



\subsubsection{Calibrated bootstrap}


Most recently, revolving the problem of bootstrap inavoidably demands finite-sample valid inference methods using resampling.
To achieve reliable finite-sample performance, \cite{jiang2024finite} proposes an efficient resampling-based strategy to closely approximate the theoretical solution. Their innovation lies in recognizing that resampling variability and inferential uncertainty are distinct concepts and that they consider an approach aiming to align resampling variability with inferential uncertainty. Using an inferential models (IMs) perspective, rooted in the pivotal method, they numerically assess uncertainty via loss functions in parameter estimation, applying cumulative distributions to introduce pivotal quantities. This leads to the method of calibrated bootstrap, a resampling method that matches the theoretical distribution for valid inference, making it suitable for high-dimensional cases where standard bootstrap methods are known to falter.

\footnote{See \cite{cella2024variational} etc?}
With the rapid advancements in statistical inferential methods driven by the needs of artificial intelligence, we may be on the cusp of a significant paradigm shift: the emergence of finite-sample exact inference. Unlike traditional inference, which often relies on asymptotic approximations that may fail in high-dimensional or complex models, finite-sample exact inference aims to provide rigorous, reliable results even with limited data. Such a paradigm could reshape not only AI and machine learning but also fields such as genomics, finance, and healthcare, where accurate, model-consistent inference from small or complex data sets is essential. In this context, developing computationally feasible methods with strong theoretical foundations becomes increasingly critical, laying the groundwork for a future where inference is both precise and robust, irrespective of sample size.
