\item[(c)] $\tilde{p}_{144}\tilde{p}_{414}\tilde{p}_{441}-\tilde{p}_{111}\tilde{p}_{444}^2=0.$ 
\end{enumerate}
\end{prop}


\begin{proof}
\begin{comment}
We know that $\bar{p}_{ijk}=\lambda^1_i\lambda^2_j\lambda^3_k \pp_{ijk}.$ For a general point on $CV_T$ all eigenvalues are different from zero, implying that $\bar{p}_{ijk}=0$ vanishes on $CV_T$ if and only if $\pp_{ijk}=0.$ For the indices $ijk$ for which $\pp_{ijk}\neq 0$, we have 
\[\tilde{p}_{ijk}=\frac{\bar{p}_{ijk}}{\pp_{ijk}}=\lambda^1_i\lambda^2_j\lambda^3_k.\]

As $\lambda^l_{2}=\lambda^l_{3}=\lambda^l_{4}$ for any $l$, we obtain the desired equalities.
\end{comment}

By \Cref{tilde_rho}, we know that $\tilde{p}_{ijk}=\lambda^{e_1}_i\lambda^{e_2}_j\lambda^{e_3}_k$ for the indices listed in \eqref{eq:tripod_rho} and zero otherwise. The desired equalities follow from the fact that $\lambda^e_{2}=\lambda^e_{3}=\lambda^e_{4}$ for any $e\in E$.

From these equalities, we consider the variety $CV_T$ for the tripod to be living in the affine space 
$\mathbb{C}^5=\mathbb{C}\langle\tilde{p}_{111},\tilde{p}_{122},\tilde{p}_{212},\tilde{p}_{221},\tilde{p}_{222}\rangle$ (cut out by the linear equations above). 
By \Cref{prop:dim}, the dimension of $CV_T$ is $4$, and we need to check that equation (c) holds. It is straightforward to verify that the subtracted terms are equal to $\lambda^{e_1}_1\lambda^{e_2}_1\lambda^{e_3}_1(\lambda^{e_1}_4\lambda^{e_2}_4\lambda^{e_3}_4)^2.$ %This is easy to see because the subtracted terms are equal to $\lambda^{e_1}_1\lambda^{e_2}_1\lambda^{e_3}_1(\lambda^{e_1}_4\lambda^{e_2}_4\lambda^{e_3}_4)^2.$
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
    \rm Note the analogy of the F81 model with the Jukes-Cantor model on the tripod: as proven in \cite[\S 4.5]{ASCB2005} the variety for the Jukes-Cantor model is also a cubic hypersurface inside its ambient space (and, in Fourier coordinates, the cubic equation coincides with the one given above).
\end{remark}

