\subsubsection{Rule Dependency}
Due to the properties of the ECA rule space, that space also includes rules that are invariant to one or more of their neighbours, therefore they strictly do not compute on 3 neighbours. Essentially, they do not differentiate on all the cells in the neighbourhood; e.g. rule 170 only differentiates on the left neighbour and is indifferent to the values of the right and central neighbour, or similarly, rule 90 only differentiates on the left and right neighbour, but not the central neighbour. This is called the rule dependency, and the 3 neighbour rules can be of either 0,1,2 or 3-dependency. An overview of the ME can be found in Table \ref{tab:dependancyECA} and a complete overview in \citep{wolframAtlasDependencies, wolfram2018Tables}. 

