{\bf \punct The number of elements of $\boldsymbol{\Pi_\alpha}$. Formula (\ref{eq:number-Pi}).} The group $S_\alpha\times S_\alpha$
acts on $\Pi_\alpha$ by left and right multiplications, 
$X\mapsto g_1 X g_2^{-1}$. 
Orbits are enumerated by the rank of a matrix. Summands of
 \eqref{eq:number-Pi} are numbers of elements in such orbits.

Indeed, consider the matrix 
$T_{1,\dots,r}\in\Pi_\alpha$, see \eqref{eq:idempotent}.
Its stabilizer in $S_\alpha\times S_\alpha$ consists of pairs
$(g_1,g_2)=(h p_1, h^{-1} p_2)$, where $h$ is an element
of $S_{\alpha-r}$
acting on $\{r+1,\dots, \alpha\}$,
and $p_1$, $p_2$ are  elemens of $S_r$ acting on $\{1,\dots,r\}$.
So, the stabilizer is $S_{\alpha-r}\times S_r\times S_r$.







\sm

{\bf \punct The number of elements of a double coset.
  Formula (\ref{eq:preimage}).%
\label{ss:preimage}}
Without loss of generality, we can take
$$\sigma=T_{1,2, \dots,r}.$$
We must find the number of ways to include $T_{1,2, \dots,r}$ to a matrix-permutation $U\in S_{\alpha+n}$ such that
$[U]_\alpha=\sigma$.
So, we must choose units in $1$, \dots, $r$-th rows,
say at entries $(1,\alpha+i_1)$, \dots, $(r, \alpha+i_r)$.
This can be done in
$$n(n-1)\dots(n-r+1)$$
 ways. We also must choose units in $1$, \dots, $r$-th columns,
say at entries $(\alpha+j_1,1)$, \dots, $(\alpha+ j_r,1)$.
This also can be done in
$n(n-1)\dots(n-r+1)$
 ways.


 Now we have
  units in columns $1$, \dots, $\alpha$, $\alpha+i_1$, \dots, 
  $\alpha+i_r$ and in rows $1$, \dots, $\alpha$, $\alpha+j_1$,
  \dots, $\alpha+j_r$.
  Crossing out these columns and these rows, we get
  a matrix of size $(n-r)\times(n-r)$. 
  We can choose units in $(n-r)!$ ways. So, we get \eqref{eq:preimage}.

\begin{corollary} Denote by $\frt(i_1,\dots,i_k)$ the double
coset $S_nT_{i_1,\dots,i_k}S_n$. Then 
$$\iota(\delta_{\frt(i_1,\dots,i_k)})=n(n-1)\dots(n-r+1).$$
\end{corollary}

In particular, we know values of the homomorphism 
$\iota:\C[S_n\setminus S_{\alpha+n}/S_n]\to\C$ on the generators:
\begin{equation}
\iota(\theta_j)=n,\qquad \iota(A(g))=1.
\label{eq:iota-O}
\end{equation}

