{\bf \punct Relations in $\boldsymbol{\bbO[\alpha;n]}$.
Proof of Theorem 2.3.b.}
Here we prove Theorem \ref{th:homomorphism}.b, i.e., we verify the
relations \eqref{eq:rel1}--\eqref{eq:rel5} for $a(g)$ and $\theta_j$.

\sm

{\it A verification of \eqref{eq:rel1}--\eqref{eq:rel2}} is trivial.

\sm

{\it Relation \eqref{eq:rel4}}. %Denote by $T_i\in \Pi_\alpha$
%the diagonal matrix of order $\alpha$, whose $i$-th entry is zero
%and other entries on the diagonal are 1.
 The double coset $S_n\bigl(i(\alpha+1)\bigr)S_n$ consists of all 
 $Q\in S_{\alpha+n}$
 such that $[Q]_\alpha=T_i$. Our $\theta_i$ is a uniform
 measure on this set of  total mass $n$. Note, that  any element
  $\gamma\in S_n\bigl(i(\alpha+1)\bigr)S_n$ sends $m\mapsto m$
for all $m\le \alpha$, $m\ne i$. Therefore $\theta_i^2$
has the form
$$
\theta_i^2=s\, \theta_i+t,
$$ 
where $s$, $t$ are nonnegative constants.
Applying the homomorphism $\iota$ and keeping in mind \eqref{eq:iota-O},
we come to 
$$
n^2=sn+t.
$$ 
Let $P$, $Q\in S_n\bigl(i(\alpha+1)\bigr)S_n$. Then $Q$ sends $i$ to some
$\alpha+l$. The element $P^{-1}$ sends $i$ to some $\alpha+m$.
So $PQ$ sends $i\mapsto i$ if $\alpha+l=\alpha+m$. This happens
with probability $1/n$. So, 
$$
\frac 1n\, n^2=t. 
$$  
With probability $(n-1)/n$, we have  $\alpha+l\ne\alpha+m$.
Therefore,
$$
\frac{n-1}{n}\,n^2=n\,s.
$$

{\it Relation \eqref{eq:rel4}}. Consider the double coset 
$S_nT_{ij}S_n$ and the function 
$$
\theta_{ij}:=\frac{1}{n!}\sum_{g\in S_nT_{ij}S_n}\delta_g,
$$ 
so  $\iota(\theta_{ij})=n(n-1)$.
A product $\theta_j\theta_i$ has the form
\begin{equation}
\theta_j\theta_i=s\,\theta_{ij}+t\,A((ij))\theta_j
\label{eq:theta-theta}
\end{equation}
Indeed, let $P\in S_n\bigl(i(\alpha+1)\bigr)S_n$, 
$Q\in S_n\bigl(j(\alpha+1)\bigr)S_n$.
Then $Q$ sends $j$ to $j$, and $P$ sends $j$ to some $\alpha+k$.
So $[PQ]_\alpha$ has zero in the $j$-th row. Also, $Q$ sends $i$ to
some $\alpha+k$, and $P^{-1}$ sends $j$ to $\alpha+m$.
If $\alpha+m=\alpha+k$, then $[PQ]_\alpha$ sends $i\mapsto j$,
and this corresponds to the summand $tA((ij))\theta_j$.
Otherwise, $PQ$ sends $i$ to some $\alpha+h$, and this corresponds to 
the summand $s\theta_{ij}$. So, we come to \eqref{eq:theta-theta}.

Applying $\iota$, we come to
$$
n^2=s\, n(n-1)+ t\,n.
$$
The probability of the event $\alpha+m=\alpha+k$ is $1/n$.
So 
$$
\frac 1n n^2=tn,
$$
i.e., $t=1$, therefore $s=1$, i.e.
\begin{align}
\theta_j \theta_i=\theta_{ij}+A((ij))\theta_j;
\label{eq:theta-theta-theta}
\\
\theta_i \theta_j=\theta_{ij}+A((ij))\theta_i.
\nonumber
\end{align}
Subtracting these identities, we come to \eqref{eq:rel3}.

