{\bf \punct The proof of Lemma \ref{l:A-1}.}
Now we consider an arbitrary algebra $\bbO[\alpha;\nu]$. 
We start with a few simple observations on $\Theta$-degree.
Clearly, for  any permutation $\sigma$ of $i_1$, \dots, $i_k$
we have
$$
\deg_\Theta 
\Bigl( \Theta_{\sigma(i_1)}\dots \Theta_{\sigma(i_k)}
- \Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_k}\Bigr)<k.
$$
Next, for $g\in S_\alpha$ we have
$$
\deg_\Theta 
\Bigl( \Theta_{g(i_1)}\dots \Theta_{g(i_k)}\, A(g)
- A(g) \,\Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_k} \Bigr)<k.
$$
Representing the relation \eqref{eq:rel5}
in the form
\begin{equation*}
A((ij))\, \Theta_j\Theta_i-\Theta_j\Theta_i=\Theta_j- A((ij))\Theta_j,
\end{equation*}
we see
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:erase}
\deg_\Theta 
\Bigl( A((ij))\, \Theta_j\Theta_i-\Theta_j\Theta_i\Bigr)=1.
\end{equation}

Decompose our $h$ in a product of transpositions of our elements
$i_1$, \dots, $i_k$, say
$$h=(i_{p_1}i_{q_1})\dots (i_{p_m}i_{q_m}).$$
So,
$$
A(h)\, \Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_k}= 
A\bigl((i_{p_1}i_{q_1})\bigr)\dots A\bigl((i_{p_m}i_{q_m})\bigr)\,
\Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_k}.
$$
We move $\Theta_{i_{p_m}}$ to the first position, and 
$\Theta_{i_{q_m}}$ the second position.
Using \eqref{eq:erase}, 
%$$
%\deg_\Theta\Bigl(A\bigl((i_{p_m}i_{q_m})\bigr) \Theta_{i_{p_m}}\Theta_{i_{q_m}}
%-\Theta_{i_{p_m}}\Theta_{i_{q_m}}\Bigr)
%<2.
%$$
 we erase $A\bigl((i_{p_m}i_{q_m})\bigr)$; afterwards we return $\Theta_{i_{p_m}}$ and 
$\Theta_{i_{q_m}}$ to the initial positions. We get
\begin{multline*}
\deg_\Theta\Bigl(
A\bigl((i_{p_1}i_{q_1})\bigr)\dots A\bigl((i_{p_m}i_{q_m})\bigr)-
\\-
A\bigl((i_{p_1}i_{q_1})\bigr)\dots A\bigl((i_{p_{m-1}}i_{q_{m-1}})\bigr)
\Bigr)\,
\Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_k}<k.
\end{multline*}
Repeating the same actions, we come to the desired statement.

