{\bf \punct The upper estimate of the  dimension of
$\boldsymbol{\bbO[\alpha;\nu]}$.} Let $\nu\in \C$.
Denote by $\bbO[\alpha;\nu]_m$ the linear space of all elements
of $\bbO[\alpha;\nu]$ of $\Theta$-degree $\le m$.  By definition,
it is spanned by elements of the form 
$A(g)\,\Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_k}$, where $k\le m$.

\begin{lemma}
For each $m$ the linear subspace $\bbO[\alpha;\nu]_m$
is generated by elements \eqref{eq:basis} with  $k\le m$.
\end{lemma}

{\sc Proof.} Assume that the statement is valid for all
$m\le l-1$. The subspace $\bbO[\alpha;\nu]_l$
is generated by $\bbO[\alpha;\nu]_{l-1}$ and
vectors of the form 
$A(g)\,\Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_l}$.
Let us pass to the quotient 
$$
V_l:=\bbO[\alpha;\nu]_{l}/\bbO[\alpha;\nu]_{l-1}.
$$
The image of a  vector $A(g)\,\Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_l}$
in the quotient does not depend on an order of factors $\Theta$,
so we can assume $i_1<\dots< i_l$.
Let $h\in S_\alpha$ be a permutation supported by
$\{i_1,\dots,i_l\}$. By  Lemma \ref{l:A-1}, the elements
$$
A(gh)\,\Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_l},\quad 
A(g)\,\Theta_{i_1}\dots \Theta_{i_l}\in V_l
$$
coincide as elements of the quotient $V_l$.
So we can assume that  $g(i_1)<\dots< g(i_l)$,
and this implies our statement.
\hfill $\square$

