{\bf \punct Surjectivity of the  homomorphism
$\boldsymbol{\bbO[\alpha; n]\to \C[S_n\backslash S_{\alpha+n}/S_n]}$
for $\boldsymbol{n\ge \alpha}$.}
We verify the statement in the following form:

 \begin{lemma}
 For each $r\ge 0$ the image of $\bbO[\alpha; n]_r$
 consists of all elements of $\C[S_n\backslash S_{\alpha+n}/S_n]$
supported by the set of all $Q\in S_{\alpha+n}$
 such that $\rk [Q]_\alpha\ge\alpha-r$.
 \end{lemma}

 {\sc Proof.}
 We prove the statement by induction. Assume that for $r<m-1$
 the statement is valid. Consider a product
 $\theta_{i_1}\dots \theta_{i_m}$, where $i_1<\dots<i_m$,
 i.e., 
 $$
 \delta_{S_n T_{i_1} S_n}\cdot \delta_{S_n T_{i_2} S_n}
 \cdot\vphantom{.}\dots\vphantom{.} \cdot
  \delta_{S_n T_{i_m} S_n}.
 $$ 
 If $m\le n$, then the support of this product contains
 the matrix $T_{i_1\dots i_m}$, the remaining points of the support
are matrices $Q\in S_{\alpha+m}$
 with $[Q]_\alpha<m-1$. By the induction
assumption, delta-functions of all such remaning double cosets
are contained in the image of $\bbO[\alpha; n]_{m-1}$.
Therefore, the delta-function of $S_n T_{i_1\dots i_m} S_n$
is contained in the image of $\bbO[\alpha; n]_{m}$.

Therefore, for any $g_1$, $g_2\in S_\alpha$
delta-functions of $S_n g_1 T_{i_1\dots i_m}g_2 S_n$
are contained in the image of $\bbO[\alpha; n]_{m}$.
But such cosets contain all matrices $Q\in S_{\alpha+m}$
 with $\rk [Q]_\alpha=\alpha-m$.
\hfill $\square$

\begin{corollary}
\label{cor:dim-ravno}
If integer $n$ is $\ge \alpha$, then $\dim \bbO[\alpha;n]= \#\Pi_\alpha$.
So, elements \eqref{eq:basis} form a basis in
$\bbO[\alpha;n]$
\end{corollary}

Indeed, by the surjectivity, we have $\ge$. By Corollary
\ref{cor:dim-less}, we have $=$.
\hfill $\square$

\sm

{\bf \punct The end of the proofs of Theorems \ref{th:homomorphism}, \ref{th:basis}.} We need some preliminaries.
Consider a space $\C^M$ with a fixed basis $e_j$.
Consider a structure of an associative algebra on $\C^M$,
$$
e_p e_q= \sum c^s_{pq} e_s.
$$
Such product is associative if the {\it structure constants} 
$c^s_{pq}$ satisfy the following collection of $M^4$
 quadratic equations:
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i} c_{pq}^i c_{ir}^t=\sum_j c_{pj}^tc_{qr}^j.
\label{eq:ass}
\end{equation}
So the set $\Assoc(M)$  of all structures  
of associative algebras on $\C^M$
is an algebraic variety. It has many irreducible components
of different dimensions (see, e.g., \cite{Ger},
and  \cite{Maz}, \cite{Ner-Lie}). Also, the group $\GL(M,\C)$ acts on 
$\Assoc(M)$ by changes of bases.

 Consider a direct sum
of several matrix algebras, say, 
$$\cE(\{l_j\}):=\bigoplus_{j=1}^\tau \End(\C^{l_j}),$$
 such that
$\sum_{j=1}^\tau l_j^2=M$. 
 Consider the
subset $\cO(\{l_j\})$ in  $\mathrm{Assoc}(M)$ consisting of algebras
isomorphic to  $\cE(\{l_j\})$,
i.e., the $\GL(M,\C)$-orbit of $\cE(\{l_j\})$.
Clearly, 
\begin{equation}
\dim \cO(\{l_j\})=M^2-M+\tau.
\label{eq:MM}
\end{equation}
 Indeed, the stabilizer is the group of automorphsism of 
 $\cE(\{l_j\})$. Its connected component%
 \footnote{The group of automorphisms also contains permutations
 of factors if some $l_j$ are equal.}
 is the product of the groups of automorphisms of the summands,
 i.e., $\prod_j (\GL(l_j,\C)/\C^\times)$, where $\C^\times$ is
 the multiplicative group of $\C$ (embedded to $\GL(l_j,\C)$
 as scalar matrices). So, the dimension is $\sum (l_j^2-1)=M-\tau$.


 It  is well-known%
 \footnote{We say, that a {\it deformation} of an algebra
 $\cA$ is a holomorphic curve $\cA(\epsilon)\subset \Assoc(M)$
 defined for small $\epsilon$
and satisfying $\cA(0)=\cA$. It is easy to verify, that if there is a sequence
 $\epsilon_k\to 0$ such that $\cA(\epsilon_k)$ have a nil-radical of dimension $l$, then the nil-radical of $\cA$ has dimension $\ge l$. So,
 deformations of semisimple algebras are semisimple. If for some 
 $\epsilon_k\to 0$ dimensions of the centers of $\cA(\epsilon_k)$
 are $p$, then the dimension of the center of $\cA$ is $\ge p$.
 If $\cA(0)\in \cO(\{l_j\})$ and the curve does not contained in this family, it must be contained in a family $\cO(\{l'_i\})$
 of larger dimension. By \eqref{eq:MM}, this requires a growth of a center, and we come to a contradiction.} that  closures
$\ov{\cO(\{l_j\})}\subset \Assoc(M)$ of 
$\cO(\{l_j\})$ are irreducible components of the variety $\Assoc(M)$.









{\sc The end of the proof of Theorem \ref{th:basis}.}
Now,  let $n\ge \alpha$ be integer.
Let  $e_1$, $e_2$, $\dots\vphantom{.}\in \bbO[\alpha;n]$ be our basis 
\eqref{eq:basis}.
It is clear, that the structure constants $c^r_{pq}(n)$
are polynomial expressions in $n$.
Since  the conditions \eqref{eq:ass} are valid for integer $n\ge\alpha$, this remains to be valid
if replace $n$ by an arbitrary $\nu\in\C$.
Denote this algebra by $\bbO'[\alpha;\nu]$.

Relations \eqref{eq:rel1}--\eqref{eq:rel5} between  basis elements $A(g)$, $\Theta_j$
in $\bbO'[\alpha;\nu]$ is collection of polynomial equations
on the structure constants $c^r_{pq}(\nu)$. They are valid
for integer $\nu\ge\alpha$, so they are valid for all $\nu\in\C$. 
Therefore,
 we have a homomorphism
$$\bbO[\alpha;\nu]\to \bbO'[\alpha;\nu].$$ 
Its image contains all elements of the basis,
and so it is a surjection. On the other hand,
$\dim \bbO[\alpha;\nu]\le\dim \bbO'[\alpha;\nu]$.
So we have a bijection.
 \hfill $\square$

\sm

{\sc Proof of Theorem \ref{th:homomorphism}.d}.
The map $\nu\mapsto \bbO[\alpha;\nu]$ determines an algebraic curve
in $\Assoc(\#\Pi_\alpha)$.
 Countable number of its points is contained in one component
 of $\Assoc(\#\Pi_\alpha)$, namely $\ov{\cO(\{l^\circ_j\})}$,
 where $l^\circ_j$ is the collection \eqref{eq:multi} . So the whole curve
 curve is contained in $\ov{\cO(\{l_j^\circ\})}$. On the other hand,
 $\ov{\cO(\{l_j^\circ\})}\setminus \cO(\{l_j^\circ\})$ has codimension
 $\ge 1$ in  $\ov{\cO(\{l_j^\circ\})}$. Therefore all but a finite set
 of points of the curve are contained in $\cO(\{l_j^\circ\})$.
  \hfill $\square$

\sm

{\bf\punct Trace. Proof of Proposition \ref{pr:trace}.}
 If $\nu=n\ge \alpha$ is integer, then
 $$
 \Tr H=%\frac{1}
 \tr H,
 $$
where $\tr$ is the trace in $\C[S_{\alpha+n}]$.

Let $X$, $Y$ be elements of our basis of $\bbO[\alpha;\nu]$.
Clearly, $\Tr (XY)$, $\Tr(YX)$ are polynomial expressions
in $\nu$. For integer $\nu\ge \alpha$, we have
$\Tr (XY)=\Tr (YX)$. Therefore, this is valid for all $\nu\in\C$.

\sm

{\bf \punct Positivity. Proof of Proposion \ref{pr:positivity}.}
The positivity of  our form is a finite collection
of polynomial inequalities on $\nu$ (for instance, we can require the positivity of principal minors). These inequalities take place 
for integer $\nu\ge\alpha$,  therefore they are valid
for sufficiently large $\nu$. 

\sm

{\bf \punct The limit algebra $\boldsymbol{\bbO[\alpha]}$.
The proof of Proposition \ref{pr:limit}.}
The semigroup algebra $\C[\Pi_\alpha]$ is semisimple
(a general statement: the semigroup algebra of an inverse semigroup
is semisimple, see \cite{Oga} or \cite{CP}, Theorem 5.26).
Irreducible representations of $\Pi_\alpha$.
Irreducible representations of the semigroup $\Pi_\alpha$
 can be easily described, see, e.g., \cite{Ner-book},
Subsect.~VIII.2.2. They are enumerated by
pairs $(t, \mu)$, where  $t=0$, 1, \dots, $\alpha$
and $\mu$ is a Young diagram of size $\alpha-t$.
The explicit description shows that such representation
is realized in the space of functions on the space
of $(\alpha-t)$-elements subsets in $\{1,\dots,\alpha\}$
taking values in the space of representation $\rho(\alpha-t;\mu)$
of $S_{\alpha-t}$ (we keep the notation of the Subsect. \ref{ss:young}).
So, the dimension of such a representation
is
$$
\frac{\alpha!}{t!\,(\alpha-t)!}\, \dim\rho(\alpha-t;\mu),
$$
and we get the same collection of dimensions \eqref{eq:multi}.



