\subsection{System description}
Consider a closed-loop control system as Fig. \ref{fig:Block diagram cl}, where $G$ is the plant, $C_1$ and $C_2$ are LTI filters and $\mathcal{R}$ is the reset element describes as follows
\begin{equation} 
		\mathcal{R} : \begin{cases}
            \dot{x}_r(t)=A_rx_r(t)+B_re_r(t),  \quad \quad \left(x_r(t),e_r(t)\right)\notin\mathcal{F},\\
            x_r(t^+)=A_\rho x_r(t), \qquad \qquad \qquad\left(x_r(t),e_r(t)\right)\in\mathcal{F},\\
            u_r(t)=C_rx_r(t)+D_re_r(t),
		\end{cases}
  \label{eq.SS reset}
	\end{equation}
 with
 \begin{equation}
    \label{reset surface}
\mathcal{F}:=\{e_r=0\wedge(A_\rho-I)x_r(t)\neq0\},
\end{equation}
where $A_r\in\mathbb{R}^{n_r\, . \, n_r}$, $B_r\in\mathbb{R}^{n_r\, . \, 1}$, $C_r\in\mathbb{R}^{1\, . \, n_r}$, and $D_r\in\mathbb{R}$ represent the state-space matrices of the reset element, and the reset value matrix is denoted by $A_\rho=\text{diag}(\gamma_1,...,\gamma_{n_r})$. $x_r(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{n_r\, . \, 1}$ is the state of the reset element, $x_r(t^{+})\in\mathbb{R}^{n_r\, . \, 1}$ is the after-reset state, and $e_r(t)\in\mathbb{R}$ and $u_r(t)\in\mathbb{R}$ represent the input and output of the reset element, respectively. By defining the base linear system of the reset element as the case when there is no reset, its transfer function is calculated as follows:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq RCS bls}
R(s) = C_r(s - A_r)^{-1}B_r + D_r,
\end{equation}
where $s \in\mathbb{C}$ is the Laplace variable.

