However, this reduction in higher-order harmonics applies specifically to cases without noise in the system. Suppose that in the loop $C_1 = C_{\mathrm{lead}}$. If the error signal $e(t)$ is noisy, then the noise will be amplified in the reset action signal $e_r(t)$, which may disrupt the reset timing and introduce additional errors. Consequently, in many applications, the lead element is positioned after the reset element or in a parallel line to mitigate noise amplification in the reset signal \cite{10473227}. 

In this study, we introduce a tunable lead for the CgLp element, allowing the lead action to be divided into two parts. Based on the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and the higher-order harmonics in the system, the amount of lead can be adjusted before and after the GFORE element in the CgLp like the Fig. \ref{fig: Tunable lead CgLp}. Thus, we set
