\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:into}
Modern software-intensive systems, ranging from cloud microservices~\cite{pujol2023edge,jawaddi2022review} with frequent deployments to self-adaptive systems and autonomous robots that navigate dynamic warehouses~\cite{wurman2007robots,zhao2019probabilistic,fisher2021overview} must reason rigorously about uncertainty~\cite{hezavehi2021uncertainty,weyns2023towards}. Markov decision processes (MDPs) allow capturing both nondeterminism and stochasticity, making them an expressive and powerful mathematical framework for sequential decision-making under uncertainty~\cite{puterman1994mdps}.
In software engineering (SE), MDPs support the continuous verification of self-adaptive systems~\cite{su2016iterative}, dynamic QoS management and optimisation of service-based applications~\cite{calinescu2011qos}, and variability-aware model checking of software product lines~\cite{profeat2016}, and
robotic software stacks for task and motion planning~\cite{survey2023robotics}.

 However, despite the widespread adoption of MDPs to support decision-making in these complex, software-intensive systems, their practical application is often hindered by the so-called state-space explosion problem~\cite{clarke2012explosion}. 
 This is because as the systems modelled grow, the corresponding MDP can expand to an unmanageable size. 
 This explosion in the number of states and transitions renders standard policy synthesis algorithms, which determine optimal actions, computationally prohibitive due to excessive time and memory requirements~\cite{su2016iterative,baier2008principles}.

For engineering teams that rely on MDP-based analysis to ensure safety requirements (e.g., ``the robot never becomes stuck'') or the achievement of performance targets (e.g., ``the task must be completed time within $T$ time steps''), the state-space explosion problem can significantly undermine the adoption of verification techniques for real-world software systems~\cite{kwiatkowska2007stochastic}.
Large models can stall verification pipelines and delay releases. For example, even adjusting a single parameter
(such as shelf layout) might force re-verification of the entire MDP, escalating development costs and turnaround time~\cite{junges2022abstraction}.

Despite significant research, the scalability of MDP solution methods remains a fundamental challenge~\cite{baier201910}. 
Recent benchmark studies~\cite{budde2021qcomp} report that state-of-the-art probabilistic verification tools, such as the widely used probabilistic model checkers PRISM \cite{prism} and Storm~\cite{storm}, still timeout on large MDP benchmarks. 
This holds for various underlying techniques. 
\emph{Symbolic approaches}, which excel in non-probabilistic settings, often fail to scale for MDPs~\cite{alfaro2000symbolic}, while
\emph{explicit state engines} that rely on static partitioning
and secondary storage can require hours of computation as the state space grows~\cite{hartmanns2015explicit}.
Similarly, \emph{sampling-based methods} may need millions of simulation traces until convergence is achieved~\cite{henriques2012statistical}. 

To address this challenge, we introduce \textbf{S}calable \textbf{H}ierarchical \textbf{A}daptive \textbf{R}efinement for \textbf{P}olicy synthesis (\textbf{SHARP}). 
The core of our approach is based upon a ``refine-where-needed'' principle, which focuses computational effort only on the most critical regions of the state space. 
\acronym\ begins with a coarse abstraction of the MDP and iteratively improves it by subdividing and solving state space partitions, yielding a refined and more detailed analysis. 
Through this iterative and selective partitioning, \acronym\ balances solution accuracy with execution efficiency, accelerating verification for large MDPs.

