\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:prelims}

\subsection{Probabilistic Modelling}
\textbf{Markov decision process (MDP)}. 
An MDP is a tuple
\(\
\mathcal{M} \;=\; (S, \bar{s}, A, \delta,r, AP, L),\) where $S$ is a finite set of states; $\bar{s}\in S$ is an initial state; $A$ is a finite set of actions; $\delta: (S \times A)\to \mathit{Dist}(S)$ is a probabilistic transition function mapping each $(s,a)$ pair to a probability distribution over $S$;
\(r : S \to \mathbb R_{\ge 0}\) is a reward structure;
$AP$ is a set of atomic propositions; and $L: S \to 2^{AP}$ is a labelling function.

MDPs support nondeterministic actions as each state $s\in S$ can have multiple enabled actions for which $\mathit{Dist}(s,a)$ is defined, given by $A(s)$. 
An MDP \textit{policy} is a possible resolution of nondeterminism. 
Resolving this nondeterminism enables to probabilistically reason about MDPs and synthesise \textit{memoryless deterministic policies}, i.e., policies where the action chosen in a state depends solely on the current state. 
Formally, a (deterministic memoryless) policy of an MDP is a function $\sigma :S \rightarrow A$ mapping each state $s\in S$ to an action in $A(s)$.

\textbf{Probabilistic Computation Tree Logic (PCTL).}
PCTL~\cite{hansson1994pctl,bianco1995model} specifies properties over MDPs.  
State formulae:
$\Phi ::= \textit{true} \mid a \mid \neg\Phi \mid \Phi_1\land\Phi_2
          \mid \PP_{\bowtie p}[\varphi] \mid \RR^r_{\bowtie q}[\rho]$,
with $a\!\in\!AP$, $\bowtie\!\in\!\{<,\le,>,\ge\}$,
$p\!\in\![0,1]$, $q\!\ge\!0$.
Path formulae:
$\varphi ::= \mathsf X\,\Phi \mid \Phi_1\,\mathsf U^{\le k}\Phi_2 \mid \Phi_1\,\mathsf U\,\Phi_2$.
Reachability is the shorthand $\mathsf F\Phi \equiv \textit{true}\,\mathsf U\,\Phi$.
Typical query: $\PP_{\max}[\mathsf F\,\textit{goal}]$ which asks the maximum probability of eventually reaching a goal state.




\subsection{Probabilistic Model Checking}
\label{subsec:pmc}
Probabilistic Model Checking (PMC)~\cite{kwiatkowska2007stochastic} is a well-known technique for the verification of stochastic systems 
Given a probabilistic model, PMC evaluates if the system satisfies a specification expressed in temporal logic (e.g., PCTL). 
Probabilistic model checkers (e.g., PRISM~\cite{prism}, Storm~\cite{storm}) automate the verification process.

\textbf{Quantitative reachability.}
Let $M=(S,\bar{s},A,\delta,r,AP,L)$ be the MDP defined above and
$T\subseteq S$ a set of target states.
We denote by $\mathsf{F} \ T$ the event that $T$ is eventually reached.
For any state \(s\in S\) and policy \(\sigma\), let \(\Pr^{\sigma}_{s}\) (and the associated expectation \(\mathbb{E}^{\sigma}_{s}\)) denote the probability measure on infinite state sequences \(\pi=\pi_{0}\pi_{1}\ldots\) with \(\pi_{0}=s\) such that, at every step \(i\), an action \(a_{i}=\sigma(\pi_{i})\) is taken and \(\delta(\pi_{i},a_{i})(\pi_{i+1})>0\).

The minimum reachability probability is \(\PP_{\min}(s,\mathsf{F} \ T)=\inf_{\sigma}\Pr^\sigma_s(\mathsf{F} \ T)\), and the maximum reachability probability is \(\PP_{\max}(s,\mathsf{F} \ T)=\sup_{\sigma}\Pr^\sigma_s(\mathsf{F} \ T)\).
The minimum expected reachability reward is \(\RR_{\min}(s,\mathsf{F}T) = \inf_{\sigma} \mathbb{E}^{\sigma}_{s}\bigl[\sum_{k<k_T} r(\pi_k)\bigr]\), and the maximum expected reachability reward is \(\RR_{\max}(s,\mathsf{F}T) = \sup_{\sigma} \mathbb{E}^{\sigma}_{s}\bigl[\sum_{k<k_T} r(\pi_k)\bigr]\), where \(k_T=\min\{j \mid \pi_j\in T\}\) is the first step index at which a state in \(T\) is reached.

\textbf{Value Iteration (VI).}
VI \cite{puterman1994mdps,chatterjee2008iteration} is a widely used dynamic programming technique \cite{bellman1957} that repeatedly applies the Bellman operator to
approximate both optimal reachability probabilities and rewards.
Let \(V^{(k)}(s)\) (resp.\ \(W^{(k)}(s)\)) be the probability (resp.\ expected
reward) obtained after \(k\) iterations. For every state $s\in S$ these sequences converge
as $k\!\to\!\infty$, namely
$V^{(k)}(s)\!\to\!\mathsf \PP_{\min/\max}(s,\mathsf F\,T)$ and
$W^{(k)}(s)\!\to\!\mathsf \RR_{\min/\max}(s,\mathsf F\,T)$.
Gauss-Seidel (GS) is an efficient value iteration variant that converges faster by incorporating the latest probability values per state during every iteration~\cite{baier2008principles}. 
