\subsection{Partitioning and Tree Hierarchy Construction}
\label{subsec:hierarchy-init} Foundational approaches to managing large MDPs include partitioning the state space to construct a smaller, aggregate model where each block becomes a single abstract state  \cite{dean1997min}. While in our work we do not perform state aggregation,
we leverage the principle of partitioning to structure the policy synthesis process hierarchically. \acronym\ is underpinned by two core concepts:
 (i) the partitioning of the state space into \emph{blocks}; and
 (ii) the \emph{hierarchical, nested} arrangement of such partitions obtained by recursively subdividing blocks, represented by a \emph{hierarchy tree}.

\begin{definition}[Partitioning]\label{def:partitioning}
Given an MDP $\mathcal{M}$,
% =(S,A,\delta, \ldots)$, 
a \emph{partitioning} of its state space $S$ is a collection $\mathcal{B}=\{B_1, B_2, \dots, B_k\}$ of subsets of $S$, called \textbf{\emph{blocks}}, such that:
(i) Each block $B_i$ is non-empty ($B_i \neq \emptyset$ for all $i=1,\dots,k$); 
(ii) The blocks are pairwise disjoint ($B_i \cap B_j = \emptyset$ for all $i \neq j$);
and (iii) the union of all blocks covers the entire state space ($\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} B_i = S$).
\end{definition}

