Algorithm~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement} shows the \acronym\ process for the synthesis of MDP policies. 
The algorithm takes as inputs: an MDP $\mathcal{M}$;
% =(S,A,\delta)$; 
an objective $\phi$, which is a PCTL reachability property of the form $\PP_{\mathsf{opt}}[\mathsf{F}\,G]$ (for probability objectives) or $\RR_{\mathsf{opt}}[\mathsf{F}\,G]$ (for reward objectives), where $\mathsf{opt} \in \{\min,\max\}$ specifies the optimisation direction and $G \subseteq S$ is the set of goal states; a reward function $R$ (required if $\phi$ is a reward objective); partition sizes $(N_x,N_y)$ that define the initial grid dimensions for partitioning the state space; maximum refinement depth $D$; the refinement threshold $\theta$ that determines when to refine a partition based on its value spread; %(its semantics depend on the objective type detailed in Section~\ref{subsec:adapt-refine}); 
the maximum number of global iterations $\mathit{maxIters}$; and a VI convergence threshold $\varepsilon>0$. 
The algorithm returns a value vector for the MDP states $S$ and a memoryless policy~$\sigma$. 
%$\pi$.

%We begin by creating a hierarchy tree over the state space $S$ of the MDP. This involves the following two steps: (i) creating a \texttt{rootNode} that contains the entire set of states; and (ii) a partitioning of $S$ into initial coarse blocks, each of which becomes a child of the \texttt{rootNode}. Together, these steps set up the ``top levels'' of the hierarchy before any subsequent refinement can occur.

\textbf{Creating the \texttt{rootNode}:}
First, as described in line 2 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement}, we create a single root node that contains the entire set of states $S$. As the root of the hierarchy tree, this node has depth 0 and no parent node. 
Each tree node, including the root, holds a \texttt{values} vector to store a provisional solution for these states. 
For probability objectives, we initialise non-goal state values to 0 and goal state values to 1. For reward objectives, we initialise non-goal states to a large constant (when minimising) or 0 (when maximising), and we initialise goal states to 0 regardless of the optimisation direction.

\textbf{Initial Partitioning.} Next, in line 3 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement}, we \emph{partition} the state space into a set of coarse partitions. 
Each partition will become a child node in the hierarchy, creating the first hierarchy layer. 
Several strategies can be used to form these subsets, including geometric partitioning, considering spatial proximity or state similarity.
For instance, if the state space corresponds to a regular grid (e.g., an $n \times n$ robotic environment), we can subdivide it uniformly into $\ell \times \ell$ blocks.

