\noindent
\textbf{Example 1.}
Consider again the warehouse robotics model from Section~\ref{sec:motiv-example}.
Our goal is to synthesise an optimal policy for the reachability property
\(\PP_{\max}\!\bigl[\,\mathsf{F}\,\text{``goal''}\bigr]\).




\noindent
\emph{Hierarchy initialisation:} 
We begin with a single root node that contains all states of the MDP, that is the 36 floor positions of the 6$\times$6 grid.
This node is placed at depth 0.
Next, we apply a coarse geometric partition to the grid: a uniform 2$\times$2 tiling yields nine non-overlapping blocks (Figure~\ref{fig:submdp-example}).
Each block is non-empty, and together they cover the entire warehouse; each becomes a child of the root node, forming a proper partition of the state space.

After the initial partitioning, \acronym\ performs an initial refinement (lines 4-6) on each of the nine blocks. 
For instance, block 9 containing the goal area might be refined further if its value spread exceeds $\theta$. 
Then, the algorithm enters its main loop (lines 8-19), iteratively solving leaf partitions and propagating values upward until convergence as described next.
