\subsection{Adaptive Partition Refinement}
\label{subsec:adapt-refine}
SHARP achieves efficiency by adaptively refining partitions only where necessary.
The decision to refine a leaf node in the hierarchy tree is based on criteria assessing its current state and value distribution.
For a node \(v\) representing block \(B_v\) with current value estimates \(V_v\), we define the \emph{value spread}
$ \Delta(v)=\max_{s\in B_v}V_v(s)-\min_{s\in B_v}V_v(s).$

\textbf{Block-refinement criteria.}
A leaf node \(v\) is refined iff all of the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Depth criterion}: \(v.\mathrm{depth}<D\), i.e., the $v$ node's depth is less than the user-imposed maximum depth.
  \item \textbf{Value spread criterion}:
        \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt,topsep=1pt]
          \item \emph{Probability properties} (\(\PP_{\mathsf{opt}}\)): $\Delta(v) > \theta$, i.e., the absolute spread \(\Delta(v)\) exceeds the threshold~\(\theta\).
          \item \emph{Reward/cost properties} (\(\RR_{\mathsf{opt}}\)): \(\Delta(v)\big/\!\bigl(\operatorname{mean}(V_v)+\beta\bigr) > \theta\), i.e.,  
          the relative reward spread exceeds~\(\theta\), where $\beta$ is a small positive constant used for numerical stability.
          % the threshold may be adapted to the node depth.
        \end{itemize}
  % \item \textbf{Optional custom predicate}: a user-supplied \textsc{ShouldSplit}\((v)\) (e.g.\ based on visit counts or safety labels).  
  %       In our experiments we leave this predicate \emph{true} for all
  %       $v$, but the hook is available to users.
\end{enumerate}


If these conditions are satisfied, $v$ is subdivided into two or more child blocks that form a partition of $B_v$ using the \textsc{RefineNode} procedure shown in Algorithm~\ref{alg:RefineNode}. 
To this end, the algorithm
% \textsc{RefineNode} 
takes as inputs a node $v$ representing block $B_v$; the maximum depth parameter $D$; and the refinement threshold $\theta$. The procedure computes the value spread $\Delta(v)$ (line 3 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:RefineNode}) and compares it against $\theta$. If the spread is below $\theta$ or if $v$ has already reached depth $D$ (line 2), \textsc{RefineNode} terminates. Otherwise, it splits $v$ into smaller sub-partitions (line 5), solves each sub-partition (line 6), recurses on the children (line 7), and finally propagates the refined values back to $v$'s parent (line 11). This ensures that only regions with large value variation are examined in greater detail while the parent's estimates remain consistent.

\vspace{1mm}
\noindent
\textbf{Example 2.}
Continuing the warehouse scenario of Example 1, assume we have solved the nine
top-level partitions in Figure \ref{fig:submdp-example} and found that
partition 9, which contains the goal cell \((5,5)\), exhibits a large value
spread~\(\Delta\).
Cells that border the goal have values close to \(1.0\), whereas those one
step farther away are noticeably smaller, so \(\Delta>\theta\).
\textsc{RefineNode} (Algorithm \ref{alg:RefineNode}) therefore triggers a split.
The procedure follows the rule that a block at depth~\(d\) is subdivided into
\((2+d)\times(2+d)\) conceptual buckets (line 5 of the algorithm). At depth 1, this
yields a \(3\times3\) partitioning scheme, which splits the \(2\times2\) block's bounding rectangle into nine equal‐sized conceptual buckets, where each grid cell is assigned to exactly one bucket, empty buckets are discarded, and every non-empty bucket becomes a refined sub-block. Partition 9 itself is a \(2\times2\)  area. So, after the 3x3 bucketing, only those conceptual buckets that actually contain grid cells (and are not otherwise excluded) are instantiated as new sub-blocks. In this example, the three single-state sub-MDPs created are \texttt{B9a} ((5,4)), \texttt{B9b} ((4,5)), and \texttt{B9c} ((4,4)). Note that the goal state ((5,5)), which was also a grid cell within partition 9, is absorbing and needs no further refinement, so its bucket does not lead to a new sub-MDP.



