\subsection{Local Sub-MDP Policy Synthesis}
\label{subsec:local-submdp}
Once a node $v$ is partitioned, either as part of the initial partitioning (line 3 in Algorithm~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement}) or due to further refinement (line 8 in Algorithm~\ref{alg:RefineNode}), \acronym\ views each partition as a sub-MDP that includes both the partition's states and boundary states (states from neighbouring partitions that are directly reachable). 
The core idea is to solve each sub-MDP independently without considering the other sub-MDPs, using the boundary values inherited from the parent node to approximate the behaviour beyond the currently solved sub-MDP.


\textbf{Constructing the sub-MDP.}
% When solving a leaf node (lines 6 and 21 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement}),
\acronym\ builds the induced sub-MDP $\mathcal{M}\!_B$ for block $B\subseteq S$
in three steps:
(i) include every state $s\in B$ together with its original actions and
any successor that also lies in $B$;
(ii) add each boundary state
$\mathrm{bd}(B)=\{\,t\notin B \mid \exists\,s\in B,\,a\in A(s):\delta(s,a)(t)>0\}$,
giving it a self-loop (probability 1);
and (iii) initialise every boundary state $t$ with the parent value
$V_{\text{parent}}(t)$, which remains fixed throughout local VI.


\begin{definition}[Induced sub-MDP] 
% by a block]
\label{def:submdp}
Let $\mathcal{M}=(S,\bar s,A,\delta,r,AP,L)$ be an MDP and
$B\subseteq S$, $B\neq\varnothing$.
We define the boundary of $B$ as $\mathrm{bd}(B) \;=\;
  \bigl\{\,t\in S\setminus B \mid
     \exists\,s \in B,\;a\in A(s):\; \delta(s,a)(t)>0 \bigr\}.$ 
The \emph{sub-MDP induced by $B$} is
\[
  \mathcal{M}\!_B
  =\bigl(B\cup\mathrm{bd}(B),\;
         \bar s_B,\;
         A_B,\;
         \delta_B,\;
         r_B,\;
         AP,\;
         L_B\bigr),
\]
where for every $s\in B\cup\mathrm{bd}(B)$ and $a\in A(s)$,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\bar s_B &=
  \begin{cases}
    \bar s & \text{if }\bar s\in B,\\
    \text{any state in }B & \text{otherwise},
  \end{cases}\\[4pt]
A_B(s) &= A(s),\\[4pt]
\delta_B(s,a)(t) &=
  \begin{cases}
    \delta(s,a)(t) & s\in B,\;t\in B\cup\mathrm{bd}(B),\\
    1              & s\in\mathrm{bd}(B),\;t=s,\\
    0              & \text{otherwise},
  \end{cases}\\[8pt]
r_B(s) &= r(s),\\
L_B(s) &= L(s).
\end{aligned}
\]


\begin{algorithm}[tb]
\caption{\textsc{RefineNode}}
\label{alg:RefineNode}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\small
\Require node $v$ with states $S_v$, max depth $D$, threshold $\theta$, reward function $R$, objective $\phi$
\Ensure possibly subdivides $v$; updates $v.\mathrm{values}$

\Procedure{RefineNode}{$v,\ D,\ \theta, R, \phi$}
    \If{$v.\mathrm{depth} < D$} \Comment check max. depth

    \State $\Delta \gets \max(v.\mathrm{values}) - \min(v.\mathrm{values})$ \Comment spread
    \If{$\Delta > \theta$} \Comment check homogeneity
    \State $v.children$ $\gets$ \Call{Partition}{$v,\dots$} \Comment partition $v$
    % \ForAll{\textbf{child } c \textbf{ of } v}
        % \State \Call{SolveSubMDP}{$c,\ v, R, \phi$}
    \ForAll{$c$ \textbf{ in } $\texttt{v.children}$}
        \State $c.values, c.\sigma \gets $\Call{SolveSubMDP}{$c, v, R, \phi$}
        \State \Call{RefineNode}{$c,\ D,\ \theta, R, \phi$}
    \EndFor

    % \State $v.\mathrm{refined} \gets \text{true}$
    \State \Call{PropagateUp}{$v$}\Comment Sect.\,\ref{subsec:value-prop} 
    \EndIf
    \EndIf
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Accordingly, each state in $\mathrm{bd}(B)$ becomes absorbing in the sub-MDP
$\mathcal{M}\!_B$. 
Its fixed value is supplied by the parent of each state in $\mathrm{bd}(B)$ in the hierarchy tree $\mathcal{T}$ as defined next.
% externally by the refinement algorithm (see Section~\ref{subsec:local-submdp}).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Boundary-value assignment]\label{def:boundary_value}
Let $B\subseteq S$ be a block represented by a node $v$ in the hierarchy
tree~$\mathcal{T}$, and let
$\mathcal{M}\!_B$ be the sub-MDP induced by $B$
with boundary states $\mathrm{bd}(B)$.
For every boundary state $t\in\mathrm{bd}(B)$, its \emph{boundary value}
is set to $V_B(t)\;=\;V_{\mathrm{parent}}(t)$,
where $V_{\mathrm{parent}}$ is the value vector stored at the
\emph{immediate} parent of~$v$ in~$\mathcal{T}$.
These values are held fixed throughout the local value iteration step on
$\mathcal{M}\!_B$, thereby incorporating the influence of
the surrounding MDP.
\end{definition}


\textbf{Local value iteration.}
The sub-MDP is solved with VI until
$\lVert V^{(k+1)}-V^{(k)}\rVert_\infty<\varepsilon$ (line~20 in Algorithm~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement}, line~7 in Algorithm~\ref{alg:RefineNode}).
The action that attains the optimum value is stored as the local policy as defined below.

\begin{definition}[Local value function]\label{def:local_value_a}
Given a reachability property
$\PP_{\mathsf{opt}}[\mathsf F\,T]$ or
$\RR_{\mathsf{opt}}[\mathsf F\,T]$ with global goal set $G=T$,
fix a block $B$ and its induced sub-MDP
$\mathcal M\!_B$ (Def.~\ref{def:submdp}).
Let $G_B=G\cap B$ and use $\mathrm{bd}(B)$ for the boundary states.

For $\mathsf{opt}\in\{\min,\max\}$, the \emph{local value}
$V_B:B\cup\mathrm{bd}(B)\to\mathbb R$ is the unique solution of:

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Probability objective} ($\PP_{\mathsf{opt}}$)
\[
  V_B(s)=
  \begin{cases}
    1, & s\in G_B,\\
    \displaystyle
    \mathsf{opt}_{a\in A_B(s)}\\
      \quad\sum_{t\in B\cup\mathrm{bd}(B)}
        \delta_B(s,a)(t)\,V_B(t),
      & s\in B\setminus G_B,\\
    V_{\text{parent}}(s), & s\in\mathrm{bd}(B).
  \end{cases}
\]

\noindent\textbf{Reward/Cost objective} ($\RR_{\mathsf{opt}}$)
\[
  V_B(s)=
  \begin{cases}
    0, & s\in G_B,\\
    \displaystyle
    \mathsf{opt}_{a\in A_B(s)}
      \bigl(r(s)+\\
        \quad\sum_{t\in B\cup\mathrm{bd}(B)}
          \delta_B(s,a)(t)\,V_B(t)
      \bigr), & s\in B\setminus G_B,\\
    V_{\text{parent}}(s), & s\in\mathrm{bd}(B).
  \end{cases}
\]

% Accordingly, boundary states values are inherited from the parent node(Def.~\ref{def:boundary_value}) and remain constant while solving $\mathcal M\!_B$.
\end{definition}




\textbf{Storing and propagating.}
After local convergence, \acronym\ stores both the value vector $\{V_B(s) \mid s\in B \cup \mathrm{bd}(B)\}$ and the corresponding partial policy $\sigma$ in the
node associated with block~$B$.
If the node is a leaf, the sub-MDP solving is complete.
Otherwise, once all child nodes are solved, we propagate their values upward to update this parent node's estimates; the full procedure is
detailed in Section~\ref{subsec:value-prop}.

Solving sub-MDPs locally by utilising the boundary states derived from their parents' values is fundamental in SHARP.  
This principle avoids running a monolithic fixed-point iteration on the entire state space and focuses computation
only on sub-regions that actually require finer analysis.

\vspace{1mm}
\noindent
\textbf{Example 3 (continued).} Suppose we have partitioned B9 into three single-state blocks: \texttt{B9a}, \texttt{B9b}, and \texttt{B9c}. To construct the sub-MDP for \texttt{B9a} (containing cell $(5,4)$), we include: (i) the state $(5,4)$ with all its original actions and transitions, and (ii) any states directly reachable from $(5,4)$ as boundary states - including the goal $(5,5)$ and adjacent cells. These boundary states are absorbing (self-loops with probability 1) and assigned fixed values from block 9.
%
For such a small sub-MDP, local VI converges quickly. Since $(5,4)$ is adjacent to the goal (whose value is 1.0), the converged value for $(5,4)$ will be high. 
After solving all three children sub-MDPs, we propagate their values back to update block B9's estimates.

\subsection{Value Propagation Up the Tree Hierarchy}
\label{subsec:value-prop}

After solving each leaf node's sub-MDP and yielding local solutions (Section~\ref{subsec:local-submdp}), \acronym\ propagates these values up the hierarchy tree to obtain a consistent global policy (line~9 in Algorithm~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement}, line~10 in Algorithm~\ref{alg:RefineNode}). 
This value propagation process recursively updates each parent node's value estimates and policy based on the refined solutions of its children. 
This essential step enables updating the boundary state values to reflect the more accurate estimates produced by the sub-MDPs.
% at finer granularity levels.
% This is essential because boundary values used during local solving must be updated to reflect the more accurate estimates obtained at finer granularity levels.

The \textsc{PropagateAllUp} procedure (line 10 in Algorithm~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement}) performs a recursive bottom-up traversal of the hierarchy tree $\mathcal{T}$. 
For each node $v \in \mathcal{N}$, it first processes all children recursively, ensuring that their values are up to date. Then, for each state $s \in B_v$, the procedure determines which child block contains $s$ and adopts that child's value and policy. Since the children's blocks $\{B_{c_1}, \ldots, B_{c_k}\}$ form a partition of $B_v$, each state belongs to exactly one child, making this assignment unambiguous.

Formally, for each state $s \in B_v$, if $s \in B_{c_i}$ for some child $c_i$, then we set $V_v(s) = V_{c_i}(s)$ and $\pi_v(s) = \pi_{c_i}(s)$. This propagation ensures that the refined value estimates and policy decisions from deeper levels of the hierarchy are incorporated into coarser levels, ultimately producing a globally consistent policy $\sigma$ at the root node.

\textbf{Example 4}.
With the local solutions for \texttt{B9a}, \texttt{B9b}, and \texttt{B9c} now computed, we merge them back into block B9 by assigning each single-state block's value (and policy) to the corresponding cell. 
For instance, if \texttt{B9a} determined that $(5,4)$ has a probability $\approx\! 1.0$ to reach $(5,5)$, we set $V_9(5,4) \approx 1.0$. Similarly, \texttt{B9b} and \texttt{B9c} provide values for their respective cells. 
At this point, block B9 value estimates are updated based on its children's refined solutions. 
Although in this example, we reached single-state blocks that cannot be subdivided further, \acronym, in general, checks in subsequent iterations if further refinement is needed based on the updated value spread. 


\section{Implementation}
\label{sec:implementation}
\acronym\ is implemented in Java on top of the probabilistic model checker PRISM~\cite{prism}. 
The core of our implementation lies in newly developed algorithms integrated within PRISM's explicit engine, including hierarchical partitioning, adaptive refinement based on value spread, sub-MDP construction with boundary states, and bottom-up value propagation.
