\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}
\noindent
\textbf{Case studies.}
Our experimental evaluation uses robotic warehouse scenarios represented by grid-based MDPs of varying complexities (Table~\ref{tab:case_studies}), which have served as standard benchmarks for evaluating MDP algorithms in both reinforcement learning~\cite{kaelbling1996rlsurvey,sutton1999temporal,deepmind2017gridworlds} and formal verification~\cite{velasquez2022controller,bals2024multigain,cai2021LTL}.
We evaluate 512×512 and 1024×1024 grids with three obstacle configurations: no walls (NW), single wall (SW), and multiple walls (MW). 
Each configuration has two variants: 
(i) maximise probability to reach the goal state (0.9 move, 0.09975 self-loop, 0.00025 transition to failure state); 
and (b) minimising expected steps without failures (0.8 move, 0.2 self-loop).

Further to the benchmarks in Table~\ref{tab:case_studies}, and to assess \acronym's robustness in randomised environments,  we conducted experiments using 5 randomly generated 1024×1024 MW MDPs, with various wall configurations (sparse, maze, dense, etc.). 

\vspace{0.5mm}\noindent
\textbf{Baselines and configurations.}
We compare \acronym\ against PRISM's explicit engine using both standard VI and its GS variant %(a VI variant) 
with and without pre-computations~\cite{prism}. 
For our evaluation, PRISM's explicit engine served as a more suitable baseline than other tools like Storm~\cite{storm} because it allows for a fair and controlled comparison. 
Since \acronym\ is implemented as a Java-based extension within the PRISM ecosystem, benchmarking it against PRISM's own engine effectively isolates the performance impact of our hierarchical algorithm, which is the core contribution of this work. Introducing an external, highly-optimised C++ tool like Storm would add significant confounding variables (language, compiler, specialised libraries), making it difficult to distinguish between algorithmic gains and implementation optimisations. Thus, PRISM provides a transparent baseline to validate the specific benefits of our hierarchical refinement strategy within \acronym.

We explored \acronym\ configurations with: maximum depth $D \in \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}$; initial partition sizes from $8 \times 8$ to $25 \times 25$; reward thresholds $\theta \in \{200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 800\}$; %for $\mathtt{R_{\min}}$;  
and probability thresholds $\theta \in \{0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\}$. % for $\mathtt{P_{\max}}$.
All sub-MDPs were solved using VI (convergence threshold $\varepsilon=10^{-6}$).





