\textbf{RQ1 (Efficiency).} 
We assess \acronym's efficiency against the state-of-the-art model checker PRISM~\cite{prism} by measuring the model checking time and memory overhead across the case studies from Table~\ref{tab:case_studies}. In Table \ref{tab:results}, we compare SHARP with PRISM's explicit-engine methods, VI and GS, both with and without precomputation on the two properties described earlier.

We observe that across the smaller model instances (512x512, e.g., W512NW-a/b) PRISM yields a policy in $\approx\!6$-$15$s, using $1.6$--$1.9$\,GB of memory. On the other hand, SHARP yields the policy in about $8$--$13$s and uses $6.4$--$8.5$\,GB. For the 512SW-a/b, W512MW-a/b instances, both PRISM and \acronym\ finish in less than 
$<20$s, although PRISM is sometimes faster, surprisingly, when no pre-computation is used.
%
However, in larger problem instances, with over 1 million states, such as the W1024* ones, PRISM-VI typically takes 46--118s and uses at least 5 GB of memory, whereas PRISM-GS does not consistently perform better, except in a few scenarios. 
Instead, \acronym's hierarchical analysis completes the \(\mathtt{P_{\max}}\) instances in $\approx 39$--$47s$ s and the \(\mathtt{R_{\min}}\) instances in $\approx  65$--$66s$ (Table~\ref{tab:results}).

We visually compare the %absolute 
runtimes for PRISM-VI, PRISM-GS, and SHARP in~Figure~\ref{fig:performance-comparison}(top).
For example, in \(\mathrm{W1024SW\text{-}a}\) (\(\mathtt{P_{\max}}\)), PRISM-VI requires around 47.3s, whereas \acronym\ takes only about 39.1s, resulting in a saving of over 8s. 
For the reward property, the performance gains yielded by \acronym\ are more significant. 
For instance, for \(\mathrm{W1024SW\text{-}b}\) (\(\mathtt{R_{\min}}\)), PRISM-VI/GS need approximately 118s/113s, while \acronym\ requires 65.4s, achieving a 1.7\(\times\) speedup.

This performance gap is further illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:performance-comparison}(bottom), which shows the relative speedup of \acronym\ over PRISM, computed as the ratio of (best PRISM time) / (best SHARP time).
A bar above 1.0 indicates that \acronym\ is strictly faster.
% Furthermore, in this figure, we illustrate the relative speedup of SHARP over PRISM, computed as the ratio of (best PRISM time) / (best SHARP time). 
% A bar above 1.0 indicates that SHARP is strictly faster. 
For example, the bar near 1.7 for \(\mathrm{W1024SW\text{-}b}\) signifies that SHARP finishes in roughly 60\% of PRISM's time. Note that even in the \(\mathtt{P_{\max}}\) cases where the performance gap is smaller than the 8s difference observed in \(\mathrm{W1024SW\text{-}a}\), SHARP still offers a noticeable speedup of about 1.2$\times$. 
For instance, in \(\mathrm{W1024MW\text{-}a}\), PRISM takes $\approx$46.3--46.6 s, while SHARP completes in $\approx$40.8 s.
Overall, we observe that \acronym\ consistently outperforms the best PRISM variant by up to nearly 2\(\times\) on the more complex model instances.
% Evidently, \acronym\ consistently outperforms the best PRISM variant  on these large MDPs, sometimes by nearly 2$\times$.%


Concerning memory usage, we note that although \acronym\ may require 18--24\,GB in certain cases, it remains within typical machine limits. 
Also, in terms of solution quality, we do not notice any deviations from the final result reported by PRISM.

Figure~\ref{fig:boxplot-prism-sharp-detailed} shows boxplots for the model checking times (in seconds) of the larger model instances ($1024 \times 1024$) and the different warehouse layouts (MW, NW, SW) across 30 independent runs.
The boxplots compare \acronym\ against the PRISM configurations (VI and GS, with and without precomputations) for both $\mathtt{P_{\max}}$ and $\mathtt{R_{\min}}$ properties, corroborating \acronym's efficiency. 
For example, the median execution time for \acronym\ is lower than that of the PRISM methods in 5 out of 6 cases, with particularly noteworthy performance gains for the $\mathtt{R_{\min}}$ property where SHARP consistently achieves the lowest median times across all model instances.

