We evaluate the scalability of \acronym\ by comparing it against the best-performing PRISM configuration (GS nopre), on the SW warehouse model across instances of increasing size: $128\!\times\!128$, $256\!\times\!256$, $512\!\times\!512$, and $1024\!\times\!1024$. 
Figure~\ref{fig:scalability}(left) shows model checking times for the $\mathtt{P_{\max}}$ and $\mathtt{R_{\min}}$ properties. Evidently, the scalability capabilities of \acronym\ are significantly better than those of PRISM for both properties in the largest MDP models.
In particular, for the smaller grid sizes up to $256\times256$, \acronym\ exhibits a near-linear scaling behaviour, while PRISM's model checking time grows in a super-linear manner. Looking at Figure~\ref{fig:scalability}(left), PRISM's model checking times are between 0.14-0.81s for $\mathtt{P_{\max}}$ and 0.38-1.86s for $\mathtt{R_{\min}}$, while \acronym's are 1.09-2.16s and 1.00-3.04s, respectively. We note that although \acronym\ remains relatively fast for these particular model sizes, its hierarchical refinement logic does not offer benefits. 
However, for the larger model sizes (i.e., $512\times512$ and especially in $1024\times1024$), the benefits of hierarchical refinement become more apparent. 
Specifically, as the state space grows beyond $2.6 \times 10^5$ states (i.e., a $512 \times 512$ grid), \acronym's near-linear scaling enables it to perform better for $\mathtt{R_{\min}}$ (12.49s vs 14.28s), though it remains slower for $\mathtt{P_{\max}}$ (8.29s vs 5.94s). 
At grid sizes over $1 \times 10^6$ states (i.e., $1024\times1024$), \acronym\ is significantly faster than PRISM for both properties, completing $\mathtt{P_{\max}}$ in 39.10s vs 46.68s and $\mathtt{R_{\min}}$ in 65.39s vs 112.74s.

On the other hand, \acronym's memory footprint is higher across all grid sizes (Figure~\ref{fig:scalability}(right)). 
This is expected, since \acronym\ maintains additional data structures to store intermediate refinements and instrument its hierarchical refinement. 
For instance, for the  $512\times512$ grid size, PRISM's memory usage remains $\approx$ 1.1-1.9 GB, whereas \acronym\ can require up to 8.2 GB. 
However, these overheads are acceptable for modern computers, and the trade-off in extra memory usage is justified by the significantly improved runtime on larger models.

