\acronym's predictable scaling behaviour is valuable and can help engineers verify their models within acceptable time limits. 

\vspace{1mm}\noindent
\textbf{RQ3 (Hyperparameter sensitivity).} 
We evaluate \acronym's sensitivity to key hyperparameters (maximum refinement depth $D$, reward threshold $\theta$, and initial partition sizes) using the $1024\times1024$ SW instance ($\RR\mathtt{_{\min}}$). Table~\ref{tab:config-params} and Figure~\ref{fig:hyper-param1} show the performance impact of configurations A--E.

Results show that initial partition size dominates performance, more than refinement depth or threshold. The $D=1$ configurations (group A, $8\times8$ partitions) achieve best performance with minimal overhead. While deeper refinements ($D$=2-5) increase resource usage, the initial partition size remains the critical factor: B1-B2 ($8\times8$) outperform B3-B4 ($16\times16$) despite more refinement steps; C2 ($20\times20$) requires 23\% more time than C1 ($12\times12$) with identical refinements; and E2 ($25\times25$) shows worst overall performance. This pattern confirms that smaller initial partitions ($8\times8$ to $12\times12$) yield better performance regardless of refinement depth, as the overhead from managing numerous partitions outweighs refinement benefits.
% \\
% \textbf{Decision making guidelines.} 
Hence, \acronym\ users should begin with $D=2$ and $8 \!\times\! 8$ partitions, as this configuration performs well in diverse problems, while initial partitions larger than $12 \!\times\! 12$ typically degrade performance.
Deeper refinements (higher $D$) are justified mainly when observing large spreads in critical regions.
Finally, shallower depths and moderate partition sizes are likely to yield better results instead of deep hierarchies.

