\subsection{Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:threats}
\noindent
% \textbf{Construct validity threats.}
We mitigate \textbf{construct validity threats} that might arise due to the lack of global context in sub-MDPS due to the hierarchical decomposition by grounding \acronym\ in the well-established VI algorithm for probabilities and rewards.
\acronym\ also propagates values down the tree, while refinement is triggered when the value spread criterion is violated, therefore, ensuring that accuracy is recovered where needed across the tree hierarchy.
\vspace{0.5mm}\\
% \textbf{Internal validity threats.}
We limit \textbf{internal validity threats} by integrating \acronym\ %as a new module 
into PRISM’s explicit-state engine, reusing its core and supplying our algorithms for hierarchical partitioning, adaptive refinement, and local sub-MDP solving.
By building on a widely tested code base, we minimise the risk that implementation bugs skew our measurements.
We reduce threats due to \acronym's stochasticity by performing 30 independent runs per problem instance and reporting measures of central tendency and dispersion.
% We integrated SHARP as a new module into PRISM’s explicit-state engine.
%
\vspace{0.5mm}\\
% \textbf{External validity threats.}
% \textbf{External validity threats.}
We mitigate \textbf{external validity threats} by evaluating \acronym\ on large benchmarks that correspond to realistic warehouse robot scenarios of varying complexity and model sizes~\cite{fragapane2021planning} and comparing it against the VI and GS algorithms of PRISM~\cite{prism}.
Assessing the performance gains yielded by \acronym\ against other state-of-the-art tools like Storm~\cite{storm} entails implementing the core of our approach (Algorithms~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement} and~\ref{alg:HierarchicalRefinement}) within these tools to leverage the highly-optimised numerical libraries used internally. 
Doing this would allow for a fair and transparent comparison focusing purely on algorithmic gains and mitigating any potential bias strictly due to implementation optimisations.

Lastly, more experiments on non-grid and highly irregular MDPs (e.g., self-adaptive systems~\cite{calinescu2017engineering,hezavehi2021uncertainty}, software product lines~\cite{camara2019synthesizing}) are needed to establish \acronym's applicability and scalability in domains different from those in our evaluation.

