\\
\textbf{Search-based methods.}
Evolutionary approaches~\cite{coello2007evolutionary} tackle probabilistic verification tasks intractable for exact techniques. EvoChecker~\cite{gerasimou2015evochecker,gerasimou2018evo} and Haiq~\cite{camara2020haiq} produce models to satisfy quantitative requirements, while EvoPoli~\cite{gerasimou2021evopoli} approximates Pareto fronts for multi-objective models too large for exact verification.
\acronym\ is complementary; it accelerates exact synthesis for single objectives while preserving optimality, which evolutionary methods do not guarantee.
\\
\textbf{Hierarchical RL.}
Early HRL frameworks such as Feudal RL~\cite{dayan1992feudal},
MAXQ~\cite{dietterich2000maxq}, and Options~\cite{sutton1999temporal} decompose an MDP into subtasks or macro-actions, reducing sample complexity.
Recent abstractions (e.g., AVI~\cite{jothimurugan2021abstractvi}) extend these ideas to continuous spaces, while Monte Carlo-based approaches~\cite{bai2016hierarchical} treat abstracted MDPs as POMDPs to scale tree search. 
Unlike \acronym, HRL methods usually learn from samples and
provide no optimality guarantees for the original model.


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced \acronym, a hierarchical adaptive refinement approach for accelerating policy synthesis in large MDPs. 
\acronym\ is general to handle diverse MDP structures via configurable partitioning strategies, and flexible to balance accuracy and efficiency via adaptive refinement criteria. 
We have implemented \acronym\ as an open-source extension to PRISM and demonstrated its effectiveness and scalability through comprehensive experiments on warehouse robotics scenarios with up to 1M states, showcasing that \acronym\ is faster than PRISM-based policy synthesis using VI and GS.
% Moreover, in our experimental evaluation we have also provided actionable guidelines to practitioners.
%
Our future work includes: 
(i) extending \acronym\ to support non-grid domains and multi-objective properties;
(ii) enhance \acronym's refinement strategy by incorporating domain knowledge; and
(iii)  examine \acronym's scalability in larger model instances. %applications and scenarios.
% By making large-scale MDP verification computationally feasible, SHARP enables rigorous safety and performance analysis for software-intensive systems operating under uncertainty.
