\subsection{Instance Search Performance}
In the first experiment, the effectiveness of our method is studied in comparison to several representative methods in instance search. Among these methods, R-MAC~\cite{r-mac}, CAM-weight~\cite{cam}, BLCF~\cite{blcf} and BLCF-SalGAN~\cite{blcf} are deep global features which collapse all the features of an image into one vector for retrieval. In contrast, methods like DASR~\cite{dasr} and ours extract the instance level feature from the candidate images and are able to localize the instance from the candidate images. In addition, we also treat the unsupervised object detector CutLER~\cite{cutclr} as an instance region detector. The same feature as ours is built for the regions detected by CutLER. Moreover, we also report the results when the bisecting clustering in CLAID is replaced by NCut~\cite{ncut}, while keeping other configurations unchanged. The instance search performance is shown in Table~\ref{tab:3search}.

As seen from the table, global features show poor performance on the instance search task. Object level features such as DASR, CutLER, and CLAID demonstrate considerably better performance. CLAID outperforms the rest of methods on three benchmarks. Particularly, CLAID shows considerably better performance than the rest on \textit{INSTRE} where the queries are more challenging and there are a lot of distractors in the reference set. Although CutLER is also able to build instance level features, its performance on instance search is considerably lower than CLAID. The relatively poor performance mainly dues to its limited number of partitions over the feature set. Moreover, the cut in a chain in CutLER also causes the loss of potential instance regions. Compared to CLAID (NCut), CLAID performs considerably better. According to our observation, the results from bisecting clustering better reflect the multi-scale semantic structure than the partitions produced by NCut. In addition, we also tested different backbones with CLAID at its feature extraction stage. As shown from the table, our method shows considerably better performance when it is supported by the same backbone as the competing methods (e.g., DASR$^*$ and CutLER). This largely owes to its high localization accuracy. Figure~\ref{fig:visualres} further showcases the instance search results from CLAID.

In order to study the behavior of our method on multi-instance search task, the performance on \textit{250} queries from \textit{INSTRE} is particularly evaluated. For these queries, there are more than one objects in presence in a query. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:multi}, most of the methods show relatively better performance on this task than that of single instance search (reported in Table~\ref{tab:3search}). The search quality from all global features is considerably better. This is mainly because the large coverage of the instance query over the image makes it close to a conventional content-based image search task. The mixture of features from different instances becomes helpful in this context. Our method still shows the best performance as it keeps the features extracted from different scales.
%
The localization accuracy of different methods (measured by mIoU) is reported on Table~\ref{tab:iou}. Since the localization is impossible to fulfill for the global features, only the performance from instance level features is reported.
The patch-level region masks from our method and CutLER are transformed to bounding boxes when we calculate the mIoU score.
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:iou}, CLAID shows considerably better localization accuracy over DASR* and CutLER.

