\section{Conclusions}
We have presented a novel instance level feature descriptor, namely CLAID. Given an image is represented by the feature set produced by self-supervised ViT, a novel top-down repeated bisecting procedure is proposed to partition the feature set into compact and spatially connected instance regions. The nodes on the resulting hierarchy correspond to salient instance regions mined from the image. Compared to the existing methods, it provides a more exhaustive description about the latent instances in one image. Most importantly, our framework is class-agnostic, which allows it to remain effective on various object categories. Excellent performance is achieved on single and multiple instance search tasks as well as image search task. It, therefore, becomes possible to unify the visual instance search and the content-based image search under one framework. Moreover, only around \textit{30} features are extracted per image, it allows the search to be scaled up to billion images level on a single server.
