Similar to the spatially homogeneous case, we can consider the stochastic counterpart of the reduced PDE model as a reduced model of the full spatial stochastic system \cite{Chae2025homohete} (Table~\ref{table:4}). In this reduced model, all fast reactions are removed, and the fast variable \( n_{D,i} \) in the propensity functions is replaced by the stochastic tQSSA for each compartment:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&n_{D_{tq},i}(n_{D_T,i}, n_{P_T,i}) \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left \{(n_{D_T,i} - n_{P_T,i} - K_d\cdot\Omega_i) + \sqrt{(n_{D_T,i} - n_{P_T,i} - K_d\cdot\Omega_i)^2 + 4n_{D_T} K_d\Omega_i} \right \}, \label{eq:stQSSA_i}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $n_{D_T,i} = n_{D,i} + n_{D\text{:}P,i}$, $n_{P_T,i} = n_{P,i} + n_{D\text{:}P,i}$, and $\Omega_i$ is the compartment volume.
Simulating this reduced model is significantly more efficient than the full model due to the elimination of all fast reactions, as in the spatially homogeneous case.

