\section{Introduction}





The $SL(2,\C)$ character variety $V_g$ of compact Riemann surface of genus $g$ is  equipped with the natural complex symplectic form, which is the inverse to the Goldman Poisson bracket on $V_g$ \cite{goldman1984symplectic}. Being restricted to the moduli space of Riemann surfaces $\Mcal_g$ this symplectic form coincides with the Weil-Petersson form.
The exists a set of Darboux coordinates on $V_g$ which is obtained by analytical continuation of Fenchel-Nielsen coordinates on the moduli space $\Mcal_g$  \cite{wolpert1985weil} in complex domain.



Once one introduces $n$ punctures on the Riemann surface and considers the character  $V_{g,n}$ for $n>0$, there exist a sets of  coordinates  which are (complex extensions of) the  Thurston's shear coordinates  which are log-canonical  for the  Goldman    form on the symplectic leaves of the Goldman bracket
(the coordinates are called log-canonical if  the coefficients of the 2-form written in terms of  these coordinates are constants).  
%Thurston's shear coordinates admit a far-reaching generalization to $SL(n)$ character varieties by Fock and Goncharov \cite{fock2006moduli}
The actual Darboux coordinates can be then obtained as linear combinations of log-canonical coordinates with constants coefficients. 
%Another set of log-canonical coordinates on $\Mcal_{g,n}$ for $n\geq 1$ is given by Penner's $\lambda$-lengths \cite{penner2002decorated}.

However, so far it was unclear how to efficiently use the (complexified) shear coordinates on $V_g$, although
 important steps in this direction were made in 
 \cite{bonahon2018goldman}.  In the case of $V_{2}$  a set of log-canonical coordinates alternative to Fenchel-Nielsen were recently constructed in \cite{chekhov2023symplectic}; however, it remained unclear how to  generalize this construction to higher genus.

In this paper we construct new systems of log-canonical  coordinates on $V_g$ for any genus  by combining the ideas behind (complexified) shear coordinates   and twist-length coordinates. 
The technical machinery is based on the work \cite{bertola2023extended} where to each graph on a Riemann surface (such that each edge is equipped with an $SL(2)$  jump matrix)  one associates a canonical two-form which turns out to be an extension of 
the Goldman symplectic form.


One can  associate a new  system of (local) log-canonical  coordinates on $V_g$ to any system of simple non-intersecting loops $\gamma_1,\dots,\gamma_m$ with any $m=1,\dots, 3g-3$ on a  Riemann surface $\Ccal$.
(in fact, it means that out systems  of the log-canonical coordinates are labeled by 
components of the Deligne-Mumford boundary of the Teichm\"uller space ${\mathcal T}_g$). 

In the case of maximal number of loops $m=3g-3$ 
which correspond to a  trinion  decomposition of the Riemann surface;  our coordinates are closely related (but not exactly coinciding) 
%due to shift a of the origin of twist coordinates) 
with complexified Fenchel-Nielsen length-twist coordinates.

%n the first non-trivial case  $m=1$   the part of  DM boundary of codimension 1 case  is the union of   the following   components.  The reducible components $\Delta_{g_1,g_2}$  corresponds to the case when
% the geodesic $\g$ is homologically trivial i.e. it  separates $\Ccal$ into two Riemann surfaces with one boundary each, $\Ccal_1$ of genus $g_1$, and $\Ccal_2$ of genus $g_2$, such that $g_1+g_2=g$.  The irreducible component $\Delta_{0}$ corresponds to 
% homologically non-trivial contour $l$; of one cuts $\Ccal$ along $l$ one gets the Riemann surface of genus $g-1$ with two boundary components of the same geodesic length..  Each of these components corresponds to its own system of Darboux coordinates on $M_g$ or $T_g$.

 In this paper we discuss in detail the  simplest case of  a simple contour $\gamma$  separating  $\Ccal$ into two Riemann surfaces,
 $\Ch$ and $\Ct$ of genera $\gt$ and $\gh$, respectively.   The associate system of log-canonical  coordinates on $V_g$ consists of a pair of length-twist type coordinates along  $\gamma$ and also the shear-type coordinates on moduli spaces of Riemann surfaces of genera $\gt$ and $\gh$ with one boundary component.

 More precisely, let $\l_\g$ and $\l_\g^{-1}$ be eigenvalues of the monodromy matrix $M_\g$, and denote $\len_\g=\ln\l_\g$ (in the case of the Fuchsian component of $V_g$ the absolute value  $|\len_\g|$ gives the length
 of the closed geodesics in the homotopy class of $\gamma$). The sign of $\len_\g$ will be fixed below.





% More precisely, let us  denote the hyperbolic length of the geodesic $\g$ by $|\len_\g|$ (we use this notation since in this paper we reserve $\len_\g $ itself for the ``oriented length" which can be both positive and negative). The surfaces $\Ct$ and $\Ch$ are then elements of the moduli spaces $\Mcal_{\gt}[|\len_\gamma|]$ and
