 The integer coefficients $ \t c_{ij}$, $\h c_{kl} $ lie  in the interval between $-4$ and $4$. They are determined by the 
order of  the edges of the uni-valent graphs $\t\Gamma$ and $\h\Gamma$ at there vertex. 

 This construction admits a straightforward generalization to the case when one chooses several simple non-intersecting contours on $\Ccal$ which may split it into several components (essentially, one can assign a different set of log-canonical coordinates to any component of the Deligne-Mumford boundary of $\Mcal_g$).
 Another natural  generalization gives new systems of log-canonical coordinates on an arbitrary $SL(N)$ character variety of $\pi_1(\Ccal)$. Here one needs to use the Fock-Goncharov coordinates associated to triangulations 
 $\t\Gamma$ and $\h \Gamma$ together with eigenvalues and toric variables associated to $M_\gamma$.
Both of these generalizations  will be described in detail elsewhere.


\section{Main spaces}

\subsection{ Character variety of a Riemann surface}

Let  $\Ccal$ be an oriented Riemann surface of genus $g$. Then the standard generators
$\{\alpha_k,\beta_k\}_{k=1}^g$ of the fundamental group
  $\pi_1(\Ccal)$ can be chosen such that
\be
   \prod_{i=1}^g \left[ \alpha_i, \beta_i \right]= id 
   \la{relfun}
   \ee
where $$\left[ \alpha_i, \beta_i \right] := \alpha_i \beta_i^{-1} \alpha_i^{-1} \beta_i \;.$$
A point of $SL(2)$ character variety  $V_g$ is  represented by the equivalence class of the  set of monodromy matrices $\{M_{\alpha_1}, M_{\beta_1}..., M_{\alpha_g}, M_{\beta_g} \}$ satisfying
    \begin{equation}\label{repcl}
        \prod_{i=1}^g \left[ M_{\alpha_i}, M_{\beta_i} \right]=\mathbb{I}_2
    \end{equation}
Traces of  monodromy matrices can be used as coordinates on $V_g$.
The Goldman  Poisson bracket on $V_{g}$ (the complexification of the Weil-Petersson bracket on $\Mcal_g$)   can be written as follows in terms of trace coordinates 
\begin{equation}
\label{GoldBrack}
\{ \tr M_{\gamma}, \tr M_{\Tilde{\gamma}} \} := \sum_{p \in \gamma \cap \Tilde{\gamma} } \nu(p) (\mathrm{tr}M_{\gamma \circ_p \Tilde{\gamma} }- \frac{1}{2} \tr M_{\gamma} \,\tr M_{\Tilde{\gamma}})
\end{equation} 
where $ \gamma, \Tilde{\gamma} \in \pi_1(\Ccal)  $ and $\nu(p) $ is the contribution of the point $p$ to the  intersection index of $\gamma$ and $\Tilde{\gamma}$. 
%An appropriate subspace of the character variety  $V_g$ can be identified with the moduli space of hyperbolic fuchsian groups i.e. with the moduli space of Riemann surfaces $\Mcal_g$. 
The symplectic form inverting the Goldman bracket on the Fuchsian component coincides then with the Weil-Petersson symplectic form $ \omega_{WP}$ on $ \Mcal_g$. 

The Goldman bracket on $V_g$ is non-degenerate. 
%Wolpert \cite{wolpert1985weil} have found that a set of Darboux coordinates on $\Mcal_g$ is given by Fenchel-Nielsen length-twist coordinates which automatically gives the expression for the corresponding symplectic form.
%However, the analogs of the Fenchel-Nielsen coordinates are not known for $SL(N)$  character varieties of $\Ccal$ for $N\geq 3$. 
The symplectic form 
inverting  the  Goldman's bracket on an arbitrary  $SL(N)$ character variety of a Riemann surface    was found in \cite{alekseev1995symplectic}, but the corresponding log-canonical coordinates  coordinates for them are unknown. 

Below we show how to  construct systems of Darboux coordinates on $V_g$ which can be naturally extended to higher $SL(N)$ character varieties using the machinery of Fock-Goncharov coordinates.





Let now $\Ccal$ be a closed and oriented Riemann surface of genus $g$ with $n \geq 1$  marked points $t_1,\dots,t_n$. Then the standard 
generators $\{\gamma_j\}_{j=1}^n$,
$\{\alpha_k,\beta_k\}_{k=1}^g$ of the fundamental group
  $\pi_1(\Ccal \setminus \{t_j\})$ can be chosen such that:
$$
 \quad \gamma_1...\gamma_n \prod_{i=1}^g \left[ \alpha_i, \beta_i \right]= id \;.
 $$


Consider now a representation  of $\pi_1(\Ccal \setminus \{t_j\})$ in $SL(2) $.
Then monodromy matrices corresponding to the standard generators of the fundamental group  satisfy the same relation:
\be
M_{\gamma_1} ... M_{\gamma_n} \prod_{i=1}^g  \left[ M_{\alpha_i}, M_{\beta_i} \right] =\mathbb{I}_2 \;. 
\la{relMn}
\ee
We  denote  eigenvalues of  $M_{\gamma_j}$  by $ \lambda_j$ and $ 1/\lambda_j$.

Define  the character variety $V_{g,n}$ as the quotient of the space of monodromy representations  by the equivalence relation under simultaneous adjoint action on all monodromies. The coordinates on $V_{g,n}$   can be again chosen to be the trace coordinates i.e. $\tr M_\gamma$ for 
sufficiently many elements $\gamma$ of the fundamental group.



 The Goldman bracket on $V_{g,n}$  is defined by the same formula (\ref{GoldBrack}).
However,  on $V_{g,n}$ it is  degenerate, with the Casimir elements being the eigenvalues of $M_{\gamma_j}$.

%The  hyperbolic length $\ell(\gamma_j)$  of the boundary component $\gamma_j$ equals to $| \len_j|$ where
%$ \len_j=\ln{\lambda_j}$ (the eigenvalue $\lambda_j$ can be greater or smaller than $1$). 
%Therefore, an open domain of the  character variety  $V_{g,n}$ can be identified with  the moduli space  $\Mcal_{g, n}[|\len_1|,\dots, |\len_n|]$ of Riemann surfaces with  $ n$ boundary components of geodesic lengths $|\len_1|, \dots,|\len_n|$. The trace coordinates can also be used as local coordinates on $\Mcal_{g,n}[|\len_1|,\dots, |\len_n|]$


\subsection{Extended character varieties}



The character varieties $V_{g,n}$  admit natural extensions  ${\Hcal}_{g,n}$   which possess a non-degenerate Poisson structure. 
The idea of such extension is to introduce canonical partners to the Casimirs $\len_1, ..., \len_n$ of the Goldman bracket on the moduli space of Riemann surfaces with boundaries.
%The resulting extension of the character variety is called the \textit{extended character variety}. 
Suppose that all the monodromies $M_{\gamma_k}$ are diagonalizable with 
$$M_{\gamma_k}=C_k \Lambda_k C_k^{-1} \;.$$
Here we don't   fix the order of the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $M_{\gamma_k}$.
% by choosing the first eigenvalue to be $\lambda_k>1$ and the second one to be  $ 1/\lambda_k <1$ (the lengths $|\len_k|=\ln \lambda_k$). 

Define  the extended space ${\Hcal}_{g,n}$ as follows
\begin{equation}\label{ext}
{\Hcal}_{g,n}=\left \{ \{ M_{\alpha_j}, M_{\beta_j} \}_{j=1}^g, \{ C_i, \Lambda_i \}_{i=1}^{n} : \hskip0.4cm C_1 \Lambda_1 C_1^{-1}\dots C_N \Lambda_N C_N^{-1} \prod_{i=1}^g \left[ M_{\alpha_i}, M_{\beta_i} \right]=\mathbb{I}_2 \right \}/\sim
\end{equation} 
The sets 
related by transformation $M_{\gamma} \to G M_{\gamma} G^{-1}$, $C_j \to G C_j $ for a fixed $G \in SL(2) $ (while $\Lambda_j$ remain invariant) are assumed to be equivalent.

The space ${\Hcal}_{g,n}$ for an arbitrary matrix dimension was studies in  \cite{jeffrey1994extended}, \cite{boalch2018wild} and \cite{bertola2023extended}. It possesses the natural symplectic form \cite{boalch2018wild,bertola2023extended}.
 The canonical partners to the Casimirs  are the (logarithms of)
the so-called toric variables $b_j$ which are the normalizing factors of the eigenvectors of $M_{\g_j}$ encoded in the matrices $C_j$. In  \cite{bertola2023extended} theis symplectic form
was represented  in log-canonical from for an arbitrary $SL(N)$ group using the Fock-Goncharov coordinates.


\section{Graphs on surfaces and canonical symplectic form}


Let $\Gcal$ be a finite ciliated oriented graph embedded into a Riemann surface $\Ccal$, with $V(\mathcal{G})$ and $E(\Gcal)$ denoting the sets of its vertices and edges respectively. 
The cilium chosen at each vertex induces the ordering of edges attached to this vertex. One writes $ e< e'$ when the edge $e$ precedes the edge $e'$ in a counterclockwise order from the cilium.

 We will use the following notations: $e=[v_1, v_2]$ denotes the oriented edge connecting $v_1$ to $v_2$ and $-e=[v_2, v_1]$ denotes the oriented edge connecting $v_2$ to $v_1$. 
 If an  edge $e$ is incident to a vertex $v$ we write  $e \perp v$.  We define the valency $ n_v$ of a given vertex $ v$ to be the total number of edges incident to $v$ counted with multiplicity.  


\begin{dref}\rm
\label{can graph}
    A pair $(\Gcal, J)$ consisting of an oriented graph on $\Ccal$ up to isotopy and a map $J: E(\Gcal) \rightarrow SL(2)$ is canonical if it satisfied the following conditions: 
    \begin{enumerate}

\item
 The map $J: E(\Gcal) \rightarrow SL(2)$ satisfies $J(-e)=J(e)^{-1}$. The matrix $ J(e)$ is called the jump matrix associated to the edge $ e$.\\
    \item
    For each vertex $v \in V(\Gcal)$ of valence $n_v \geq 2$ with incident {\it incoming} edges $\{ e_1, e_2,..., e_{n_v}\}$ enumerated counterclockwise starting from an arbitrary chosen edge the local monodromy around $v$ is trivial, i.e. 
     \be
     J(e_1) J(e_2)...J(e_{n_v})=\mathbb{I}_2\;.
     \la{JJJ}\ee
    \end{enumerate}
\end{dref}
To each pair $(\Gcal, J) $ we associate the \textit{canonical} two-form $ \Omega(\Gamma) $ as in \cite{bertola2023extended}:
\begin{equation} \label{OmegaG}
        \Omega ({\Gcal})= \frac{1}{2} \,\tr \sum_{v \in V(\Gcal)} \sum_{l=1}^{n_v-1} \left(J_{[1...l]}^{(v)}\right)^{-1} dJ_{[1...l]}^{(v)} \wedge \left(J_l^{(v)}\right)^{-1} dJ_l^{(v)}
\end{equation}  
where $J_l^{(v)}=J(e_l)$, $ e_l \perp v$, are the matrices defined by the map in Definition \ref{can graph} corresponding to the edges $e_1, e_2,..., e_{n_v}$ incident to $v$, and 
$$ J_{[1...l]}^{(v)} := J_1^{(v)} J_2^{(v)}...J_l^{(v)} \;$$

The form  $ \Omega({\Gcal}) $ is invariant under the set of the following admissible moves \cite{bertola2023extended}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item merging of two vertices $ v_1$ and $ v_2$ of valency $ n_{v_1}+1$ and $ n_{v_2}+1$ respectively, connected by a common edge $ e_0$, to get a single vertex $ v$ of valency $ n_{v_1}+n_{v_2}$\\
    \item zipping of two edges $ e_1$ and $ e_2$ with jump matrices $ J_1$ and $ J_2 $ respectively, connecting the same two vertices $ v_1$ and $ v_2$, to get a single edge $ e $ with jump matrix $ J_1 J_2$. \\
\end{enumerate}


\section{Standard graph and symplectic form on $ V_g$}


Let $ \Ccal$ be a compact unpunctured Riemann surface of genus $g$. Choose a base point $v_0$ and a set of standard generators $\{\a_j,\b_j\}_{j=1}^g$ 
of $\pi_1(\Ccal,v_0)$ satisfying (\ref{relfun}). Denote by $M_{\alpha_i}$, $M_{\beta_i}$ the corresponding monodromies
 which satisfy (\ref{relMn}) for $n=0$:
\be
\prod_{j=1}^g [M_{\a_j} , M_{b_j}]=I
\la{MMM}
\ee

Let us choose some oriented closed contours on $\Ccal$ starting at $v_0$ representing the elements $\a_j$, $\b_j$; with a slight abuse of notations we denote these loops by the 
same letters. Consider the one-valent  ciliated graph $\Gcal_0$ (which we call the \textit{standard graph}, see Figure \ref{fig:SG} (A)) with vertex at $v_0$ and $2g$ oriented edges 
$\{\a_j,\b_j\}_{j=1}^g$; the valency of $v_0$ equals $4g$. Define the jump matrices on the edges of $\Gcal_0$ as follows:
\be
J(\alpha_j)=M_{\beta_j} \;, \hskip0.7cm J(\beta_j)=M_{\alpha_j} \;.
\la{JJMM}
\ee
The cilium at $v_0$ is chosen between $\alpha_g$ and $\beta_1$ (see Fig \ref{fig:SG}a).
Notice that the relation ({\ref{JJJ}) in the general definition of jump matrices on a given graph is written under assumption  that all the edges $e_j$ are incoming to 
the vertex. In out case, since every edge meets the vertex twice, while defining the canonical form (\ref{OmegaG}) we need to inverse the orientation 
of $2g$ out of $4g$ half-edges incident to $v_0$ as shown  in Fig \ref{fig:SG}b.

